        "title": "13.1.1.2 Trend in Atomic Radius",
        "theory": "[Ne]3s2,3p2\nPhosphorus\n15P\n[Ne]3s2,3p3\nSulphur,\n16S\n[Ne]3s2,3p+\nChlorine\n17C1\n[Ne]3s2,3ps\nArgon\n18Ar\n[Ne]3s3p\n13.1.1.2 Trend in Atomic Radius\nThe distance between the nucleus and the outermost shell of electrons of an atom or an ion is generally named as the atomic radius or ionic radius respectively. The exact position. occupied by an electron cannot be defined with certainty. So the radius of an atom cannot be determined precisely. Thus, we deal with the radii of atoms linked together by some type of chemical bond.\n3\n1. Covalent Radius\nNOT FOR SALE\nIt is defined as &quot;one-half of the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms bonded together by a single covalent bond.&quot; X-ray diffraction or spectroscopic measurements are used to determine the distance between the nuclei of any two bonded atoms. For example, the inter-nuclear distance in Cla molecule is 198 pm. This means covalent radius of Cl atom\nis 99 pm.\n2. Van der Waal&#x27;s Radius\nIt is defined as &quot;one-half of the distance between the nuclei of two non-bonded and identical neighbouring atoms of two adjacent molecules in the solid state.&quot; Since this radius corresponds to Van der Waal&#x27;s forces of attraction between the molecules, its magnitude is always greater than that of covalent radius, e.g. Van der Waal&#x27;s radius of hydrogen atom is 120 pm while its covalent radius is 37 pm.\n3. Metallic Radius\nThis term is used for radius of metallic atoms e.g. Na, Mg, and Al. It is half of the total distance between nuclei of two adjacent atoms in the metallic cluster. The metallic radii are also smaller than Van der Waal&#x27;s radii because metallic bonds are stronger than the Van der Waal&#x27;s forces.\nPeriodic Trends\nIn general, atomic radii shows an increase in magnitude on moving from top to bottom in any group and decrease in magnitude on moving from left to right in any given period.\n(a) Variation in a Group\nin\nAs we move in a group from top to bottom, the number of shells and the effective nuclear charge increases but the effect of addition of new shell dominates over the effect of effective nuclear charge and hence the atomic size increases from top to bottom in a group. For example, see Table 13.2\nTable 13.2 Trend in Atomic Radius of group IA\nElements of Group LA\nOuter-shell electronic\nconfiguration\nAtomic radius (pm)\n(b) Variation in a Period\navtom\nLi\nNa\nK\nRb\nCs\n[He]2s [Ne]3s [Ar]4s! [Kr]5s [Xe]6s&#x27;\n152\n186\n227\n248\n265.\nAs we move in a period from left to right, the number of shells remains the same as the electrons are added in the same shell. However, the effective nuclear charge increases and pulls the electron cloud closer to the nucleus. As a result, the atomic size decreases from sodium (Na) to chlorine (CI) in period third.\n4\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe atomic size of noble gas (Argon) is greater than the prior elements in the respective period (3rd period). This is due to the reason that in noble gases, Van. der Waal&#x27;s radius is determined, whose magnitude is larger than the covalent radius.\nTable 13.3 Trend in Atomic Radius of 3rd Period Elements Na to Ar.\nSymbol with atomic number\n11Na 12Mg 13A 14Si 15P\n16S\n17C 18Ar\nAtomic radius (pm)\n186 160 143\n118\n114 104\n99 188\nNa\nMg\nAL\nSi\nP\nS\nCI\nAr\nFigure 13.1 Atomic radius changes across third period elements.\n13.1.1.3 Trend in First lonization Energy\nIonization Energy (IE)\nIonization energy of an element is the minimum amount of energy (in kJ/mol) required to remove the outermost electron of an isolated gaseous atom to form an ion, the result is the formation of the gaseous cation, i.e.\nMg(g)\nMg*(s) + e ̃\nAH=737kJ.mol-1\nIonization energy for removal of first electron from the outermost shell of an atom is called 1&quot; L.E. Similarly, the ionization energy for the removal of an electron from M is called 2&quot; I.E.\nMg (g)\nPeriodic Trends\nMg+",
