        "theory": "AH=1476kJ.mol1\nIn general, the ionization energy decreases in moving from top to bottom in a group increases from left to right in a period.\n(a) Variation in a Group\nand\nAs we move in a group from top to bottom, there is an increase in the atomic size, nuclear charge and shielding effect. However, the combined effect of increase in atomic size and shielding effect dominates over the effect of increase in the nuclear charge. Hence, ionization energy decreases from top to bottom in a group with some exceptions. Variation in a Period\nAs we move in a period from left to right, the number of shells remain the same but atomic size decreases as the electrons are added in the same shell, hence pulling the electrons. closer towards the nucleus.\n5\nNOT FOR SALE\nThus, more energy is required to remove the electron from the outermost shell of the atom resulting in an increase of ionization energy. The first ionization energy values of period 3\nelements are given below.\nTable 13.4 First Ionization Energy Values of Period 3rd Elements\nElements of period 3rd Na 12Mg 13Al 14Si 15P 16S 17CI 18Ar\n1o ionization energy (kJ mol−1)\n495 737\n577\n786 1011 1000 1251\n1520\nTidbit\nScreening effect or shielding effect\nIn a multi-electron atom, the electrons in the valence shell are attracted to the nucleus. Like same charges repel, so these electrons are repelled by the electrons present in the inner shells. On account of this, the force of attraction between the nucleus and the valence electrons decrease. This decrease in the force of attraction on the valence electrons due to the presence of electrons in the inner shells is called screening effect or shielding effect.\nThe magnitude of the screening effect depends upon the number of inner électrons higher the number of inner electrons greater shall be the value of the screening effect. As we move down ward in the the number of shells increases gradually between the nucleus and the valence shell.\ngroup These shells act like shields. These shells protect the electrons from the attraction of the nucleus.\n13:1.1.4 Trend in Electronegativity\nElectronegativity\nThe relative tendency of an atom to attract a shared electron pair towards itself is called its electronegativity.\nPeriodic Trends\nIn general, electronegativity decreases on moving from top to bottom\nincreases from left to right in a period.\n(a) Variation in a Group\nWa\na group and\nAs we move in a group from top to bottom, there is an increase in the atomic size, nuclear\ncharge and shielding effect.\nHowever, the combined effect of increase in atomic size, nuclear change and shielding effect results in decrease in the force of attraction of the nucleus for the shared pair of\nelectron, thus electronegativity decreases.\n(b) Variation in a Period\nAs we move in a period from left to right, there is no change in the number of shells but effective nuclear charge increases. This results in decrease in atomic size and hence electronegativity increases.\nFluorine (F) is the most electronegative element and Caesium (Cs) is the least electronegative element in the periodic table. Electronegativity values of period 3 elements are given in the table.\n¥\n6\nTable 13.5 Trend in Electronegativity of 3 Period elements\nNOT FOR SALE\nAl 14Si 0.93 1.31 1.61 1.90\n15P\n16S 17CI 18Ar 2.19 2.58 3.16\nElements of period 3rd Na 12Mg Electronegativity\nNote that electronegativity of argon (Ar) is not included. As the electronegativity is related to the tendency of an atom to attract the shared pair of electrons. As, argon does not form covalent bond(s), so no electronegativity value is assigned to argon (Ar).\n13.1.1.5 Trend in Electrical Conductivity\nElectrical Conductivity\nElectrical conductivity is the measure of the amount of electrical current a material can carry. Electrical conductivity values of period 3 elements relative to aluminium are given below. Table 13.6 Trend in Electrical Condutivity in 3rd Period Elements\n0\nElements of period 3rd Na 12Mg 13A 14S 15P 16S 17C 18Ar Electrical conductivity 0.55 0.61 1.00 0.10 0\n0 0\nSodium, magnesium and aluminium are all metals and are good conductors of electricity. These metals conduct electricity because they contain free electrons. Going from sodium to aluminium, the number of free electrons increases and thus the electrical conductivity increases. Silicon has a giant covalent structure. It is a semiconductor, so it is not a good. conductor. Phosphorus, sulphur, chlorine and argon are non-conductor as they are non-metals. 13.1.1.6 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points\nThe following table shows melting points and boiling points for the elements Na to Ar. Melting points and boiling points tell us how strongly the atoms in an element are held together.\nTable 13.6 Trend in Electrical Conductivity in 3rd Period Elements\nElements of period 3rd\nNa Mg Al\nSi\nP\nS CI Ar\nMelting point °C\n98 651 660\n1410\n44(white) 119-103-189\n883 1107 2470 2355\n260\n445 -35-486\nBoiling point °C\nSodium, magnesium and aluminium have metallic bonding, in which the nuclei of metal atoms are attracted by free electrons. Going from sodium to aluminium, the number of free electron increases and the melting and boiling points also increase. Silicon is a metalloid with a giant covalent structure. Silicon has a very high melting and boiling points because of strong covalent bonds. P, S, Cl and Ar in period 3 are non-metals and have low melting and boiling points because they have weak Van der Waal&#x27;s forces between their molecules.\n7\nNOT FOR SALE\ni.\nThe strength of the Van der Waal&#x27;s forces decreases as the size of the molecule decreases,\nso the melting and boiling points decreases in the order:\n13.1.2\nS, &gt;P, &gt;Cl, &gt; Ar\nReactions of the Period 3&quot; Elements with Water, Oxygen and Chlorine\nReactions of the Period 3 Elements with Water\nSodium undergoes exothermic reaction with cold water producing hydrogen and a solution of sodium hydroxide.",
