        "sciNote": "Reactions with Air or Oxygen and the formation of Normal Oxides,\nPeroxides, Super Oxides and their Stability\nWhen the group - IA elements are heated with excess of air or oxygen, lithium forms normal oxide, Li2O; sodium forms peroxide, Na,O,, whereas potassium, rubidium and caesium form superoxides having general formula MO,\n4Li)+02)\n300°C\n2Na) + O2(g)\nK(+026)\n2Li20)\nlithium oxide\nNa2O2\nsodium peroxide\nKO2\npotassium superoxide\ne in\n“Rb\" and \"Cs\" catch fire in air and produce superoxide such as RbO, and CsO, respectively. Thus, the reactivity of alkali metals with oxygen increases down the group.\n13.2.3.2 Reactions of Oxides with Water and Dilute Acids\ni. Reactions of Oxides with Water\nOxides of group - IA elements react with water to produce the respective bases. Group IA elements react with oxygen to form normal oxides, peroxides or superoxides. These oxides react with water in different ways.\n⚫ Normal oxides\nNormal oxide reacts with water and produce metal hydroxide. For example, Li2O reacts with water and form lithium hydroxide.\nLi2O+H2O\nX\n2LiOH,\n20\nPeroxides\nNOT FOR SALE\nPeroxides (M,O,) react with water and produce metal hydroxide and hydrogen. peroxide. For example, Na,O, reacts with water and form sodium hydroxide and hydrogen peroxide.\nNa200+2H2O(1)\nSuper oxides\n2NaOH + H2O2(0)\nSuper oxides (MO,) react with water and produce metal hydroxide, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas. For example, KO, reacts with water and forms potassium hydroxide, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen",
