        "id": "13.14",
        "title": "13.3.5 Trend in Thermal Stability of the Nitrates and Carbonates",
        "theory": "2M(NO3)200 2Ca(NO3)26)\n2M0+4NO+O\n2CaO+4NO) +020\nThe nitrates become more thermally stable down the group, i.e. they require more heating to decompose.\nji. Thermal Stability of the Group - IIA Carbonates\nAll the carbonates of this group undergo thermal decomposition, to the metal oxide and carbon dioxide gas.\nThe following reactions describe the decomposition of the carbonate (Where M = Be, Mg, Ca, Ba and Sr).\nMCO\nCaCO\n200\nn\nMO+CO CaO +CO\nThe carbonates become more thermally stable down the group, they require more heating to decompose.\n13.3.6 How Beryllium Differs from other Members of its Group?\nBeryllium differs from other Group-IIA metals in many respects. This is partly due to its small size and partly due to its high electronegativity.\nBeryllium is the lightest of all the Group - IIA metals.\nBeryllium has higher melting and boiling point compared to other elements in Group - IIA.\nBeryllium unlike other Group - ILA metals is not easily affected by dry air\nBeryllium does not react with water or steam at red heat.\nThe oxide of beryllium, BeO is amphoteric whereas, other oxides of Group - IIA metals are strong bases.\nOxides, nitrides, chlorides etc. of Group-IIA elements are ionic, while those of beryllium are covalent.\n⚫ Salts of beryllium do not impart any colour during flame test unlike its other family\nmembers.\nBeryllium oxide is insoluble in water unlike oxide of other members of its family. Beryllium does not liberate hydrogen from acids as observed in other Group – IIA\nmetals.\n29\nNOT FOR SALE\n13.3.6.1 Why is Beryllium Chloride Covalent and not lonic?\nBeryllium at the top of the group has high electronegativity (1.57) compared with the rest of the group members. High electronegativity means that it attracts a bonding pair of electrons towards itself more strongly than the rest of the Group - IIA elements. This gives covalent character to BeCl,.\n13.3.6.2 Amphoteric Beryllium Hydroxide\nBeryllium hydroxide can act both as an acid and as a base.\nL Beryllium Hydroxide as an Acid\nBeryllium hydroxide reacts with concentrated solution of sodium hydroxide to produce sodium tetrahydroxoberyllate.\nAs an acid: Be(OH)2+2NaOH()\nBeryllium Hydroxide as a Base\nNa,[Be(OH),\nsodium tetrahydroxoberyllate\nBeryllium hydroxide reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid producing beryllium chloride as neutralization product.\nAs a base: Be(OH)2(aq) + 2HCl(aq)\nBeCl2 + 2H2O\n13.4\nGroup-IVA Elements\nThe Group-IVA of the periodic table is often referred to as the carbon group. This group consists of five elements carbon (C), silicon (Si), germanium (Ge), tin (Sn) and lead (Pb). This group is present in the middle of periodic table forming a link between more electropositive elements of Group - IA to IIIA and more electronegative elements of Group VA to VIIA. Each element in this group contains four valence electrons. The elements of group - IV A evidentially exhibit an intermediate character. The general electronic configuration of Group - IVA elements in valence shell is ns, np2, where n varies from 2 (for carbon) to 6 (for lead), for example, the electronic configuration of carbon is 1s2,252, 2p2.\n13.4.1 Physical Properties: Melting and Boiling Points\nThe melting point of carbon is extremely high. The melting and boiling points of silicon and germanium are very high because these elements have a very stable arrangement of solid structure (diamond-type lattice). Sn and Pb have a lower melting point because only two electrons are involved in bond formation instead of four, due to inert pair\neffect.\n30\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable 13.23 Melting and Boiling Points of Group IVA Elements\nElements\nC\nSi\nGe\nSn\nPh\nMelting point (°C) 3550 (Diamond)\n1410\n937\n232\n328\nBoiling point (°C)\n4827\n2355\n2830\n2260\n1744\n13.4.2 The Trend from Non-Metal to Metal\nThe change from non-metallic to metallic character with the increase of atomic number is best illustrated in this group e.g. C is non-metal, Si and Ge are metalloids, while Sn and Pb exhibit metallic character.\n13.4.3 Oxidation State\nOxidation state is the apparent charge (positive or negative) on an atom of an element in a molecule or ion.\nCarbon and silicon show +4 oxidation states in carbonates and silicates. The remaining members can show +2 as well as +4 oxidation states. Since, the electronegativity of these elements is low; they do not have such a high tendency to form negative ions.\n13.4.4 Possible Oxidation States\nOxidation state of group - IVA elements may be positive or negative depending upon the nature of the compounds. The typical oxidation state shown by elements of Groups - IVA is +4, found in compounds like CCl4, SiCl4 and SnO2. However, as we move down the group, there are more and more examples, where the oxidation state is +2, such as SnCl2, PbCl, and PbO.\nHowever, carbon forms Cand C22-ions in certain compounds, such as Be,C(C=-4, Be = +2), Na2C2(C=-2, Na=+1) etc. Besides this, CH,, is not an example of carbon with an oxidation state of +4. Because carbon is more electronegative than hydrogen, so its oxidation state is -4.\n13.4.4.1 Inert Pair Effect in Formation of Ionic Bonds\nThe tendency of the two electrons in the outermost atomic s-orbital to remain unshared in compounds is called inert pair effect. For example, C and Si show a covalence of four due to sp1 hybridization. However, hybridization may not always be feasible in case of the lower elements of Group - IV. For hybridization to be energetically feasible, net enthalpy change involved in the following two processes should be negative.\n31\nii.\nNOT FOR SALE\nPromotion of one electron of the s-orbital into higher energy p-orbital. Energy must be provided for this process.",
