        "sciNote": "a) Variation in a Group\nAs we move in a group from top to bottom, there is an increase in the atomic size, nuclear charge and shielding effect. However, the combined effect of increase in atomic size and shielding effect dominates over the effect of increase in the nuclear charge. Hence, force of attraction of the nucleus for the electron to be added decreases resulting in a decrease in electron-affinity values.\nHowever, it has been seen that fluorine has lower electron affinity than chlorine because fluorine has very small size and high charge density which opposes the electron to be added. Electron affinity generally decreases in the following order (I<Br<F<CI).\n38\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable 13.27 Trend in Electron Affinity in Group-VIIA Elements\nHalogen\nF\nCI\nBr\nElectron Affinity (kJ/mol) -328.0\n-349.0\n-324.6\n-295.2\n13.5.1.4 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points\nThe melting and boiling points of Group - VIIA increases down the group due to increase\nin strength of the Van der Waals forces. This trend is given below.\nTable 13.28 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points in Group-VIIA Elements\nHalogen\nF\nCI\nBr\nI\nMelting point (\"C)\n-220.0\n-101.0\n-7.2\n+114.0\nBoling point (°C)\n-188.0\n-35.0\n+58.8\n+184.0\n13.5.1.5 Bond Enthalpies\nBond enthalpy is the amount of heat required to break one mole of covalent bonds to form individual atoms. It begins with the original substance in a gaseous state and ending with gaseous atoms.\nاصلی\nCI-Cl\n2C\n13.5.1.5.1 Bond Enthalpies in Halogens\nBond breaking is an endoth",
