        "title": "13.5.2 Strength of Halogens as Oxidizing Agents",
        "theory": "2Na*CI 6\nFor example, fluorine is a powerful oxidizing agent. Fluorine is the most electronegative, so forming F is the most energetically favourable of the reductions. This makes fluorine the most powerful oxidizing agent.\nChlorine has the ability to take electrons from bromide ions and oxidize/change them to bromine in potassium bromide solution.\nCla+2Br (aq)\nThe bromine appears as an orange solution.\nW\n2C1\n+B\nChlorine can also oxidize iodide ions to iodine in potassium iodide solution.\nCla+21\n2C1(a)+126)\nThe iodine appears either as a red solution or as a dark grey precipitate if the chlorine is in\nexcess.\nBromine and iodine cannot regain those electrons from the chloride ions formed. This indicates that chlorine is more powerful oxidizing agent than either bromine or iodine. In short, oxidizing ability decreases down the group.\n40\nThe order of decreasing power of halogens as oxidizing agent is\nF&gt;Cl2 &gt; Br2 &gt;1\n13.5.3 The Acidity of Hydrogen Halides\nNOT FOR SALE\nمل کرتا رہا ہوتا\nAccording to Bronsted and Lowery, an acid is a proton donor. Hydrogen chloride gas is soluble in water, its solvated form is hydrochloric acid. HCI is soluble in water and produces H2O and Clions.\nHCl +H,O\nH,O+CI\nThis reaction shows that HCl completely dissociates in water. Hydrochloric acid is therefore a strong acid. An acid is strong if it is fully ionized in solution.\nHydrogen bromide and hydrogen iodide dissolve in (and react with) water and completely dissociate in water. Therefore, both HBr and HI are strong acids.\nHydrofluoric acid is an exception to strong halogen acids. Although hydrogen fluoride dissolves in water, but hydrofluoric acid is only a weak acid. In strength, it is similar to organic acids like methanoic acid.\n13.5.4\nHalide Ions as Reducing Agents and Trends in Reducing Strength Ability of Halide Ions R-X\nThe trend in reducing power of the halide ions is opposite to the trend in oxidizing power of the halogen elements. This means that the iodide ion is the best reducing agent. The more reactive halogen will displace the ions of a less reactive halogen from its solution. This is a redox reaction with the halogen acting as an oxidizing agent and the halide ion as a reducing agent. Chlorine oxidizes iodide ions to iodine and is itself reduced to chloride ions.\n21 ̄(44) + Cl2(g)\nتعالی باہر کرنا\n2C19 +12)\nBeside this, Br reduces sulphuric acid (H,SO,) to SO,, which results in a decrease of oxidation state of sulphur from &#x27;+6&#x27; in H,SO, to &#x27;+4&#x27; in SO..\nH2SO4()+2H)+2Br()\nBr+SO+2H2O\nIodide (I) is stronger reducing agent than bromide ion (Br), as it reduces H2SO,\nto HS in which oxidation state of &#x27;S&#x27; changes from +6 to -2.\nH, SO+8H\nH2SO) +8H ()+21,\n12+H2S (\n+4H2Om\nThe reducing agents, halides show the following trend in reducing strength:\n¥\nI&gt;Br&gt;C&gt;F\n41\nNOT FOR SALE\nSociety, Technology And Science\nBleaching powder is extensively applied to remove stains from fabrics or various surfaces. Besides, there are several other applications of bleaching powder. Some of their applications are as follows:\nOld items such as jewellery or showpieces made up of steel or metal can be cleaned with bleaching powder.\nMixing equal parts of water and bleaching powder are used as a bathroom cleaner.\nBleaching powder and soapy dishwater, clean the glass dishware, make it shining, clean and disinfect ◆ Bleaching powder can be applied as a disinfectant.\nIt can be used for cleaning and sterilizing kitchen and garden tools by washing them with bleaching powder.\nBy mixing bleaching powder with detergent, make the plastic furniture; look new and free of germs. By adding some bleach in the vase, water can preserve the fresh cut flowers.\nBy mixing, the bleaching powder with warm water can efficiently disinfect and clean thoroughly.\nBleaching powder mixed in water can be used to clean and sanitize the doors, windows and floors in households, offices, hospitals, malls and other places. This removes the germs and bacteria from surfaces.\nBleaching powder can be used in various industries and manufacturing units as an oxidizing agent. The chemical formula of bleaching powder is CaOCh. It reacts with dilute acids to release chlorine gas because chlorine is a strong oxidizing agent and contributes to the real action of bleaching powder.\nBleaching powder can act as an oxidizing agent. It is used to prepare chloroform in laboratories by reacting with ethanol or acetone.\n42\nawaz in\nNOT FOR SALE\nKey Points\nGroup - IA and Group - IIA elements belong to s-block. Group-IVA and Group - VIIA elements belong to p-block. Atomic radius of all the groups increases down the group.\nPeriod 3&quot; consists of metals (Na, Mg and Al), metalloid (Si) and non-metals (P, S, CI and Ar).\nAtomic radius decreases in period 3 up to chlorine but argon does not follow this trend. All the elements of period 3&quot; react with oxygen and form the oxides, except argon. Period 3&quot; oxides do not conduct electricity in solid state.\nThe chlorides of period 3&quot; i.e. NaCl, MgCl, and Al,Cl, are in solid state, while the SiCl,, PCI, and S,Cl, are in liquid state.\nDensity of Group - IA elements increases down the group.\nNormal oxides of Group - IA on reaction with dilute acids produce salts and water. The carbonates of Group - IA do not decompose on heating except lithium carbonate. Group – IA metals give characteristics colours to the Bunsen flame. Beryllium is the only alkaline earth metal, which does not react with water even at red heat.\nBe, Mg and Ca on reaction with oxygen form normal oxides.\nSr and Ba react with oxygen and form peroxide.\nBeO are amphoteric whereas other oxides of Group - IIA metals are strong bases. Inert pair effect is the non-participation of the two &#x27;s&#x27; electrons in bonding. In CO2 molecule, carbon atom is sp and each oxygen atom is sp2 hybridized. Silicon dioxide is weakly acidic.\nElectron affinity is the amount of energy released when an electron is added to an isolated gaseous atom to form an anion.\nIn Group-VIIA the electron affinity generally decreases in the order (I&lt;Br&lt;F&lt;CI). In Group-VIIA the order of decreasing power as oxidizing agent is F, &gt;Cl, Br, &gt;I.\nC C C C\nC C C C\nC C C C C C C\nCC\n43\nExercise\nNOT FOR SALE\nQ1. Choose the correct one from the given options.",
