        "sciNote": "2Na(s) + 2H2Oα)\n2NaOH(aq) + H2(g)\nMagnesium reacts slowly with cold water but burns in steam. A clean coil of magnesium dropped into cold water eventually is covered with small bubbles of hydrogen, which floats it to the surface. Magnesium form a very thin layer of magnesium hydroxide on the surface, which tends to stop it from further reaction.\nMg(x) + 2H2Oα)\nMg(OH)2(aq) + H2(g)\nMagnesium burns in steam with its typical white flame to produce white magnesium oxide and hydrogen.\nMg) + H2O)\nMgO) + H2(g)\nAluminium powder when heated in steam produces hydrogen and aluminium oxide. This reaction is relatively slow with bulk because of the formation of strong aluminium oxide layer on the metal and build-up of even more oxide during reaction.\n2Al(s)+3H2O(g)\nAl2O3(8) + 3H2(g) Silicon react with steam at red heat to produce silicon dioxide and hydrogen.\nSi(s) + 2H2O(g)\nSiO2() + 2H2(g)\ne in\nChlorine dissolves in water to some extent to give a pale green solution. A reversible reaction takes place to produce a mixture of hydrochloric acid and chloric (1) acid (hypochlorous acid), which decompose to HCI on exposure to sunlight.\nCl2(g) + H2Om\nHOC)\nNet reaction: Cl2(g) + H2Om\nHCl(aq) + HOCl (4) HCl(aq) + 1/202(4) 2HC1(4)+1/2020\nThere is no reaction between argon and water.\nii.\nReactions of the Period 3rd Elements with Oxygen\nSodium burns in a limited supply of air (oxygen) with a pale yellow flame to produce a white solid of sodium oxide. In sodium oxide, the oxidation state of oxygen is "
