        "sciNote": "lodine is found primarily in seawater and in the soil especially in coastal areas. At high elevations, iodine is often deficient in the water and soil. Lack of iodine can cause an abnormal enlargement of the thyroid gland, known as a goitre.\nA goitre is an abnormal enlargement of thyroid gland. Thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland located at the base of neck. The most common cause of goitres worldwide is a lack of iodine in the diet. Iodine deficiency may be made worse by a diet high in hormone-inhibiting foods, such as cabbage, broccoli and cauliflower.\nOur body does not naturally make iodine, so the only way to get this nutrient is through diet. Adults typically require 150μg per day. Pregnant and breastfeeding women need 200μg per day.\n13.1.3 Physical Properties of the Oxides\nThe oxides of period 3rd elements are following.\nNa,O\nMgO\nAl,O,\nSiO2\nP010\nSo,\nCI,O,\nPO\nSo,\nC12O\nThe oxides in the top row are the oxides where period 3 elements are in their highest oxidation state. In these oxides, all the outer electrons in period 3 elements are being involved in bonding from just one in sodium to all seven outer electrons of chlorine in Cl2O,. The oxides (high oxidation state) of sodium, magnesium, aluminium, silicon, phosphorous are in solid state and those of sulphur and chlorine are in liquid state. The oxides having low oxidation state of sulphur and chlorine are in gaseous state.\n10\n13.1.3.1 Structure\nSodium, magnesium and aluminium oxides consist of giant structures containing"
