        "theory": "NOT FOR SALE\nH2SO 300\nH2SO4)\nChlorine forms several oxides, but only two are considered here. i.e. dichlorine heptoxide. (Cl2O7), and dichlorine monoxide (ClO).\nDichlorine heptoxide (Cl207) reacts with water to give the very strong acid, perchloric acid. (HCIO4). While Cl20 forms hypochlorous acid (HOCI)\nCl200+ H200) CLO+H2O\nReaction of Oxides with Acids and Bases\nNa2O and MgO react with acids to form salt and water.\n2HCIO4(4)\n2HOCl)\nNa2O+ 2HCl)\nMgO (3) + 2HCl (84)\n2NaCl(aq) + H2O(1)\nMgCl2(aq) + H2O)\nAluminium oxide is amphoteric. It reacts with hot dilute hydrochloric acid and produces aluminium chloride and water.\nand\nAs a Base: Al2O3(s) + 6HCl(29)\nasic\n20,\nand\nthe\nwater\nurous\n2A1C13(aq) + 3H2O(l)\nAluminium oxide also reacts with bases. When concentrated sodium hydroxide solution is added to aluminium oxide, complex ion (sodium tetrahydroxoaluminate (III)) is formed. As an Acid: Al2O3(s) + 2NaOH(aq) +3H2O@\n2NaAl(OH)4(4)\nSiO2 is chemically very inert and weakly acidic. It reacts with hot concentrated sodium hydroxide to produce sodium silicate and water.\nSiO200+2NaOH\nNa2SiO30) + H2O\nPhosphorus oxides are acidic oxides, they react with alkalies to form salts and water Phosphorus trioxide reacts with sodium hydroxide to form sodium phosphite and water.\n4Na,PO3(aq) + 6H2O(1)\nP2066)+12NaOH()\nPhosphorus pentaoxide reacts with sodium hydroxide to form sodium phosphate and water.\n4NaPO4(4)+6H2O\nP4010(3) +12NaOH(aq)\nSulphur dioxide (SO2) reacts with aqueous solution of sodium hydroxide and produces salt (sodium sulphite) and water. Sulphur trioxide (SO3) reacts violently with water to produce sulphuric acid, which reacts with sodium hydroxide and produces salt (sodium sulphate).\n200\nSO2(g) +2NaOH(2) H2SO4+2NaOH\n&#x27;(x)\nNa,SO+H2O Na,SO44)\n+2H2O\n13\nDichlorine heptoxide (C207) reacts directly with sodium hydroxide to give sodium\nchlorate and water.\nCl20) + 2NaOH(aq)\n2NaClO4(aq) + H2O\nDichlorine monoxide (Cl,O) also reacts directly with sodium hydroxide to give sodium\nhypochlorite and water.\nCl2O + 2NaOH\nArgon (Ar) is noble gas and it does not form oxides.\n2NaClO + H2O\n13.1.5 Chlorides of the Period 3rd Elements\nChlorides are compounds formed between chlorine and another element. They are binary compounds, in which chlorine is combined with only one other element. The chlorides of Period 3&quot; elements Na (sodium) to S (sulphur) are given in the following table with their physical properties.\nTable 13.10 Chlorides of the Period 3&quot; Elements and their Physical Properties\nChloride\nPhysical State(20°C)\nAlCl NaCl MgCl AlCl\nSolid Solid\nMelting Point(°C)\n801\n708\n192.4 -69\nSiCl\nSolid Liquid Liquid Liquid\n-93.6\nPCI,\nS2Cl2\n-80\nBoiling Point (&quot;C)\n1465\n1418\n423\n57\n74\n136\nThere are three chlorides of sulphur, but only one is mentioned here i.e. S2Cl2. Al1⁄2CI ̧ is the dimer of aluminium chloride (AICI).\n13.1.5.1 Structure\nważ\nNaCl and MgCl, are ionic and consist of giant ionic lattices at room temperature. AICI, is complicated due to its structural changes as temperature increases. The structure is an ionic lattice - although with a lot of covalent character. PCI, is structurally more complicated. The chlorides of the other elements of this period are simple covalent molecules.\n13.1.5.2 Melting and Boiling Points",
