        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 8",
        "theory": "376\n13.2.1.3 Trend in Electronegativity\nElements of group - IA are metals and are highly electropositive, so their electronegativity values are very low. As we move in group from lithium to caesium their electronegativities. decrease in the same order.\nTable 13.14 Trend in Electronegativity in Group IA Elements\nElements\nElectronegativity\nLi\nNa\nK\nRb\nCs\n0.98\n0.93\n0.82\n0.82\n0.79\n18\nNOT FOR SALE\n13.2.1.4 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points The binding forces between the atoms in crystal lattice of these metals are relatively weak. So these metals are soft and possess low melting and boiling points, which decrease down\nthe group.\nTable 13.15 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points in Group IA Elements\nElements\nLi\nNa\nK\nRb\nCs\nMelting point (&quot;C)\n180.5\n97.8\n63.7\n39.0\n28.6\nBoling point (°C)\n1,326\n883\n756\n688\n690\n13.2.1.5 Trend in Density\nDensity is defined as mass per unit volume of a substance.\nDensity increases with an increase in atomic mass and decreases with an increase in atomic\nvolume.\nGenerally, we see that in alkali metals down the group, both the atomic size and atomic mass increase but the increase in atomic mass compensates the bigger atomic size. As a result, the density of alkali metals increases down the group. Potassium is, however, lighter than sodium.\nTable 13.16 Trend in Density in Group IA Elements\nElements\nLi\nNa\nK\nRb\nCs\nDensity at 20 °C (g/cm3) 0.53\n0.97\n0.86\n1.53\n1.88\n13.2.2\nTrend in Reactivity with Water\nGroup - IA elements are very good reducing agents. They all react vigorously with water\nreducing it to hydrogen gas.\nMo\nM*(aq) + le\nH2Om + le ̄\n12\nH2(g) + OH (24)\nMOH(14) + H2(g)\nMo+ H2O\n(Where M = Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs)\nLithium reacts slowly with water while sodium reacts vigorously. Potassium reacts even more vigorously, while Rb and Cs explode violently in contact with water. They all on reaction with water produce the respective hydroxides.\n19\n13.2.3\nReactions with Oxygen\nNOT FOR SALE\nGroup - IA elements tarnish in air due to the formation of an oxide or hydroxide on the surface. These metals have to be stored without contact with air to prevent their oxidation. Their reactivity increases down the group.\nLi, Na and K are stored in oil, Cs and Rb are normally stored in a sealed glass tubes to prevent air contact.\nThe group - IA elements react with oxygen to form normal oxides with general formula, M2O.\n4M()+O2(g)\n(Where M = Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs)\n13.2.3.1\n2M,0",
