        "theory": "CO2 + H2Ou\nH*)+HCO3 (2)\nCarbon dioxide reacts with cold sodium hydroxide (NaOH) to give either sodium carbonat or sodium hydrogencarbonate solution, depending on the proportion.\nCO+2NaOH(\nCO+NaOH)\n36\nNa,CO)+H2O NaHCO3(29) -\nог\nSilicon dioxide is weakly acidic. It reacts with hot and concentrated sodium hydroxide to NOT FOR SALE give sodium silicate. However, it does not react with water due to its strong covalent\nbonds.\nSiO 200 +2NaOH\nNa,SiO+H2Om\nGermanium, tin and lead dioxides are amphoteric oxides. They can act as an acid as well as a base. S As a base:\nعمل اسیدی\nSnO 200+ 4HCI\nSnCl+2H2O\nFor germanium and tin dioxides, hot and concentrated hydrochloric acid is used. However, for lead dioxide, cold hydrochloric acid is used. This is because, lead dioxide will decompose if the temperature increases.\nAs an acid: SnO26) +2NaOH)\nNa,SnO+H2Om\nFor all reactions, hot and concentrated sodium hydroxide is used.\n13.5\nGroup-VIIA Elements: Halogens\nconjunction of (you combined.\nسب مل کر\nGroup-VIIA comprise the elements fluorine (F), chlorine (CI), bromine (Br), iodine (1), and astatine (At). The elements are collectively known as halogens due to their salts forming tendency. All these elements are non-metals.\n13.5.1\nAtomic and Physical Properties\nElements of Group - VIIA (halogens) possess seven electrons in the outermost (valence) shell, the characteristics feature being &#x27;ns np in the outer most shell. The elements and their atomic number etc. are given in Table 13.24.\nTable 13.24 Elements Symbol with Atomic no and Their Electronic Configuration\nElements\n9F\n17CI\n35 Br\n53 I\nElectronic Configuration\n1s2, 2s2, 2p\n[He] 2s, 2p3\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p3\n[Ne] 3s2, 3p\n1s2, 2s2, 2po, 3s2, 3po, 4s2, 3d00, 4p3\n[Ar]3d10, 4s2, 4ps\nX\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d10, 4p6, 5s2, 4d10, 5p5 [Kr]4d0, 5s2, 5p5\n37\nHK\nNOT FOR SALE\nHalogens in the uncombined state exist as diatomic covalent molecules (F2, Cl, etc). These discrete molecules are held together by weak Van der Waals&#x27;s forces, which explain the volatile nature of these elements. These elements are also poisonous in nature.\n13.5.1.1 Trend in Atomic Radius\n-\nAtomic radii of Group – VII A elements increase down the group as shown in Table 13.25. Table 13.25 Trend in Atomic Radius in Group -VIIA elements\nHalogen Covalent Radius\n(pm)\n9F\n17CI\n71\n99\nTend\n13.5.1.2 Trend in Electronegativity Electronegativity of Group-VII A elements decreases gradually from fluorine to iodine down the group as shown below.\n35 Br\n114\n133\n- allvar felections logros a Tidbit\nOnanica (barel\nFluorine is the most electronegative element of the periodic table.\nTable 13.26 Trend in Electronegativity in Group-VIIA Elements\nHalogen\nF\nCI\nBr\nI\nElectronegativity\n4.0\n3.0\n2.8\n2.5\n13.5.1.3 Trend in Electron Affinity\nElectron affinity greatly influences the chemical behaviour of atoms by accepting one or more electrons. Electron affinity of an element is defined as &quot;the amount of energy released when an electron is accepted by an atom in the gaseous state to form an anion,&quot; ie.\nCluste\nAH=-349kJmol\nAs the energy is released, so electron affinity is given the negative sign. Electron affinity is the measure of the attraction of the nucleus of an atom for the extra electron.",
