      "C12O",
      "The oxides in the top row are the oxides where period 3 elements are in their highest oxidation state. In these oxides, all the outer electrons in period 3 elements are being involved in bonding from just one in sodium to all seven outer electrons of chlorine in Cl2O,. The oxides (high oxidation state) of sodium, magnesium, aluminium, silicon, phosphorous are in solid state and those of sulphur and chlorine are in liquid state. The oxides having low oxidation state of sulphur and chlorine are in gaseous state.",
      "10",
      "13.1.3.1 Structure",
      "Sodium, magnesium and aluminium oxides consist of giant structures containing metal ions and oxide ions. Magnesium oxide is similar in structure to sodium chloride. The crystalline aluminium oxide is known as corundum, in which oxygen ions form nearly hexagonal close-packed structure with the aluminium ions.",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Tidbit",
      "Corundum, naturally occurring that is, after diamond, the hardest aluminum oxide mineral (Al,O,) known natural substance.",
      "Silicon dioxide is covalent oxide having giant structure. There are three different crystal forms of silicon dioxide; the most suitable structure is similar to that of diamond. Phosphorus, sulphur and chlorine form molecular oxides. Some of these molecules are simple, while others are polymeric. Thus, the trend in structure in period is",
      "Giant metallic ionic oxides",
      "13.1.3.2 Melting and Boiling Points",
      "giant covalent oxide",
      "molecular oxide.",
      "The giant structures (the metal oxides and silicon oxide) have high melting points and boiling points because a lot of energy is needed to break the strong bonds (ionic or covalent) operating in their lattices. The oxides of phosphorous, sulphur and chlorine consist of individual molecules, some small and simple, others polymeric.",
      "present in",
      "The attractive forces between these molecules will be dispersion forces and dipole-dipole interactions. The strength of the attractive forces depend on the size, shape and polarity of various molecules but are always much weaker than the ionic or covalent bonds a giant structure. These oxides are gases, liquids or low-melting solids. Table 13.8 Melting and Boiling Points of Period 34 Oxides",
