    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "s and p-Block Elements",
        "def": "To easily understand the properties of the elements in the period table, the elements are placed in vertical columns and horizontal rows. The vertical columns are called groups or families and the horizontal rows are called the periods. The elements in the periodic table are divided into s, p, d and f blocks. Those elements in which last electrons occupy &#x27;s subshell (orbital) are called s-block elements. The s-block elements are at the extreme left of the periodic table and placed in Group IA (alkali metals) and Group IIA (alkaline earth metals) elements. Those elements in which last electrons occupy p subshell are called p-block elements. The p-block elements are placed in Groups IIIA, IVA, VA, VIA, VIIA\nBoth s-block and p-block elements are normal elements. s-block elements consist of only metals but p-block elements include both metals and non-metals.\nIn previous grades, you have learnt about the periodic classification of elements in groups and periods. You have also learnt the basics about the 1&quot;, 2&quot; period elements. In this unit you will study more detail about the periodic trends and some properties of period (Na to Ar), Groups IA, IIA, IVA and VIIA elements.\nPeriod 3 elements consists of sodium (Na), magnesium (Mg), aluminium (Al), silicon (Si), phosphorous (P), sulphur (S), chlorine (CI) and argon (Ar). Sodium and magnesium are metals and belong to s-block. Aluminum (metal), silicon (metalloid), phosphorus, sulphur, chlorine and argon are non-metals and belong to p-block."
      }
    ],
