        "theory": "Br Kr\nRb\nSr\n38 39 Y\n41\n42\n43\n44\n45\n46\n47\n49\n50\n51\n52\n53\n54\nZr\nNb Mo\nTe\nRu\nRh\nPd\nAg Cd\nIn Sn\nSb\nTe\nXe\n56 57 72 73\n74\n75\n76 77\n78\n79 80\n84\n85\n86\nBa\nLa Hf\nTa W\nRe\nOs Ir Pt\nHg HOT\nPo At\nRn\nFr\n87 88 89 104 105 106\nRa Ae Rf Db Sg Bb\n107 108\n109 110 Mt Ds\n111 112 113 114\nRg Cn Uut FT\n58\n59 60 61\n63\nf-block\nOTRE\nCe Pr\nPr Nd Pm Sm Eu\n64 65 66 67 Gd Td Dy Ho\nΕΤΑ\n68 Er Tm Yb Lu\n69 70 71\n90\n91 Th Pa\nU\nAm Cm Bk Cf\nFm Md No\nLr\n92 93 94 95 96 97 98 99 100 101 102 103\nNp Pu Am Co\nFigure 14.1 Demarcation of the Periodic Table\n47\n*\nElements from caesium (atomic number 58) to lutetium (atomic number 71) make first series of f block elements known as lanthanides. In this series of 14 metallic elements, 4f subshell are progressively filled. Elements from thorium (atomic number 90) to lawrencium (atomic number 103) make the second series of f-block elements, the actinides, in which the 5f subshell is progressively filled. The lanthanides and actinides together comprise the f-block elements, or inner transition elements.\nTidbit\nNOT FOR SALE\nTypical and Non-Typical Transition Elements Group IIB Elements (Zn, Cd and Hg) do not have a partially filled d-subshell either as do they show typical properties of transition elements or in any of their ionic state. Neither elements to any appreciable extent (except) complex formation).\nof\nSimilarly, IIIB (Sc, Y and La) is another group elements, which do not show many of the properties associated with typical transition",
