        "theory": "Figure 14.6 Geometrical shapes of complexes.\nMost of the compounds of the transition metals are coloured in solid or solution state. In transition elements, the d orbitals are responsible for the development of colour in the transition compound. When these orbitals form bonds with the ligands, they splits up, e.g in octahedral complexes they divide into two energy levels, one set has higher energy than the other. The electrons residing in low energy d-orbital absorb a part of the visible light and jump to higher energy d-orbital. This process is called d-d transition. When light is allowed to fall on a substance, it absorbs from it the light of a particular colour whose wavelength is in the visible region (4000 7000Å) and reflects or transmits back the remaining light which is\nTidbit\nComplementary Colours\nColours directly opposite each other on the colour wheel are said to be complementary colours. Blue and yellow are complementary colours; red and cyan are complementary; and so are green and magenta. Copper (II) sulphate solution is pale blue (cyan) because it absorbs light in the red region of the spectrum. Thus, cyan is the complementary colour of red.\nYellow\nRed\nMagenta\nGreen\nCyan\nBlue\nin\n62\n¥\nNOT FOR SALE\nthe colour complementary to that of the absorbed light. The colour of the transmitted light is different from the colour of the absorbed light and is known as the complementary colour of the absorbed light. The colour of the transition metal complex is the colour of this transmitted light.\nIf the complex absorbs all colours except orange, the complex appears orange. The complex will also appear orange if it absorbs only light of the colour blue. In a complementary manner, if the complex ion absorbed only orange, it would appear blue. Blue and orange are referred to as complementary colours. In [Ti(H2O)], yellow and green lights are absorbed, while most of the blue and red lights are transmitted, therefore the solution of [Ti(H2O)&lt;]* ions looks purple in colour.\nIncoming 2.\ne jumps to higher level\ncg\nt2g\nAbsorbed λ\neg\nt2g\nTransmitted 7.\nFigure 14.7 Absorbance and transmittance of light in [Ti(H2O)] and splitting of d-orbital. The ions which have completely filled or empty d-orbitals are colourless e.g. complexes of Cu*(3d), Zn2+(3d) and Sc3(3do), Ti** (3d) are colourless.\nTable 14.8 Colours and outer Electronic Configurations of Transition Metal lons\nOuter configuration\nIon\nColour\nSc3+\n3d\nColourless\nTi3+\n3d1\nPurple\nTi4+\n3d\nColourless\nV3+\n3d2\nGreen\nCr3+\n3d3\nViolet\nMn2+\n3d3\nMn3+\n3d4\nFe2+\n3d6\nFe3+\n3d5\nLight pink\nViolet\nLight yellow\nYellow\n63\nCo\n3d7\nNi 2+\n3do\nCu\nNOT FOR SALE\nPink\nGreen\n3810\nColourless\nCu2+ Zn2+\n3d&quot;\nBlue ·\nColourless\n14.3 Chemistry of Some important Transition Elements\n14.3.1 Vanadium\nVanadium symbol &quot;V&quot;, silvery white metallic element with an atomic number of 23 and atomic mass 50.941 a.m.u. Vanadium is used for vanadium steels, a particularly hard alloy employed for knife blades and various workshop tools.\n14.3.1.1 Oxidation States\nOxidation states of atoms depend upon unpaired electrons in d-orbitals. Compounds of vanadium in +5, +4, +3 and +2 oxidation states are important.\nTidbit\nDifference between Valency and Oxidation Number\nValency is different from the oxidation number. Thus, the valency of nitrogen is 3, whereas it can have oxidation numbers from -3 to +5. The oxidation",
