        "id": "14.13",
        "title": "14.3.2.3 Reduction of Chromate (VI) lons with Zn and an Acid",
        "theory": "Conc. H2SO,\nCH,\nketone\niii.\n2-propanol(Secondary alcohol)\nTertiary Alcohols\nTertiary alcohols are not oxidized by acidified sodium or potassium dichromate(VI)\nsolution.\n67\nNOT FOR SALE\n14.3.2.5 Potassium Dichromate as an Oxidizing Agent in Titrations\nIn redox titration, a standard solution of potassium dichromate (K,Cr,O,) is used to\nTidbit Mohr&#x27;s salt\ndetermine the unknown concentration of a Ferrous ammonium sulphate or Mohr&#x27;s solution of Fe2. Dichromate ion reduces to salt [FeSO, (NH),SO, 6H,O].\nchromium (III) ions.\nThe half-equation for the dichromate (VI)\nion is:\nCr2O,+ 14H++ 6e\nand Fe(II) is oxidized to Fe(III)\n6Fe\nCombining these equations gives:\nCr,O,+6Fe+ 14H\nOrange\nDetermining the unknown concentration of a solution of Fe, is actually titration\nagainst the Mohr&#x27;s salt.\nThe molecular form of the equation can be written as,\n2Cr31 + 7H2O\n6Fe++ 6e\n2Cr&quot; + 6Fe&quot; + 7H20,\nGreen\nK,Cr2O, + 6FeSO, + 7H,SO, K2SO, + Cr2 (SO), + 7H2O+3 Fe, (SO), 14.3.3 Manganese\nManganese is the third most abundant transition metal after iron and titanium. Manganese, (Mn), is silvery white, brittle metallic element used principally in making alloys. The atomic number of manganese is 25 and atomic mass 54.938 a.m.u.\n14.3.3.1 Oxidation States\nAccording to the electronic configuration of manganese (3d3, 4s2), it shows a maximum oxidation state of +7. In addition to this state, it also shows lower oxidation states such as +6, +4, +3 and +2.\nManganese compounds in which &quot;Mn&#x27; is in +7 oxidation state, are restricted to the unstable oxide Mn,O, and compounds of intensely purple permanganate ion (MnO4) and are powerful oxidizing agents. Mno* is present in MnO, in the salt of manganic acid (H,MnO,). The free acid is unknown. Its salts are called manganates e.g. KMnO4-\nThe compounds having Mn&quot; are very limited in number. The most familiar\ncompound of Mn** is MnO,.\n68\nחו\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe compound of Mn3 is called manganic compound. In aqueous solution Mn+ ions is quite unstable since it is easily reduced to Mn2+ ions e.g. H.Mn,O,, Mn,(SO),.\nMn+ le\nMn2+\n+2 is the most stable oxidation state of Mn in neutral or acidic solution. Mn2+ ion exists as pale pink hexaaquamanganese(II) [Mn(H,O),], which is quite stable to oxidation. The stability of Mn2+ is due to half-filled 3d orbital.\nThe compounds of Mn2+ are called manganous compounds such as MnO, MnCO,, MnSO.. MnCl, etc.\n14.3.3.2 Potassium Manganate (VII) as an Oxidizing Agent in Organic Chemistry Potassium manganate (VII) is a powerful oxidizing agent. It is usually used in neutral or alkaline solution in organic chemistry. Acidified potassium manganate (VII) tends to be destructively strong oxidizing agent, breaking carbon-carbon bonds. The potassium manganate (VII) solution is usually made mildly alkaline with sodium carbonate solution and the typical colour changes are:\na\nOriginal dark purple\nReduced to dark green\nFurther reduced to\nmanganate (VII) ion\nmanganate (VI) ion-\nblack precipitate of MnO,\nMnO\nMnO2-\ni.\nIn Testing for a C-C Double Bond\nPotassium manganate (VII) oxidizes carbon-carbon double bonds and so goes through the colour changes. The colour change depends on whether the potassium manganate (VII) is used under acidic or alkaline conditions. Ethene, for example, is oxidized to ethane-1,2-diol.\nCH, =CH, +H,O+[O]\n-\nCH2 — CH2\nOH OH\nUnder acidic conditions, the manganate (VII) ions are reduced to manganese (II)\nions.\nX\n69\nSCH, CH, +2H,0 +2MnO,+6H\nNOT FOR SALE\n5CH-CH,+ 2Mn\nOH OH\nUnder alkaline condition, the manganate (VII) ions (MnO4) are first reduced to\ngreen manganate (VI) ions.\nCH, CH, +2Mn0, +20H\nCH, CH2+ 2MnO\nOH\nон\nDark green solution\nThen dark brown solid manganese (IV) oxide MnO2 (manganese dioxide) is\nformed as a result of further reduction of MnO\n2CH, CH, +2MnO2+4H2O",
