        "theory": "number is the hypothetical charge of an atom in a molecule or ion, and it is measure of its apparent capacity to gain or lose electrons within that species.\nThe compounds in the lower oxidation state are good reducing agents. They also have ionic character and due to incomplete electron sub-shell they are also coloured. The compounds in the higher oxidation states are generally colourless due to the empty electron shell. When a colourless compound of V(3d°, 45°) is reduced by suitable reducing agent, it first turns blue VO2+ (3d1, 4so), then green V3(3d2, 4s&quot;) and finally violet V2+(3d, 4s). Vanadium also shows +1,0,-1 oxidation states in the compounds [V(dipy)s] 1+, V(CO), and [V(CO)6] respectively.\n14.3.1.2 As Catalyst in Contact Process\nThe contact process is the common method of producing sulphuric acid in high concentrations needed for industrial process.\nVanadium pentoxide (V,O,) is used as catalyst in various oxidation reactions\nin\n64\nNOT FOR SALE involving the use of oxygen (O2) as the oxidizing agent e.g. conversion of SO, to SO, in the manufacture of H2SO,.\nVO, (400-450°C)\n2S0200\n+ O2(2)\n250\n14.3.2 Chromium\nChromium, symbol Cr, atomic number 24 and atomic mass is 51.99 a.m.u, is a shiny metallic element. Chromium metal provides a shiny protective coating for iron and, steel surfaces due to brilliant and corrosion resistant nature. Chromium metal is not inert in itself; instead, it forms a very thin, tough and transparent oxide coating that gives protection.\n14.3.2.1 Oxidation States\nChromium forms three common oxidation states in its compounds, +2, +3 and +6. Chromium in +3 oxidation state is most stable, while Cr(+2) compounds are not much stable and are strong reducing agents, while those of Cr(+6) compounds are strong oxidizing agents.\nWith the increase in oxidation state of the chromium,\na. The acidic character of its oxides also increases.\nOxides\nOxidation state of Cr\nNature\nCro\nCr20\nCros\n+2\n+3\n+6\nBasic\nAmphoteric\nAcidic\nb. Similarly the covalent character of the compounds also increases. Thus, Cr(+2) compounds are ionic, Cr(+3) compounds ionize to some extent, while those of Cr(+6) are covalent.\nThe compounds in which chromium shows +2 oxidation state are called chromous salts. These salts are unstable and tend to oxidize on exposure to air into corresponding Cr(+3) compounds. Some of chromous salts are CrCl,, CrS, CrSO,.7H2O, CrCO, etc. The compounds in which chromium shows +3 oxidation state are called chromic compounds. These may be derived from chromic oxide, CO,. These are formed as chromate or dichromate. Examples of chromic compounds are chromic chloride (CrCl, ), chromic sulphate, Cr2(SO,),.18H2O.\n65\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe compounds in which chromium shows +6 oxidation state are chromium trioxide (CrO,), potassium chromate (K,CrO,) and potassium dichromate (K,Cr,O,) etc.\n14.3.2.2 Chromate - Dichromate Equilibrium\nWhen solid potassium chromate, K,CO, is dissolved in water it forms a yellow solution. When solid potassium dichromate, K,Cr,O, is dissolved in water the resulting solution is orange. The colours come from the negative ions Cro, and Cr0, However, in solution these ions are actually in equilibrium as indicated by the equation:\n2CrO,+2H\n(g)\n(mg)⋅\nCr,0,2)+ H2O\nThis is dynamic equilibrium and sensitive to the acidity and basicity of solution. According to Le-Chatelier&#x27;s principle, the addition of acid to the reactant shifts the equilibrium towards right and yields more dichromate (orange). On the other hand, the addition of base promotes the conversion of dichromate to chromate.\n20H+Cr0,\nOrange\n2CrO2 + H2O Yellow\n14.3.2.3 Reduction of Chromate (VI) lons with Zn and an Acid\nDichromate (VI) ions (for example, in potassium dichromate (VI) solution) can be reduced to chromium (III) ions and chromium (II) ions using zinc and either dilute sulphuric acid or hydrochloric acid.\nAcidic potassium dichromate solution reacts with zinc forming Cr3+ ions and colour of the solution changes from orange to green.\nFor reduction from +6 to +3,\nC120 + 14H* + 3Zn\n2Cr&quot; + 7H2O+ 3Zn\nand then after further reduction by the zinc, Cr+2 ions are formed and colour changes from green to blue.\nFor reduction from +3 to +2\nin\n2Cr13 + Zn\n66\n2Cr2+ + Zn2*\nNOT FOR SALE\n14.3.2.4 Potassium Dichromate as an Oxidizing Agent in Organic Chemistry Potassium dichromate (VI) is used as an oxidizing agent in organic reactions. The solution of potassium dichromate (VI) is acidified with dilute sulphuric acid. When oxidation occurs, the orange solution containing the dichromate (VI) ions is reduced to a green solution containing chromium (III) ions.\ni. Primary Alcohols\nPrimary alcohol are oxidized to either aldehydes or carboxylic acids depending on the reaction conditions.\nIn the case of the formation of carboxylic acids, the alcohol is first oxidized to an aldehyde, which is then oxidized further to the acid.\nPartial Oxidation to Aldehydes\nWe get an aldehyde if we use an excess of alcohol and remove aldehyde as soon as it is formed in order to avoid further oxidation.\n3CH,CH,OH + C120, + 8H\nprimary alcohol\n3CH,CHO+ 2Cr2 + 7H2O Acetaldehyde\nFull Oxidation to Carboxylic Acids\nThe alcohol is heated with an excess of the oxidizing agent i.e. potassium dichromate (VI), on completion of the reaction, the carboxylic acid is produced.\n3CH,CH,OH+2C10,\nprimary alcohol\n+16H*\n3CH,COOH + 4Cr* + 11H,0\nethanoic acid\nii. Secondary Alcohols\nSecondary alcohols are oxidized to ketones. When the secondary alcohol, 2-propanol is heated with potassium dichromate (VI) solution acidified with dilute sulphuric acid, ketone (propanone) is produced.\nOH\nK,Cr2O,\nCH, C-H +[0]\nCH,-C-CH, +H2O",
