        "sciNote": "Conc. H2SO,\nCH,\nketone\niii.\n2-propanol(Secondary alcohol)\nTertiary Alcohols\nTertiary alcohols are not oxidized by acidified sodium or potassium dichromate(VI)\nsolution.\n67\nNOT FOR SALE\n14.3.2.5 Potassium Dichromate as an Oxidizing Agent in Titrations\nIn redox titration, a standard solution of potassium dichromate (K,Cr,O,) is used to\nTidbit Mohr's salt\ndetermine the unknown concentration of a Ferrous ammonium sulphate or Mohr's solution of Fe2. Dichromate ion reduces to salt [FeSO, (NH),SO, 6H,O].\nchromium (III) ions.\nThe half-equation for the dichromate (VI)\nion is:\nCr2O,+ 14H++ 6e\nand Fe(II) is oxidized to Fe(III)\n6Fe\nCombining these equations gives:\nCr,O,+6Fe+ 14H\nOrange\nDetermining the unknown concentration of a solution of Fe, is actually titration\nagainst the Mohr's salt.\nThe molecular form of the equation can be written as,\n2Cr31 + 7H2O\n6Fe++ 6e\n2Cr\" + 6Fe\" + 7H20,\nGreen\nK,Cr2O, + 6FeSO, + 7H,SO, K2SO, + Cr2 (SO), + 7H2O+3 Fe, (SO), 14.3.3 Manganese\nManganese is the third most abundant transition metal after iron and titanium. Manganese, (Mn), is silvery white, brittle metallic element used principally in making alloys. The atomic number of manganese is 25 and atomic mass 54.938 a.m.u.\n14.3.3.1 Oxidation States\nAccording to the electronic configuration of manganese (3d3, 4s2), it shows a maximum oxidation state of +7. In addition to this state, it also shows lower oxidation states such as +6, +4, +3 and +2.\nManganese compounds in which \"Mn' is in +7 oxidation state, ar"
