        "sciNote": "The net reaction is given as,\n3CH, CH, +2MnO; +4H,O\n2CH-CH2+ 2MnO, + 40H-\nOH\nOH\nDark brown precipitate\n3CH, CH, + 2Mn0, + 20H\nT\nOH\nOH\nDark brown precipitate\nii.\nCH,\nIn the Oxidation of Aromatic Side Chains Alkaline potassium manganate (VII) solution oxidizes any hydrocarbon side chain attached to a benzene ring back to a single -COOH group. Prolonged heating is necessary. For example: as shown in the figure.\nIn case of the ethyl side chain, carbon dioxide is also produced but\nCH,CH,\nin each case, the main product is benzoic acid.\nAlkaline MnO,\nHeat\nawa\nCOOH\nBenzoic acid\n14.3.3.3 Potassium Manganate (VII) as an Oxidizing Agent in Titrations KMnO, is a strong oxidizing agent with intense dark purple colour. During reduction, the purple permanganate ion changes into colourless Mn ion.\nPotassium manganate (VII) solution is used to find the concentration of all sorts of reducing agents. It is always used in acidic solution.\nFor example, it oxidizes\n70\nin\nNOT FOR SALE\nIron(II) ions to iron(III) ions\nFe\nacidic\nFee\nHydrogen peroxide solution to oxygen\nH2O,\nSulphite ions (IV) to sulphate ions (VI) ions.\nSo+H2O\n02+ 2H + 2e\nSO+ 2H + 2e\nIn each case, the half-equation for the manganate (VII) ions in acidic solution is:\n14.3.4\nMnO;+8H+ Se\nIron\nMn2+ 4H,O\nIron is the second most abundant metal in the Earth's crust. Concentrated deposits of iron ore are found in many localities, thus making it easy to mine. Iron is a transition. metal with a symbol \"Fe\" and atomic number 26 and its atomic ma"
