        "sciNote": "Hydroxide ions (sodium hydroxide solution) remove hydrogen ions from the water ligands attached to the copper ion.\nThe hydrogen ion has been removed from two of the water molecules, as a result a complex with no charge (a neutral complex) is produced. This complex is insoluble in water and so precipitation occurs.\n[Cu(H2O),]*+ 20H\nBlue solution\n[Cu(H2O), (OH)] + 2H2O\nBlue ppt\nReaction of Hexaaquacopper (II) Ions with Ammonia\nThe ammonia acts both a base and a ligand. When a small amount of ammonia is added to solution of hexaaquacopper (II) ions, it pulls out the H+ ions from the complex ion. and produce neutral complex.\n[Cu(H2O),]2 + 2NH,\nBlue (solution)\nThe precipitate dissolves in excess ammonia.\n[Cu(H2O),(OH),] + 2NH;\nBlue ppt\nThe ammonia replaces water as a ligand to give tetraamminediaquacopper (II) ions.\n[Cu(H2O),(OH)2] + 4NH,\n[Cu(NH,),(H2O),]**+ 2H2O+20H\nBlue ppt\n75\nBlue solution\nNOT FOR SALE\nReaction of Hexaaquacopper (11) Ions with Carbonate Ions\nThe hexaaquacopper (II) ions are not acidic enough to release CO, from carbonates. In these cases precipitation of metal carbonate takes place and gives rise to the following reaction.\nCu + CO (aq)\nSociety, Technology And Science\nCuCO\n♦ Pigments give colour and opacity to paints. Amongst the organic pigments, particularly\nimportant are azo—, phthalocyanine and anthraquinone derivatives.\nTransition metals and their oxides when dissolved form coloured pigments.\nDifferent transition metals and their oxides pigments are known f"
