      "14.2.3 Shapes of Complex Ions with Coordination Number 2, 4 and 6 Coordination compounds have definite geometrical shapes because the bond formed between central metal atom and the ligands are directional in nature. Hence, the ligands are arranged around central metal atom in a clear spatial arrangement. This arrangement depends on the number of ligands (co-ordination number) and the relative sizes of the metal ion and the ligands. As the ligand gets larger, fewer of them can coordinate to the metal ion. Thus, iron(III) is able to coordinate to six fluorides in [FeF6] but to only four chlorides in [FeCl4]\"",
      "in",
      "There are various geometries for the coordination complexes. The most common geometries for coordination complexes are tetrahedral geometry, square planar geometry and octahedral geometry. Complexes in which the coordination number is 4 have two geometries-tetrahedral and square planar. Complexes with a coordination number of 6 always have an octahedral geometry. The coordination complex ions with coordination number 2, 4 and 6 is discussed here in detail.",
      "2-Coordinated Complex Ions",
