      "66",
      "2Cr2+ + Zn2*",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "14.3.2.4 Potassium Dichromate as an Oxidizing Agent in Organic Chemistry Potassium dichromate (VI) is used as an oxidizing agent in organic reactions. The solution of potassium dichromate (VI) is acidified with dilute sulphuric acid. When oxidation occurs, the orange solution containing the dichromate (VI) ions is reduced to a green solution containing chromium (III) ions.",
      "i. Primary Alcohols",
      "Primary alcohol are oxidized to either aldehydes or carboxylic acids depending on the reaction conditions.",
      "In the case of the formation of carboxylic acids, the alcohol is first oxidized to an aldehyde, which is then oxidized further to the acid.",
      "Partial Oxidation to Aldehydes",
      "We get an aldehyde if we use an excess of alcohol and remove aldehyde as soon as it is formed in order to avoid further oxidation.",
