      "[Fe(H2O),]3* + 3NH,",
      "[Fe(H2O),(OH),] + 3NH;",
      "14.3.4.5 Reactions of Iron (II) and (III) lons with Carbonate and",
      "Thiocyanate Ions",
      "There is an important difference between the behaviour of iron (II) and iron (III) ions.",
      "*",
      "73",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Iron (II) Ions and Carbonate Tons",
      "When iron (II) ion combines with carbonate ion, it produces precipitate of iron (II)",
      "carbonate.",
      "Fe",
      "(94)",
      "+ CO12",
      "Co",
      "Iron (III) Ions and Carbonate Ions",
      "FCCO 300",
      "The hexaaquairon (III) ion is acidic to react with the weakly basic carbonate ion. If we add sodium carbonate solution to a solution of hexaaquairon (III) ions, it produces the",
      "precipitate of metal hydroxide.",
      "In this reaction, the carbonate ions remove hydrogen ions from the hexaaqua ion and",
      "produce the neutral complex.",
      "Depending on the properties of carbonates ions to hexaaqua ions, we will get either hydrogen carbonate ions or carbon dioxide gas which is formed by the reaction between the hydrogen ions and carbonate ions. The more usually used equation shows the",
      "formation of carbon dioxide.",
      "2[Fe(H,0),]\" + 3C0;",
      "2[Fe(H,O),(OH),] + 3CO, + 3H2O",
      "Reaction of Iron (III) ions with Thiocyanate ions",
      "Iron(III) ions reacts with thiocyanate ions, SCN (from sodium or potassium or ammonium thiocyanate solution) and an intense blood red solution containing the ion [Fe(SCN)(H,O),] is produced. This test confirms the presence of iron (III) ion in",
      "solution.",
      "14.3.5 Copper",
      "e in",
      "Copper (Cu), is brownish-red metallic element that is one of the most widely used metals. Copper is one of the two common yellow metals (the other being gold), although a thin coating of copper (1) oxide, Cu,O, often makes copper look reddish. The atomic number of copper is 29 and its atomic mass is 63.546 a.m.u.",
      "14.3.5.1 Oxidation States",
      "Copper shows a variety of oxidation states ranging from +1 to +4 in its compounds. The two principal oxidation states of copper are +1 and +2.",
      "⚫ Copper (1) compounds are expected to be diamagnetic in nature and are usually colourless. Examples of these compounds are Cu,O, CuCl, CuBr etc. In solid compounds copper (1) is often the more stable state at moderate temperatures. The compounds of this ion are called cuprous compounds.",
      "74",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "The copper (II) ion is usually more stable in aqueous solutions. Compounds of this ion are called cupric compounds and are usually coloured. Examples are CuO, CuF, CuCl, CuCO, CuSO, etc.",
      "Copper (III) is most characteristically found in oxides. A simple example is potassium cuprate, KCuOz, a blue-black solid. The best studied copper (III) compounds are the cuprate superconductors. Yttrium barium copper oxide (YBa Cu,O,) consists of both Cu (II) and Cu(III) centres.",
      "Like oxide, fluoride is a highly basic anion and is known to stabilize metal ions in high oxidation states. Indeed, both copper (III) and even copper (IV) fluorides are known, K,CuF, and Cs,CuF, respectively.",
      "14.3.5.2 The Reaction of Hexaaquacopper (II) Ions with Hydroxide Ions,",
      "Ammonia and Carbonate Ions",
      "Reactions of Hexaaquacopper (II) Ions with Hydroxide Ions",
      "Hydroxide ions (sodium hydroxide solution) remove hydrogen ions from the water ligands attached to the copper ion.",
      "The hydrogen ion has been removed from two of the water molecules, as a result a complex with no charge (a neutral complex) is produced. This complex is insoluble in water and so precipitation occurs.",
      "[Cu(H2O),]*+ 20H",
      "Blue solution",
      "[Cu(H2O), (OH)] + 2H2O",
      "Blue ppt",
      "Reaction of Hexaaquacopper (II) Ions with Ammonia",
      "The ammonia acts both a base and a ligand. When a small amount of ammonia is added to solution of hexaaquacopper (II) ions, it pulls out the H+ ions from the complex ion. and produce neutral complex.",
      "[Cu(H2O),]2 + 2NH,",
      "Blue (solution)",
      "The precipitate dissolves in excess ammonia.",
      "[Cu(H2O),(OH),] + 2NH;",
      "Blue ppt",
      "The ammonia replaces water as a ligand to give tetraamminediaquacopper (II) ions.",
      "[Cu(H2O),(OH)2] + 4NH,",
      "[Cu(NH,),(H2O),]**+ 2H2O+20H",
      "Blue ppt",
      "75",
      "Blue solution",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Reaction of Hexaaquacopper (11) Ions with Carbonate Ions",
      "The hexaaquacopper (II) ions are not acidic enough to release CO, from carbonates. In these cases precipitation of metal carbonate takes place and gives rise to the following reaction.",
      "Cu + CO (aq)",
      "Society, Technology And Science",
      "CuCO",
      "♦ Pigments give colour and opacity to paints. Amongst the organic pigments, particularly",
      "important are azo—, phthalocyanine and anthraquinone derivatives.",
      "Transition metals and their oxides when dissolved form coloured pigments.",
      "Different transition metals and their oxides pigments are known for their specific colour",
      "The most common pigment is white titanium dioxide (titanium (TV) oxide) which provides over 70% of total pigments used. This pigment has a high refractive index and gives a gloss to the paint.",
      "Another widely used inorganic pigment is finely divided calcium carbonate. This has a low refractive index and is used, together with titanium dioxide, to produce 'matt' paints. (Matt paint is defined by experts as dull. It does not shine at all and dries to a smooth finish that hides surface imperfection)",
      "Other pigments include iron oxides (black, yellow and red), zinc oxide, Co (II) nitrate (red), K,Cr2O, (orange), K,CrO, (yellow), Ni (II) Chloride (green) and KMnO (purple).",
      "awaz",
      "76",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Key Points",
      "Transition elements are those elements, which have partially filled d or f orbitals either in their atomic state or in any common oxidation states.",
      "Binding energies, oxidation states and paramagnetism of transition metals increase with increasing number of unpaired electrons.",
      "Transition elements can exist in several oxidation states.",
      "Transition metal compounds are often coloured because of d-d orbital transition. When a complex ion absorbs a wavelength from visible light, it transmits a set of radiation that impart colour",
      "Those substances, which are attracted in magnetic field, are called paramagnetic substances and the phenomenon is known as paramagnetism.",
      "A ligand is an atom or ion or neutral molecule with one or more lone pairs of electrons available to donate to a transition metal ion.",
      "Transition elements form complexes by combining with ligands. Ligands bond to transition metal ions by one or more co-ordinate covalent bonds.",
      "Monodentate ligands possess only one donor site.",
      "Multidentate or polydentate ligands possess more than one donor sites",
      "Coordination sphere is the central atom along with the surrounding ligands enclosed in a square bracket.",
      "Transition metal atoms have similarity in their structure hence they are able to replace one another in the metallic lattice and form alloys.",
      "Coordination compounds have definite geometrical shapes because the bond formed between central metal atom and the ligands are directional in nature. Ionic and covalent compounds of transition metals are mostly coloured.",
      "All forms of Cr (VI) are powerful oxidizing agents.",
      "Many reactions involving transition elements are redox reactions. Some redox reactions are used in titrations to determine concentrations.",
      "Fe (II) compounds are octahedral in geometry.",
      "Permagnate (VII) ions are strong oxidizing agent, oxidize ethane to ethyl alcohol.",
