        "theory": "CH,\nisobutane (a branched chain compound)\n1 open chains, which may\nii. Closed chain or cyclic Organic Compounds (Alicyclic compounds) In closed chain compounds carbon atoms are arranged in closed chain forms. They may be homocyclic or heterocyclic organic compounds.\nHomocyclic, which are also known as carbocyclic organic compounds, are those in which cycle or ring is made only of carbon atoms. They are further divided into alicyclic having properties similar to open chain compounds and aromatic in which benzene ring is present.\n89\nStraight chain\nAcyclic\nBranched chain\nOrganic compounds.\nNOT FOR SALE\nCyclic\nHomocyclic\nHeterocylic\nAlicyclic\nAromatic\nOn the other hand, heterocyclic organic compounds are those cyclic compounds in which ring contain at least one atom other than carbon.\nCH\nor\nCH-CH2\nΔ.\nCyclopropane\nCH-CH\nог\nCH2—CH2 Cyclobutane\nAlicyclic\nOrganic\nCompounds\nCyclohexane\nSimilarly\nor\nCH\nCH\nCH\n대|대\nCH,\nCH\nCH\nBenzene\nCH\nToluene\nNaphthalene\nAromatic (Homocyclic)\nOrganic Compounds\nn\nPyridine\nThiopene\nFuran\nN\nH\nHeterocyclic Organic Compounds\nPyrrole\nW\nTidbit\nb. Classification on the basis of Functional group An atoms or a group of atoms or multiple bonds that gives certain characteristic properties to an organic compound is called a functional group because it is the chemically active or functional part of a molecule. Each functional group represents a different class of organic compounds.\nAlkanes contain only single bonds and having general formula is C,H., where\n1,2,3,... shows number of carbon atoms e.g. methane (CH,), ethane (C,H,)etc.\nDifferent classes of organic compounds, their functional groups and general\nformulae are summarized in the following table along with example.\n90\n¥\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable: 15.1 Classification of Organic Compounds Based on Functional Groups\nClass\nof Compounds\nName of\nFunctional Group\nFunctional General Formula\nExample\ngroup\nAlkane\nCH2+2\nCH, methane\nC.H1\nDouble\nAlkene\nC=c&lt;\nC.H2\nот\nbond",
