        "title": "16.4 Oxidation of Organic Compounds",
        "theory": "CH,OH\nformaldehyde\nHCHO\nformic acid\nHCOOH\ncarbon dioxide\nCO2\nOxidation state\n-4\n-2\n0\n+2\n+4\nCombustion of alkanes is an important reaction, which is also a type of oxidation reaction. Alkanes (and also other hydrocarbons), burn in air to produce carbon dioxide\n114\nNOT FOR SALE\nand water. The reaction is highly exothermic and large quantity of heat is evolved. The heat released on complete combustion of a substance is called its heat of combustion. For example,\nignition\nCH1 + 202\nCO2+ 2H2O+ 890.95 kJ/mol\nThe reaction is of high significance, as it provides basis for their use as fuels to get heat and power. When there is an insufficient supply of oxygen, alkanes on combustion, form carbon monoxide and water or carbon soot and water.\nignition\n2CH, + 30,\n(limited)\n200 + 4H2O + heat\nignition\nCH, + O,\nC+ 2H2O+ heat\n(very limited)\nMethane on heating at high pressure, with a regulated supply of oxygen and in the presence of a suitable catalyst, provides a variety of products like methyl alcohol etc. For example, when a mixture of methane and air is passed over copper catalyst at a temperature 250°C and pressure of 100 atm, as a result, by controlled oxidation process, methane is converted to methanol. Later on this methyl alcohol by controlled. oxidation, can be further converted to formaldehyde.\n2CH1 + 02\n16.5 Alkenes\nCu/250°C/100atm\n→2CH,OH\nAlkenes are the unsaturated hydrocarbons which contain a carbon-carbon double bond. They can be represented by the general formula CH. They are also known as olefins, derived from Latin oleum meaning &quot;oil&quot; and ficare meaning &quot;to form, because their lower members form oily derivatives on treatment with chlorine or bromine.\n16.5.1\nNomenclature\nThere are two systems for naming alkenes i.e. Common and IUPAC system.\n(a)\nCommon System\nThe common names of fist four members are derived from those of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending &quot;ane&quot; with &quot;-ylene&quot;.\nCH2 = CH2 ethylene\nCH-CH=CH2 propylene\n115\nNOT FOR SALE\nV.\ni.\nii.\nGreek letters, a, B, y etc. are used to distinguish isomers having double bonds at\ndifferent positions.\n(b)\nCH, =CH-CH-CH,\na - butylene\nIUPAC System\nThe IUPAC rules for naming alkenes are;\nCH, -CH=CH-CH,\nB-butylene\nTidbit\nSelect the longest continuous carbon chain Now a, B.y are replaced by\nthat include the maximum number of double bonds.\nnumbers 1, 2, 3 in IUPAC.\nThe name given to the longest chain will act as parent name, whereby the suffix -ane of the corresponding alkane is replaced by suffix -ene.\nIf there are two double bonds, the suffix is -diene; for three, -triene; for four,\n-tetraene; and so on, are used.\nNumber the chain from the end that gives lower number to the doubly bonded carbons. Since double bond appears between two carbon atoms, therefore its position is indicated by the number of that carbon atom, bearing lower number and this number is written before suffix-ene, -diene etc.\nCarbon-carbon double bonds take precedence over alkyl groups in determining the main carbon chain and the direction in which it is numbered.\nvii. Name substituent groups as in alkanes, indicating their locations by the number\nof the main-chain carbon to which they are attached.\nFor compounds that show geometric isomerism, add the appropriate prefix: cis- or trans-.(see section 16.6)\nExamples\nCH1 =CH,\nethene\nawa\nCH, -CH=CH2\npropene\nCH-CH-CH-CH, CH-CH-CH-CH,\nbut-1-ene\nCH,\nbut-2-ene\nCH-CH-CH-CH-CH, CH, -CH-CH-CH-CH,",
