        "sciNote": "acetic acid\nOn industrial scale, ethylene oxide is prepared, by air oxidation of ethene with silver oxide catalyst at 300°C.\nCH2=CH2 + 102\"\nAg2O\n300°C\nCH, CH,\nethene\nethylene oxide\n16.5.7.7 Ozonolysis\n(oxirane)\nWhen ozone (O3) gas is passed through a solution of alkene in an inert solvent, at low emperature (-80°C), an addition product molozonide is formed. This molozonide earranges rapidly to form ozonide.\nCH2=CH2+0,\nethene\nrearrangment\nH,C\nCH2\nH2C-CH2\n0-0\nethylene molozonide\nethylene ozonide\n123\nNOT FOR SALE\nOzonides are highly unstable and explosive and cannot be isolated. Ozonides are decomposed using reducing agents like zinc dust, to form carbonyl compounds. For example, cleavage of ethylene ozonide with zinc, yield two molecules of formaldehyde.\nH.C\nH2O\nCH, + Zn\n+2\n100°C\nH\nCH\n+ ZnO\nformaldehyde\n0 0\nethylene ozonide\nThis conversion process of an alkene using ozone to carbonyl compounds is called ozonolysis. This reaction can be used for locating the position of the double bond in an\nunknown alkene.\n16.5.7.8 Polymerization\nPolymerization is a process in which small molecules (monomers) chemically join together to form large molecules (polymers). Simple alkenes when subjected to high temperature and pressure in the presence of a catalyst, form long chain addition polymers. Polymerization of alkenes is essentially the conversion of pi bonds to sigma bonds, which is a thermodynamically favorable process. For example ethene polymerizes to form polyethene in different ways. O",
