        "title": "16.8.5.2 Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution Reactions",
        "theory": "benzene\n(slow)\n+ E*\narenium ion\n146\nH\nE\nNOT FOR SALE\nhe intermediate arenium ion is resonance stabilized and is a hybrid of the following ree structures. This resonance-stabilized carbocation is called a sigma-complex ecause the electrophile is joined to the benzene ring by a new sigma-bond.\nH\nE\nH\nE\nH\nE\n#tep-3: The intermediate arenium ion is non-aromatic species and is unstable. It loses proton to form a stable aromatic substitution product.\narenium ion\nH\nE\nB catalyst\n(fast)\nE\n+H-B+ catalyst\nsubstitution product\nThe overall reaction is the substitution of a proton by an electrophile on the aromatic ing: electrophilic aromatic substitution.\n6.8.5.2.2 Nitration\nBenzene reacts with concentrated nitric acid in the presence of concentrated sulphuric cid at 60°C to form nitrobenzene.\nto\n+ HNO,\nH2SO,\n60°C\nbenzene\nNO2\n+ H2O\nnitrobenzene\nWhile nitration of toluene in above conditions yield a mixture of isomeric o,-m- and -nitrotoluene, in unequal proportions. The ortho and para products predominate, while\nvery little meta product is obtained due to ortho/para orienting effect of methyl group (see Section 16.8.5.2.7).\nCH,\nCH,\nCH,\nNO\nHNO,\n+\nH2SO\nNO\n-H2O\ntoluene\no-nitrotoluene (63%) m-nitrotoluene (3%)\nCH,\nNO2\np-nitrotoluene (34%)\nIn case of nitration, the electrophile is nitronium ion, produced by reaction of concentrated nitric and sulfuric acid. The electrophile nitronium ion attacks on benzene ring and forms substitution products via arenium ion intermediate (Section\n16.8.5.2.1).\n147\nNOT FOR SALE\nHNO, + 2H2SO\nNO, + 2HSO, +H,0*\nnitronium ion (Electrophile)\n16.8.5.2.3 Sulphonation\nBenzene reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid at 120°C or fuming sulfuric acid at room temperature, to give benzenesulphonic acid. Fuming sulphuric acid is concentrated sulphuric acid in which sulphur trioxide has been dissolved.\nbenzene\nbenzene\n120°C\n+ H2SO4\nSO,H\n+ H2O\nمیں\nbenzenesulphonic acid\nSO,H\n+ SO,\nfuming H2SO\n25°C\nbenzenesulphonic acid\nand\np-\nSulphonation of toluene also yield a mixture of isomeric o, m methylbenzenesuphonic acid, in different percentages. The ortho and para products again predominate, while meta product is obtained in small amount.\nCH1\nCH:\nSO2H\nfuming H2SO,",
        "content": "benzene<br>(slow)<br>+ E*<br>arenium ion<br>146<br>H<br>E<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>he intermediate arenium ion is resonance stabilized and is a hybrid of the following ree structures. This resonance-stabilized carbocation is called a sigma-complex ecause the electrophile is joined to the benzene ring by a new sigma-bond.<br>H<br>E<br>H<br>E<br>H<br>E<br>#tep-3: The intermediate arenium ion is non-aromatic species and is unstable. It loses proton to form a stable aromatic substitution product.<br>arenium ion<br>H<br>E<br>B catalyst<br>(fast)<br>E<br>+H-B+ catalyst<br>substitution product<br>The overall reaction is the substitution of a proton by an electrophile on the aromatic ing: electrophilic aromatic substitution.<br>6.8.5.2.2 Nitration<br>Benzene reacts with concentrated nitric acid in the presence of concentrated sulphuric cid at 60°C to form nitrobenzene.<br>to<br>+ HNO,<br>H2SO,<br>60°C<br>benzene<br>NO2<br>+ H2O<br>nitrobenzene<br>While nitration of toluene in above conditions yield a mixture of isomeric o,-m- and -nitrotoluene, in unequal proportions. The ortho and para products predominate, while<br>very little meta product is obtained due to ortho/para orienting effect of methyl group (see Section 16.8.5.2.7).<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>NO<br>HNO,<br>+<br>H2SO<br>NO<br>-H2O<br>toluene<br>o-nitrotoluene (63%) m-nitrotoluene (3%)<br>CH,<br>NO2<br>p-nitrotoluene (34%)<br>In case of nitration, the electrophile is nitronium ion, produced by reaction of concentrated nitric and sulfuric acid. The electrophile nitronium ion attacks on benzene ring and forms substitution products via arenium ion intermediate (Section<br>16.8.5.2.1).<br>147<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>HNO, + 2H2SO<br>NO, + 2HSO, +H,0*<br>nitronium ion (Electrophile)<br>16.8.5.2.3 Sulphonation<br>Benzene reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid at 120°C or fuming sulfuric acid at room temperature, to give benzenesulphonic acid. Fuming sulphuric acid is concentrated sulphuric acid in which sulphur trioxide has been dissolved.<br>benzene<br>benzene<br>120°C<br>+ H2SO4<br>SO,H<br>+ H2O<br>میں<br>benzenesulphonic acid<br>SO,H<br>+ SO,<br>fuming H2SO<br>25°C<br>benzenesulphonic acid<br>and<br>p-<br>Sulphonation of toluene also yield a mixture of isomeric o, m methylbenzenesuphonic acid, in different percentages. The ortho and para products again predominate, while meta product is obtained in small amount.<br>CH1<br>CH:<br>SO2H<br>fuming H2SO,",
        "rules": [],
        "sciNote": "benzene\n(slow)\n+ E*\narenium ion\n146\nH\nE\nNOT FOR SALE\nhe intermediate arenium ion is resonance stabilized and is a hybrid of the following ree structures. This resonance-stabilized carbocation is called a sigma-complex ecause the electrophile is joined to the benzene ring by a new sigma-bond.\nH\nE\nH\nE\nH\nE\n#tep-3: The intermediate arenium ion is non-aromatic species and is unstable. It loses proton to form a stable aromatic substitution product.\narenium ion\nH\nE\nB catalyst\n(fast)\nE\n+H-B+ catalyst\nsubstitution product\nThe overall reaction is the substitution of a proton by an electrophile on the aromatic ing: electrophilic aromatic substitution.\n6.8.5.2.2 Nitration\nBenzene reacts with concentrated nitric acid in the presence of concentrated sulphuric cid at 60°C to form nitrobenzene.\nto\n+ HNO,\nH2SO,\n60°C\nbenzene\nNO2\n+ H2O\nnitrobenzene\nWhile nitration of toluene in above conditions yield a mixture of isomeric o,-m- and -nitrotoluene, in unequal proportions. The ortho and para products predominate, while\nvery little meta product is obtained due to ortho/para orienting effect of methyl group (see Section 16.8.5.2.7).\nCH,\nCH,\nCH,\nNO\nHNO,\n+\nH2SO\nNO\n-H2O\ntoluene\no-nitrotoluene (63%) m-nitrotoluene (3%)\nCH,\nNO2\np-nitrotoluene (34%)\nIn case of nitration, the electrophile is nitronium ion, produced by reaction of concentrated nitric and sulfuric acid. The electrophile nitronium ion attacks on benzene ring and forms substitution products via arenium ion intermediate (Section\n16.8.5.2.1).\n14",
