        "sciNote": "Lactic acid (2-hydroxypropanoic acid) is an example of a compound which shows optical isomerism and has two optical isomers. The isomer which is dextrorotatory is called (+)-lactic acid, while the levorotatory isomers is called (-)-lactic acid (Section 16.6.5).\n16.6.3 Chiral Centres\nA chiral center is an atom that has four different atoms or groups bonded to it. A chiral atom may have one lone pair and three different atoms or groups attached. It is also called chirality center or stereogenic center. Interchange of two groups at stereogenic. center (or stereocenter) in a molecule gives another stereoisomer.\n16.6.4 Carbon based Chiral Center - Chiral Carbon\nA tetrahedral carbon atom in a molecule that bears four different substituents is referred to as a chiral carbon atom, stereogenic carbon or asymmetric carbon atom, and is often designated by an asterisk (+). For example, lactic acid has a chiral carbon which is bonded to four different groups (-H, OH, CH,,-COOH).\n16.6.5\nChirality\nOH\nCH, C-COOH\nH 2-hydroxypropanoic acid (Lactic acid)\nawaz e in\nA molecule is chiral if its two mirror-image forms are not superimposable in three dimensions. The mirror image of chiral molecules is actually another stereoisomer. The word \"chiral\" is derived from the Greek word cheir, meaning \"hand,\" and it is entirely appropriate to speak of the \"handedness\" of molecules. All chiral molecules are also dissymmetric (do not have any plane of symmetry). The opposite of chiral is achiral. A molecule "
