        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 23",
        "theory": "CH,\nCH1\ne in\ntoluene\no-methylbenzenesulphonic m-methylbenzenesulphonic\nacid (43%)\nacid (4%)\nSO,H\nW\nSO2H p-methylbenzenesulphonic acid (53%)\nThe electrophile is sulphur trioxide in this reaction. The electrophile sulphur trioxide attacks on benzene ring and forms the products via arenium ion intermediate.\n2H2SO\nSO, + HSO, + H2O sulphur trioxide\n16.8.5.2.4 Halogenation\n(Electrophile)\nBenzene reacts with molecular halogen at room temperature, in the presence of Lewis acid catalyst to form halobenzene. For example reaction of chlorine with benzene in the presence ferric chloride is given below.\n148\n+ Cl2\nFeCl,\n25°C\nbenzene\nNOT FOR SALE\nCI\n+ HCI\nchlorobenzene\nChlorination of toluene in presence of FeCl results in a mixture of isomeric o-, m- and p-chlorotoluenes, in different percentages. The ortho and para products predominate, while meta product is obtained in small amount.\nCH,\nCH,\nCl2\nFeCl\n&amp;\nCH,\n+\n-HCI\nCI\no-chlorotoluene (43%) m-chlorotolene (2%)\ntoluene\nCH,\np-chlorotoluene (55%)\nThe electrophile is chloronium ions formed by reaction with ferric chloride and chlorine molecules. The electrophile chloronium ion attacks on benzene ring and form products via arenium ion intermediate.\na\nTidbit\nCl2+ FeCl,\nCl + FeCl,\nchloronium ion (Electrophile)\nChlorine reacts with AICI to form a complex, which reacts as if it were CT.\nCI-CI:\n:CI:\nAl\nCI\nCH:\n:C1: lo CI-CI-AI-CI:\n#C]:\n:C1: le CPCI-AL-CI:\nCH\n16.8.5.2.5 Friedel-Crafts Alkylation\nBenzene reacts with alkyl halides in the presence of Lewis acid catalyst to form alkyl benzenes. For example, reaction of benzene with methyl chloride in the presence of aluminum chloride yields toluene.\nCH1\nbenzene\nAICI,\n+ CH1CI\nmethyl chloride\n+ HCI\ntoluene\nSubstituting methyl groups into toluene, via Friedel-Craft&#x27;s alkylation results in formation of three isomeric xylenes. Mainly the o- and p-xylene are formed.\n149\nCH,\nCH,\nCH,\nCH,Cl\n+\nFels -HCI\ntoluene\no-xylene (54%)\nCH1\nCH1\nCH,\nCH1\nm-xylene (17%)\np-xylene (29%)",
        "content": "CH,<br>CH1<br>e in<br>toluene<br>o-methylbenzenesulphonic m-methylbenzenesulphonic<br>acid (43%)<br>acid (4%)<br>SO,H<br>W<br>SO2H p-methylbenzenesulphonic acid (53%)<br>The electrophile is sulphur trioxide in this reaction. The electrophile sulphur trioxide attacks on benzene ring and forms the products via arenium ion intermediate.<br>2H2SO<br>SO, + HSO, + H2O sulphur trioxide<br>16.8.5.2.4 Halogenation<br>(Electrophile)<br>Benzene reacts with molecular halogen at room temperature, in the presence of Lewis acid catalyst to form halobenzene. For example reaction of chlorine with benzene in the presence ferric chloride is given below.<br>148<br>+ Cl2<br>FeCl,<br>25°C<br>benzene<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>CI<br>+ HCI<br>chlorobenzene<br>Chlorination of toluene in presence of FeCl results in a mixture of isomeric o-, m- and p-chlorotoluenes, in different percentages. The ortho and para products predominate, while meta product is obtained in small amount.<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>Cl2<br>FeCl<br>&amp;<br>CH,<br>+<br>-HCI<br>CI<br>o-chlorotoluene (43%) m-chlorotolene (2%)<br>toluene<br>CH,<br>p-chlorotoluene (55%)<br>The electrophile is chloronium ions formed by reaction with ferric chloride and chlorine molecules. The electrophile chloronium ion attacks on benzene ring and form products via arenium ion intermediate.<br>a<br>Tidbit<br>Cl2+ FeCl,<br>Cl + FeCl,<br>chloronium ion (Electrophile)<br>Chlorine reacts with AICI to form a complex, which reacts as if it were CT.<br>CI-CI:<br>:CI:<br>Al<br>CI<br>CH:<br>:C1: lo CI-CI-AI-CI:<br>#C]:<br>:C1: le CPCI-AL-CI:<br>CH<br>16.8.5.2.5 Friedel-Crafts Alkylation<br>Benzene reacts with alkyl halides in the presence of Lewis acid catalyst to form alkyl benzenes. For example, reaction of benzene with methyl chloride in the presence of aluminum chloride yields toluene.<br>CH1<br>benzene<br>AICI,<br>+ CH1CI<br>methyl chloride<br>+ HCI<br>toluene<br>Substituting methyl groups into toluene, via Friedel-Craft&#x27;s alkylation results in formation of three isomeric xylenes. Mainly the o- and p-xylene are formed.<br>149<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>CH,Cl<br>+<br>Fels -HCI<br>toluene<br>o-xylene (54%)<br>CH1<br>CH1<br>CH,<br>CH1<br>m-xylene (17%)<br>p-xylene (29%)",
        "rules": [],
        "sciNote": "CH,\nCH1\ne in\ntoluene\no-methylbenzenesulphonic m-methylbenzenesulphonic\nacid (43%)\nacid (4%)\nSO,H\nW\nSO2H p-methylbenzenesulphonic acid (53%)\nThe electrophile is sulphur trioxide in this reaction. The electrophile sulphur trioxide attacks on benzene ring and forms the products via arenium ion intermediate.\n2H2SO\nSO, + HSO, + H2O sulphur trioxide\n16.8.5.2.4 Halogenation\n(Electrophile)\nBenzene reacts with molecular halogen at room temperature, in the presence of Lewis acid catalyst to form halobenzene. For example reaction of chlorine with benzene in the presence ferric chloride is given below.\n148\n+ Cl2\nFeCl,\n25°C\nbenzene\nNOT FOR SALE\nCI\n+ HCI\nchlorobenzene\nChlorination of toluene in presence of FeCl results in a mixture of isomeric o-, m- and p-chlorotoluenes, in different percentages. The ortho and para products predominate, while meta product is obtained in small amount.\nCH,\nCH,\nCl2\nFeCl\n&\nCH,\n+\n-HCI\nCI\no-chlorotoluene (43%) m-chlorotolene (2%)\ntoluene\nCH,\np-chlorotoluene (55%)\nThe electrophile is chloronium ions formed by reaction with ferric chloride and chlorine molecules. The electrophile chloronium ion attacks on benzene ring and form products via arenium ion intermediate.\na\nTidbit\nCl2+ FeCl,\nCl + FeCl,\nchloronium ion (Electrophile)\nChlorine reacts with AICI to form a complex, which reacts as if it were CT.\nCI-CI:\n:CI:\nAl\nCI\nCH:\n:C1: lo CI-CI-AI-CI:\n#C]:\n:C1: le CPCI-AL-CI:\nCH\n16.8.5.2.5 Friedel-Crafts Alkylation\nBenzene reacts with alkyl halides in the presence of Lewis"
