      "16.5.6 Reactivity of Alkenes",
      "Alkenes are more reactive than alkanes due the presence of pi (n) bond in the alkene molecules. The overlap of atomic orbitals forming the pi bond is not as effective as that in sigma (5) bonds. Thus, pi bond is weaker than a sigma bond and more easily broken. The electron density of pi bond lies above and below the bond axis. It is therefore, more exposed and easily accessible to an external electrophilic (electron seeking) reagent, commonly known as electrophile. It is, therefore, favorable for alkenes to add an electrophile across the double bond by breaking the weak pi bond to form strong sigma bonds and to produce a saturated compound. Such reactions in which unsaturated compounds are converted into saturated compounds, are called addition reactions. When addition reaction of alkenes is initiated by electrophile, this addition is specifically called electrophilic addition.",
      "16.5.7",
      "Reactions of Alkenes",
      "vaz",
      "16.5.7.1 Hydrogenation (Addition of Hydrogen)",
      "Alkenes readily add hydrogen to form alkanes in the presence of finely divided catalyst such as nickel, platinum or palladium at a temperature of 200-250°C and under moderate pressure (1-5 atm). This is called catalytic hydrogenation and reaction occurs on the surface of catalyst. The reaction is exothermic and the heat released during hydrogenation of an alkene is called heat of hydrogenation.",
