      "The bromination of alkenes provides a useful test for the presence of a double bond in the molecules. When dilute solution of bromine is added to a compound containing carbon-carbon double bond, the colour of bromine rapidly discharges as the colorless dibromo compound is formed. This is called bromine water test.",
      "16.5.7.5 Halohydration When alkene is reacted with halogen in an inert solvent, vic-dihalides are formed, because halide ion is the only nucleophile available for reaction with the intermediate cyclic halonium ion. However if the reaction is performed in aqueous solution, water can also act as nucleophile and attack on cyclic halonium ion to form halo-alcohols, commonly known as halohydrin. For example; ethene when reacted with aqueous bromine, forms 2-bromoethanol, commonly called ethylene bromohydrin.",
