      "16.6.1 Constitutional isomerism",
      "In this type of isomerism, the isomers have the same molecular formula but different. connectivity in structure. It is also called structural isomerism. Structural isomerism is",
      "of five types.",
      "16.6.1.1 Chain Isomerism or Skeletal Isomerism",
      "Chain isomerism occurs when there is a difference in arrangement of the carbon atoms in main chain of a molecule. If two or more compounds having the same molecular formula with different carbon chains, then they are said to exhibit the property of Chain isomerism. This phenomenon is also called as skeletal isomerism. The number of possible chain isomers increases with number of carbon atoms. For example; butane has two isomers, pentane has three, hexane has five isomers and so on. The two chain isomers of butane (CH) are given below.",
      "CH-CH-CH-CH,",
      "n-butane",
      "16.6.1.2 Position Isomerism",
      "CH1-CH-CH,",
      "CH, isobutane",
      "Position isomerism arises when there is a difference in the position of functional group. When the position of the functional groups with respect to main chain changes, the phenomenon is called as position isomerism. For example; position isomers of propyl alcohols and butylenes are given below.",
      "125",
