      "alcohol",
      "H-C=C−H + 2ZnBr2",
      "acetylene",
      "Alkynes are unsaturated hydrocarbons like alkenes. Thus they resemble alkenes in most of their reactions. For example, alkyne add hydrogen, halogens and halogen acids. much like the addition of these reagents to alkenes. However, unlike alkenes which add one molecules of these reagents, alkynes add two molecules in a step-wise manner. Characteristic reaction of alkyne are also electrophilic addition reactions.",
      "It is to be noted that a carbon-carbon triple bond of alkynes, in general, is less reactive than a carbon-carbon double bond of alkenes, towards electrophilic reagents. This is due to the following reasons.",
      "i. The triple bonded carbon atoms in alkynes are sp-hybridized while double bonded c-atoms in alkenes are sp2-hybridized. Greater the s character of an orbital, the more highly the electrons in that orbital are held by the nucleus. Thus, pi electrons in alkynes (50% s-character) are more strongly held by the carbon atom than in case of alkenes (33.33% s-character) and are less easily available for reactions with electrophile. This makes alkynes, less reactive than alkenes in electrophilic",
      "reactions.",
      "ii. In alkynes, there are two pi bonds, the four lobes of which merge to form a single electron cloud. This cloud is cylindrically symmetrical about the inter-nuclear axis and occupies large volume (Figure 16.10). Thus, the pi electron-density per u",
      "134",
      "unit",
      "NOT FOR SALE volume becomes low. Due to decrease in the electron density, pi electrons are not easily available to an electrophile and hence less reactive towards electrophilic addition reactions.",
      "H-",
