      "16.8.2 Physical Properties of Benzene",
      "phenanthrene",
      "Benzene is a colourless liquid at room temperature and atmospheric pressure. It has peculiar smell and burning taste. The density of benzene is 0.88 g/mL. Benzene melts at 6°C and boils at 80°C. Benzene is more symmetrical than similar aliphatic compounds, so it packs better into crystals and has high melting point than corresponding aliphatic hydrocarbons. For example, benzene melts at 6°C, while hexane melts at -95°C. It is highly inflammable. Benzene is insoluble in water but soluble in ether, carbon tetrachloride etc.",
      "16.8.3",
      "Structure: Molecular Orbital Aspects",
      "The structure of benzene can best be described in terms of molecular orbital hybridization model. Each carbon atom of the ring is linked to others by sp2-hybrid orbitals and with s orbitals of the six hydrogen atoms, forming six C-C and six C-H sigma bonds respectively (Figure 16.11). Since, the sigma bond results from the overlap of planar sp2-hybrid orbitals, all carbon and hydrogen atoms in benzene lie in the same plane and all the bond angles are 120o.",
      "C",
      "C",
      "H",
      "H",
      "H",
      "H",
      "C",
      "H",
      "e waz e",
      "H",
      "Figure 16.11 Orbital hybriziation model of benzene and formation of sigma bonds. Each carbon atom also possesses a half-filled unhybridized p orbital, which are perpendicular to the plane of sigma bonds. The side-wise overlap of these p orbitals produces delocalized pi molecular orbitals containing six electrons, which are associated with all six carbon atoms. It is this extensive pi electron delocalization which is responsible",
      "X",
      "142",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "for the greater stability of benzene. The electron cloud of these delocalized pi electron lies half below and half above the ring (Figure 16.12). The carbon-carbon and carbon- hydrogen bond lengths are 139 pm (1.39 Å) and 108 pm (1.08 Å) respectively.",
      "H.",
      "#",
      "H",
      "H",
      "H",
      "H",
      "Η \"H H-",
