      "1.397%",
      "120°",
      "||",
      "Figure 16.12 Unhybridized p-p orbitals overlap to form delocalized pi bonds. 16.8.4 Resonance, Resonance Energy and Stabilization",
      "Resonance",
      "120°",
      "Some molecules (or ions) are not adequately represented by a single Lewis structure. Two or more valence-bond structures are drawn for such molecules, differing only in the placement of Pi electrons. These different structures are called resonance structures or resonance forms or canonical form because they are not different molecules, just different ways of drawing the same molecule. The actual molecule is said to be a resonance hybrid of its resonating forms.",
      "b",
      "cyclobe-1,3,5-triene",
      "For example, the structure (which is cyclohexa-1,3,5- triene) is used to represent benzene. It is not the real structure of benzene because if this is the real structure of benzene, it is expected that benzene should add three molecules of Br2 (in absence of light), but in actual practice it does not do so. Benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution reaction rather than addition. The bond length of bonds “a” and “b” should be 154 pm and 134 pm respectively. However, all carbon-",
      "\"b\" carbon bonds in benzene are equal and having bond lengths of 139 pm.",
      "Thus, the above structure is not the actual structure of benzene. In such case we write more than one structure to represent the actual structure of a molecule i.e. benzene. This representation of a real structure as a weighted average of two or more contributing structures is called resonance. The following resonance structures, commonly called Kekule structures, are used to represent benzene.",
      "kekule structures",
      "143",
      "resonance hybrid",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "It should be noted that the concept of resonance does not mean that these structures rapidly shifts between canonical forms. In fact, all the molecules of a substance have the same structure all the time, in which all the electrons are delocalized over the whole system involved in resonance.",
      "Resonance Energy and Stability of Benzene",
      "The resonance energy of a molecules is the difference between the energy of the most stable canonical form and that of the actual molecule. For benzene it can be calculated by measuring its heat of hydrogenation and comparing it with that of the most stable",
      "canonical from.",
      "Hydrogenation of cyclohexene (having one double bond) evolves 120 kJ/mol heat energy to form cyclohexane.",
      "cyclohexene",
      "Pt",
      "+ H2",
      "120 kJ/mol",
      "cyclohexane",
      "Hydrogenation of cyclohexa-1,3-diene (having two double bonds) evolves 232 kJ/mol to form cyclohexane, which is almost double of the value 120 kJ/mol for cyclohexene.",
      "+2H2",
      "cyclohexa-1,3-diene",
      "+ 232 kJ/mol",
      "cyclohexane",
      "The molecule cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene (Kekule structure) normally written for benzene and having three double bonds, should release energy three time to that of cyclohexane (i.e. 120×3 = 360 kJ/mol), upon hydrogenation.",
      "Pi",
      "3H2",
      "Z",
      "360 kJ/mol",
      "cyclohexane",
      "cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene",
      "But hydrogenation of benzene actually evolves only 208 kJ/mol.",
      "PL",
      "3H2",
      "benzene",
      "+ 208 kJ/mol",
      "cyclohexane",
      "Thus, the measured (experimental) heat of hydrogenation of benzene is less than the expected value of the most stable canonical form by an amount of 152 kJ/mol. Benzene is, therefore, is said to be resonance stabilized by an amount of 152 kJ/mol which is known as its resonance energy.",
      "144",
      "16.8.5 Reactivity and Reactions",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Due to unusual stability (resonance stabilization), benzene and its derivatives do not give addition reactions like those of alkenes. In alkenes the pi electronic cloud is ocalized between the two carbon atoms and thus they readily undergo addition eactions. In benzene the pi electronic cloud makes a continuous sheet above and below all the six nuclei and the pi electrons are, thus delocalized, which give benzene tability. Benzene, therefore, prefers to undergo electrophilic substitution reactions ather than addition reactions, to retain its aromatic character. 16.8.5.1 Addition Reactions",
      "mpre",
      "Benzene is very resistant to addition reaction and requires much more vigorous onditions than generally required for alkenes and alkynes. It does not undergo lectrophilic addition reaction, but may give free-radical type addition and catalytic ddition reactions in proper conditions.",
      "16.8.5.1.1 Addition of Hydrogen",
      "Benzene reacts with hydrogen in the presence of Ni, Pt or Pd catalyst at elevated emperature and high pressure to form cyclohexane. The reaction is also called catalytic ydrogenation.",
