      "ortho (40%)",
      "meta (40%)",
      "para (20%)",
      "However, in actual practice the disubstituted products are not formed with the above. expected percentages. This is because the first substituent (-E1) effect the ring making the ortho, meta and para position non-equivalent for electrophilic attack. Similarly the first substituent may also increase or decrease the reactivity of ring for second. substitution. These effects are called substitution effects.",
      "Types of effects of Substituents",
      "Effect of a substituent already present on the benzene ring on further substitution can be divided into two categories i.e. Orientation Effect and Reactivity Effect.",
      "(A)",
      "Directing or Orientation Effect",
      "The first substitutent may direct the incoming electrophile to ortho, meta or para position, depending on the nature of the first substituent. This is called the directing or orientation effect. It has been experimentally observed that some groups, when attached to the benzene ring, direct the second incoming substituent to either ortho para positions or to the meta position. Thus, the substituents (groups) can be divided into two classes.",
      "and",
      "151",
      "L",
      "Ortho Para Directing substituents",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Substituents which direct the second substituent to the ortho and para positions simultaneously are called the ortho/para (o/p) directing substituents. For example, when phenol is nitrated, with dilute nitric acid, the reaction yields mainly the o-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol. Thus the -OH group of phenol, which has directed the second substituent -NO2 to ortho and para positions is designated as ortho/para directing. Some common ortho/para directing groups are, -OH, -NH2, -NHR, −NR2, -R, -X (halogens) etc. (Table 16.6).",
      "OH",
      "phenol",
      "dil. HNO,",
      "25°C -H2O",
      "OH",
      "&",
      "OH",
      "NO2",
      "NO2",
      "o-nitrophenol (55%)",
      "m-nitrophenol (1%)",
      "OH",
      "NO: p-nitrophenol (44%)",
      "The ortho/para directing effect can be explained in terms of charge distribution and",
      "relative stability of arenium ion.",
      "a. Charge Distribution",
      "Consider, the various resonance forms of phenol.",
      "OH",
      "OH",
      "COH",
      ":ÖH",
      "8-8-8-8-8",
      "in",
      "As can be seen that one of the non-bonding electron pairs of oxygen of the -OH group is delocalized over the ring, making the ortho and para positions more electron rich than the meta position. The electrophile would naturally prefer to attack at these electron rich centers, to give mainly ortho and para disubstituted products.",
      "b. Relative Stability of Arenium ion",
      "The intermediate arenium ion in the presence of ortho/para directing groups is more stable when the second substituent is at ortho/para positions than when it is at meta position as shown below.",
      "OH",
      "H",
      "O",
      "E",
      "AND",
      "OH",
      "OH",
      "H",
      "E",
      "Ε ́ Η",
      "more stable arenium ions",
      "less stable arenium ion",
      "152",
      "ii. Meta Directing substituents",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "The substituents which direct the second incoming substituent to meta position, are called meta-directing substituents. For example, nitration of nitrobenzene in the presence of concentrated nitric acid and sulfuric acid at 100°C gives mainly -dinitrobenzene. Thus the substituent -NO2 group, which has directed the second -NO2 group to the meta position is meta directing. Some common meta directing groups are -NO2, -CN, -CHO,-COR, -COOH etc. (Table 16.5).",
      "NO:",
      "NO2",
      "conc. HNO,",
      "conc. H2SO, 100°C -H2O",
      "NO2",
      "nitrobenzene",
      "NO2",
      "NO",
      "NO2",
      "o-dinitrobenzene (6%) m-dinitrobenzene (93%)",
      "NO: p-dinitrobenzene (1%)",
      "The meta-directing effect of a group may also be due to the following two reasons i.e. charge distribution and relative stability by arenium ion.",
      "a. Charge Distribution",
      "These groups withdraw electron density from ortho and para positions. Thus, meta position of the ring has relatively more electronic density than ortho and para positions, and becomes an attractive target for electrophile. It is clear from the resonance structures of nitrobenzene that ortho and para positions have small electron density compared to meta position.",
      "8-8-8-8-8",
      "b. Relative Stability of Arenium ion",
      "Secondly, the arenium ion formed in the presence of meta orienting groups is more stable when the second substituent is at meta position than when it is at ortho or para positions, as shown below.",
      "¥",
      "NO2",
