        "sciNote": "CH,\ntert-butyl\ncation (tertiary]\nFigure 17.3 Realtive stability order of different types of carbocations.\nMechanisms of Nucleophilic Substitution reactions\nAlkyl halides can undergo nucleophilic substitution by two different types of mechanisms. The type of mechanism depends upon the nature of alkyl halides.\n17.1.6.3 S1 Mechanism\nsubstitution\ne\nS\nN\nunimolecular\nnucleophilic\nSN1 stands for Substitution, Nucleophilic, unimolecular. It is a two-step mechanism. First step is slow (rate determining or rate limiting step) and involves the formation of intermediate carbocation by dissociation of alkyl halide or substrate. The term unimolecular means there is only one molecule involved transition state of the rate-determing step. The second step is fast and involves the attack of nucleophile on planar carbocation and formation of product. SN1 mechanism is generally followed by tertiary alkyl halides. As it involves the formation of carbocation, it is favored by highly ionizing polar solvents (having O-H, N-H bond). For example the hydrolysis of tert-butyl bromide follow Sal mechanism as shown below.\n170\nNOT FOR SALE\nStep-1: The substrate undergoes ionization and yield a carbocation. The bromine atom completely detaches from the substrate as bromide ion.\nH,C\nCH,\nBr\nslow step\nH,C\nH,C\nH,C\n+ Br CH,\ntert-butyl bromide\ncarbocation\n(trigonal planar)\nStep-2: In second step, the entering nucleophile attacks the planar carbocation on either face, to produce the end product.\nH,C\nH1C\nCH,\nfast CH",
