        "sciNote": "biomolecular\nboth the base and the alkyl halide participating the transition state, so this mechanism is abbreviated E2 for Elimination, bimolecular. These are generally followed by primary alkyl halides. These reactions are completed in one step like SN2 reactions.\nFor example, reaction of ethyl bromide with hydroxide ion to form ethene follow E2 mechanism. The removal of proton from the B-carbon atom by hydroxide base and the removal of bromide ion from the a-carbon atom takes place simultaneously.\nH Iẞ\nBr\nHO--H\nBr\nal\nHỌ + CH,\nCH.\nCH2\nCH2\nCH, CH2+H2O+ Br\nbase\nethyl bromide\ntransition state\nethene\nReaction is first order with respect to each reactant, and second order overall.\nrate = k[substrate] [base]\nor rate = k [CH,CH,Br][OH]\n174\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable 17.3 Summary of Elimination Reactions in Alkyl Halides\nReaction\nE1\nPromoting factors\nE2\nStrong Base\nBase\nWeak Base\nSubstrate\n30 > 20\n1° 2°\nSolvent\nPolar protic\nPolar aprotic\nCharacteristics\nSecond Order\nKinetics\n17.1.8\nFirst Order\nSubstitution versus Elimination\nWe have studied that an alkyl halide and a Lewis base can react together in either a substitution or an elimination reaction (Section 17.1.5). Substitution and elimination reactions are very often in competition with each other. Two important factors i.e. nature of alkyl halide and nature of Lewis base, play a key role in deciding whether a principal reaction would be elimination or substitution.\nCrowding around the a-carbon in molecule of substrate generally favours. ",
