        "sciNote": "167\n*\nsubstitution reactions.\nNOT FOR SALE\nelimination and Substitutions and\nThe halogen atom is a good leaving group and detaches from carbon atom, with its bonding electron pair to form a stable halide ion. When a nucleophile attacks on a-carbon and replaces the halide ion, the reaction is a nucleophilic substitution. When the halide ion leaves along with another atom (often H) from the adjacent carbon atom,\nTidbit it forms a new pi bond, the reaction is an elimination. In elimination reactions, a moleculeBesides alkyl halides, many other of HX is lost from the alkyl halide to give an types of compounds undergo alkene. These elimination reactions are called dehydrohalogenation (Section 16.5.5.2), because a hydrogen halide has been removed from the alkyl halide. Substitution and elimination reactions often compete with each other (Section 17.1.8). The strength of carbon-halogen bond decreases down the group from fluorine to iodine. This can be explained on the basis of their bond energies that are given in the Table 17.1. The table shows that iodo compounds are most reactive while fluoro compounds are least reactive. Therefore, the reactivity of alkyl halides increases from fluorine to iodine and the reactivity order is; R-1>R-Br>R-CI>R-F. Table 17.1 Bond Energies of Carbon-Halogen Bond\neliminations are introduced in this unit using the alkyl halides as examples. In unit 18, we encounter substitutions and eliminations in\nalcohols.\nBond\nC-F\nC-CI\nC-Br\nC-I\nBond Energy (kJ/mol)\n"
