        "theory": "17.3.1\nNomenclature\nAmines are named by common and IUPAC systems.\na. Common system\nR\nTertiary\n(2)\nR\ne in\nCommon names of amines are formed from the names of the alkyl groups bonded to nitrogen, followed by the suffix -amine.\nrwo\nThe prefixes di- and tri- are used to describe two and three identical substituents, attached to nitrogen.\nIf different alkyl groups are attached to the nitrogen, then they are named\nseparately in alphabetical order.\nCH, -CH, -CH,−NH,\nn-propyl amine\nCH,\nCH,-N-CH,\ntrimethyl amine\nCH -CH,−NH–CH,\nethyl methyl amine\nCH,\nCH,-N-C,H,\nethyl dimethyl amine\n180\nb. IUPAC system\nIn IUPAC nomenclature of amines, the longest\nTidbit\nNOT FOR SALE\ncontinuous chain of carbon atoms determines Amines also possess metamerism. the root name. The -e ending in the alkane name For example, ethyl methyl amine and is changed to suffix -amine and a number trimethyl amine are metamers of each shows the position of the amino\ngroup along\nother.\nthe chain. Other substituents on the carbon chain are given numbers, and the prefix. N- is used for each substituent on nitrogen.\nCH,-CH,-CH, NH,\npropan-1-amine\nCH1\nCH1-CH2-N-CH,\nN,N-dimethylethanamine\n17.3.2 Physical Properties\nCH-CH, NH–CH,\nN-methylethanamine\nNH, CH,\nCH,-CH-CH-CH,\n3-methylbutan-2-amine\nLow molecular weight amines are generally gases or low boiling liquids at room temperature. They possess a characteristic ammonia like smell.\nAmines have higher boiling points than alkanes of similar molecular weight due to the\npresence of intermolecular hydrogen bondings. The molecules of primary and secondary amines have high boiling points because they are able to form hydrogen bondings among their molecules.\nR\nH1\nR\nH.\nN\nR\nR\n1° or 2° amine:\nhydrogen bond donor and acceptor\nMolecules of tertiary amines cannot form hydrogen bonding among themselves, thus having lower boiling points than primary and secondary amines of the same molecular weight.\nHowever, all lower primary, secondary and tertiary amines can form hydrogen. bonding with water and therefore lower amines are soluble in water.\n181\nH\nH\nR\nR\nH\nNOT FOR SALE\nH.\nH.\nH\nR\nR\n1° or 2° amine:\nhydrogen bond donor and acceptor\nR\nH.\nR\nR\nH\n3o amine:\n17.3.3\nhydrogen bond acceptor only",
