        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 10",
        "theory": "Structure of Amines\nAmines are derivatives of ammonia molecule where one or more hydrogen atoms have been replaced by alkyl or aryl groups. Since, ammonia molecule has trigonal pyramidal shape, the resulting amines also have the same geometry. Nitrogen atom in amine is sp&#x27;-hybridized with a lone-pair in one of its sp-hybrid orbital. The bond angles in amines are close to 107.5°.\nNH\nNi\nCH1\nH\nH,C\nH 107.5°\nammonia\nCH, 108° trimethylamine\naz e in\n17.3.4 Basicity of Amines\nDue to the presence of a lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atoms, amines are capable of accepting a proton and are basic in nature. Amines react with acids to form ammonium salts.\nR-NH2+H-X\n1°-amine\nR-NH, X 1o-ammonium salt\nAmines are weak bases and, therefore when dissolved in water, it abstracts a proton from water to form ammonium and hydroxide ion, and the following equilibrium is\nestablished.\nR-NH2) H2Om\n1°-amine\nR-NH\n+ OH (4)\n1°-ammonium ion\n182\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nAliphatic amines are stronger bases than ammonia. This is due to the electron donating\nalkyl group\nwhich increases the electronic density of nitrogen, thereby increasing its\npower of accepting a proton from an acid, and also stabilizes the conjugate acid,\nammonium ion.\nH\nH— N:\n+ H2O\nH\nammonia\nH\nH,C-N\n+ H2O\nH\nmethyl amine\nH\nH-NH + OH PK-4.74\nH\nH\n(weaker base)\nH,CHOH PK-3.36\n(stronger base)\nH stabilized by the alkyl group\nThe greater the number of alkyl groups, the greater will be the electronic density on nitrogen and therefore, greater will be the power of nitrogen to accept proton. We might expect tertiary aliphatic amines would be the strongest and primary aliphatic amines would be weakest. However in aqueous solutions, secondary is more basic than primary and tertiary aliphatic amines. Because ammonium ions (conjugate acid) are charged, they are strongly solvated by water and the energy of solvation contributes to their stability. The additional alkyl groups around the ammonium ions of tertiary amines. decrease the number of water molecules that can approach closely and solvate the ions. However, ammonium ions of primary and secondary amines are more stabilized by water through solvation. But the opposing trends of inductive stabilization and steric hindrance of solvation are balanced for secondary alkyl amines, which make them stronger bases than primary or tertiary alkyl amines in aqueous solutions. The pKь values of some amines in water are given in Table 17.4.\nTable 17.4 pKь Values of some Amines in Water Solvent\n1o-amines\nРКь\n2o-amines\npKь\n3o-amines\nРКь\nmethyl amine\n3.36 dimethyl amine\n3.28\ntrimethyl amine\n4.25\nethyl amine 3.36\ndiethyl amine\n3.01\ntriethyl amine\n3.24\nn-propyl amine\ndi-n-propyl\ntri-n-propyl\n3.32\n3.00\n3.35\namine\namine\n183\n*\n17.3.5 Preparation of Amines\nNOT FOR SALE\nAmines can be prepared by a number of different methods. Some of these are given below.",
