        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 13",
        "theory": "N-substituted imine (a Schiff&#x27;s base)\nN-CH,\n+ H2O\nCH, NH\nmethanamine\n(methyl amine)\nH\nCH1\nCH1\npropanone\n(acetone)\n17.3.7.4 Preparation of Amides.\nH,C\nˇCH1\nN-methylpropan-2-imine (a Schiff&#x27;s base)\nPrimary amine react with acid chloride or acid anhydride to produce a secondary amide (or N-substituted amide).\nR-NH2\n1o-amine\non\nR\nacid halide\nR\n+ HCL\nNHR\nN-substituted amide\nSecondary amines react with acid halides or anhydrides to produce a tertiary amide (or N,N-disubstituted amide).\nR\nR-NH +\nR\n2o -amine\nacid halide\n+ HCl\nR\nNR:\nN,N-disubstituted amide\nAs tertiary amines have no hydrogen atom directly attached to the nitrogen, therefore, they donot react with acid chlorides or anhydrides to produce amides.\n17.3.7.5 Preparation of Diazonium Salts\nDiazonium salts are compounds that contain diazonium ion (R/Ar-N2&quot;) as cation. When primary amines are treated with nitrous acid they yield highly unstable salts known as diazonium salts. This reaction is termed as diazotization reaction. Nitrous acid being an unstable acid is prepared in situ by the reaction of sodium nitrite and\ndilute hydrochloric acid.\nR-NH2+ NaNO, + 2HCI-\n1o-amine\n2\nCH,— NH + NaNO2 + 2HCl\nmethyl amine\n2\nRN,CI+ 2H2O + NaCI\ndiazonium salt\nCH, N,CI+ 2H2O + NaCI\nmethanediazonium\nchloride\n*\n187\nNOT FOR SALE\nSociety, Technology And Science\nChlorophyll is the substance which makes plants green. The chlorophyll molecule has the unique capacity to convert the energy of the sun into chemical energy (through photosynthesis), which the plant uses to make carbohydrates from carbon dioxide and water. Hemoglobin is the substance in human blood which carries oxygen from the lungs to the other tissues and cells of the body.\nHemoglobin and Chlorophyll have similar structures composed of four elements; carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen. The main difference is that hemoglobin is built around iron (Fe), whereas chlorophyll is built around magnesium (Mg). The primary function for hemoglobin is to transport oxygen from the lungs to other parts of the body.\nChlorophyll\nHemoglobin\nCH2\nCH3\nCH2\nCH3\nH1C.\nH2C\n-N\nN\nCH3\n-N\nMg\nFe\nN\n-N\nN\nH3C\nCH2\nH&amp;C\n-CH3\nOCH,\nwa\nOH",
