      "17.1.4.2 Reactions of Alcohols with other Halogenating Agents (SOCh, PX3) Conversion of alcohol to alkyl halides with halogen acid, has some limitations. In some cases, a side product is also formed due to rearrangement of intermediate carbocation. Secondly, alkenes are also formed as side product due to possible dehydration of alcohols (Section 16.5.5.1). The formation of these side products can be avoided by the use of phosphorus trihalides or thionyl chloride to replace the hydroxyl group with halogens.",
