      "R-Nu + X",
      "product",
      "halide ion (nucleofuge)",
      "Any species neutral or negatively charged with an unshared pair of electrons can act both as a base and a nucleophile. Nucleophile is a species which donates electron pair. to an electrophilic center in a molecule and undergoes nucleophilic substitution or addition reactions. A nucleophile is generally represented by Nu. The strength of nucleophile is called nucleophilicity and is a measure of how fast a nucleophile. displaces a leaving group from a suitable substrate.",
      "But a base is a species which has affinity for proton, and donate electron pair to a proton in a reaction, and generally represented by B. Base give elimination reaction in alkyl halides. The strength of base is called basicity and represented in terms of equilibrium constant for abstraction of a proton.",
      "17.1.6.2.3 Carbocations and their Stability",
      "A carbocation is an ion in which a carbon atom has a positive charge and three. bonds. Carbocation is formed when an electrophile adds to carbon-carbon double bond or when halogen atom detaches from the carbon atom of an alkyl halide as halide ion. The positive carbon of alkyl carbocation is sp'-hybridized and has trigonal planar structure, with empty unhybridized p orbital perpendicular to the plane, as shown in (Figure 17.2).",
