      "17.1.8",
      "First Order",
      "Substitution versus Elimination",
      "We have studied that an alkyl halide and a Lewis base can react together in either a substitution or an elimination reaction (Section 17.1.5). Substitution and elimination reactions are very often in competition with each other. Two important factors i.e. nature of alkyl halide and nature of Lewis base, play a key role in deciding whether a principal reaction would be elimination or substitution.",
      "Crowding around the a-carbon in molecule of substrate generally favours. elimination over substitution reaction. This is due to the greater steric hindrance. when the nucleophile approaches towards a-carbon atom of the substrate. Thus, greater number of alkyl groups on the substrate favours elimination over substitution because these alkyl groups stabilize the alkene more than the substitution product.",
      "As all the bases are also nucleophiles, the competition between substitution and elimination reactions is determined by the fact whether it is a better base or a better nucleophile. A stronger base will favour elimination while a stronger nucleophile will favour substitution in the same substrate. For example, 2-bromopropane on reaction with ethoxide ion (C2H,O) undergoes elimination and produces alkene as the major product because ethoxide is a strong base (by products are not shown).",
