        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 1",
        "theory": "CH-CH-CH-CH,\nsec-butyl alcohol\nCH-CH-OH\nCH,\ntert-butyl alcohol\nCH-CH-CH-OH\nCH1\nisobutyl alcohol\nSuffix glycols is used for dihydric alcohols (more commonly for vicinal diols).\nOH OH\nCH2-CH2\nethylene glycol\nOH\nOH\nCH2-CH-CH,\npropylene glycol\nThe smallest trihydric alcohol is named as glycerol.\nb. IUPAC System\nОн он он\nOH\nOH\nCH2- CH2- CH2\ntrimethylene glycol\nawe in\nCH2-CH-CH2\nglycerol\nIn this system alcohols are named as &quot;alkanols&quot;. That is, the final e&#x27; of the corresponding alkane is replaced by suffix -ol&quot;.\nThe IUPAC rules are\n2.\n1. Select the longest continuous carbon chain containing the -OH group(s). The ending&quot;-e&quot; of the cur. sponding alkane is replaced by suffix &quot;-ol&quot;. Number the chain from one end or the other, so as to give lowest number to carbon carrying the -OH group.\n3.\nthe\n196\nNOT FOR SALE\n4. Mention the position of the hydroxyl group by putting its number before the\nsuffix &quot;-ol&quot;.\n5. Indicate the position of the other substituents (if any) using the numbers of the\n7.\ncarbon atoms, to which they are attached.\n6. The substituents, along with their positions, are named in alphabetical order\nbefore the parent name.\nIf the number of carbon atoms carrying the hydroxyl group is the same from either side, then number the chain from the end which is heavily substituted. In case of dihydric, trihydric and polyhydric alcohols, the prefix di, tri, tetra etc. is used before “-ol”, along with the numbers of the carbon atoms to which these hydroxyl groups are attached.\n9. If more than one hydroxyl groups are attached to the same carbon atom, the\nnumber of that carbon atom is repeated.\nThe following examples will illustrate the above rules.\nOH\nCH,\nmethanol\nilab\nCH-CH-CH-OH\npropan-1-ol\n&#x27;CH-CH-CH2-CH,\nOH\nbutan-2-ol\n&#x27;CH2-OH\n1CH2-OH\nethane-1,2-diol\n18.1.2 Physical Properties\n⚫ Lower alcohols are colourless liquids.\nCH, CH-OH\nethanol\nOH\n&#x27;CH-CH-CH,\npropan-2-ol\nCH, OH CH1\nகூ-\n&#x27;CH,-C-CH-C-CH,\nCH,\nH\n2,2,4-trimethylpentan-3-ol\n&#x27;CH-CH-CH2\nOH OH OH\npropane-1,2,3-triol\nTidbit\nThe -e ending of an alkane name\nwhile alcohols of high molecular mass dropped before a suffix beginning with\nare solids, at room temperature.\n⚫ They have characteristic sweet smell.\nThe melting and boiling points are\na vowel (-ol) and retained before one beginning with a consonant (-diol).",
