        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 2",
        "theory": "generally increase with the size of alkyl group, and also number of -OH g\ngroups\n197\nNOT FOR SALE\n(Table 18.1). Among isomeric alcohols, the boiling points of straight chain alcohols are higher than that of branched.\nThe boiling points of alcohols are much higher than those of corresponding alkanes and alkyl halides. The high boiling points of is due to hydrogen bonding among the alcohol molecules.\nH\n0:\nR\n5+\nH-0:\nR\nLower alcohols like methyl, ethyl and propyl alcohols are completely miscible with water. However the solubility of higher alcohols in water falls rapidly, due to increase in size of non-polar hydrophobic alkyl group.\nH\n0\nH\nH\nCH,-CH2-CH2-CH,\nhydrophilic region\nH.\nhydrophobic region\nName\nmethanol\nethanol\nn-propyl alcohoi\nn-butyl alcohol\n- 90\nH\nH\nTable 18.1 Boiling and Melting Points of Some Alcohols\nMelting Point (°C)\nBoiling Points (°C)\n-97\n-115\n-126\nAo in\nW\n118\nisopropyl alcohol\n-89\n82\nisobutyl alcohol\n-108\n108\nsec-butyl alcohol\n−114\n99\ntert-butyl alcohol\n-25.5\n82\nethylene glycol\n-12\n197\nglycerol\n18\n290\n198\n18.1.3 Structure of Alcohols\nNOT FOR SALE\nAlcohols are alkyl derivatives of water molecules. Water molecule has an angular structure with H-O-H bond angle of 104.5°, which is slightly smaller than tetrahedral angle. Alcohol molecules have a similar geometry where C-O-H angle is higher than 104.5°, and the hybridization of oxygen is sp3. For example the C-O-H bond angle in methyl alcohol is 108.9° (Figure 18.1). The C-O and O-H bond lengths are 142 pm (1.42 Å) and 96 pm (0.96 Å) respectively.\n0\n104.5°\nH\nwater\n18.1.4\n4 Ac\n0.96Å\nH\nn\nm\nH\n0.96\nH\n108.9&quot;\nHH\nmethyl alcohol\nFigure 18,1 Structure of water and methyl alcohol.\nAcidity of Alcohols\nThe acidic nature of alcohol is due to presence of polar oxygen-hydrogen (O-H) bond in alcohols.\n8- 8+",
