        "sciNote": "-HCI\n-N2\nphenol\nbenzene diazomium chloride\n18.2.6\nReactivity of Phenol\nGenerally phenols exhibit two types of reactions i.e. reactions of hydroxyl group and reactions of aromatic ring.\nIn the first type of reactions, usually involve the breaking of O-H bond in formation of salts, ethers, esters etc. In these reactions phenol somewhat resemble to alcohols. In the second type of reactions, the benzene ring of phenol undergoes electrophilic substitution reactions which are characteristic of most of the aromatic compounds e.g. nitration, sulphonation, halogenation, Friedel-Crafts alkylation and acylation etc. The presence of hydroxyl group on the benzene ring in phenol increases electron density of the ring at ortho and para positions by giving its electron pair to the ring and hence increases the reactivity of the ring relative to benzene itself, for electrophillic substitution reactions (Section 16.8.5.2.7).\n18.2.7 Reactions of Phenols\n18.2.7.1 Electrophilic Aromatic Substitutions\nIn these reactions an electrophile is introduced into the aromatic ring and the hydroxyl group remains intact. Some electrophilic substitution reactions are given\nbelow.\n1. Nitration\nPhenol reacts with dilute nitric acid to form, mainly o- and p-nitrophenol (Section\n16.8.5.2.7).\nX\n213\nNOT FOR SALE\nOH\nOH\nOH\nOH\nNO2\ndil. HNO,\n25°C -H2O\nNO2\nphenol\n-nitrophenol (55%)\nNO, p-nitrophenol (44%)\nWith concentrated nitric acid, 2,4,6-trinitrophenol is formed (Section 16.8.5.2.8).\nm-nitrophenol (1%)\nOH\nO2N.\nconc. "
