        "sciNote": "using dichromate or permanganate.\nOH\nK.Cr2O,\nH2SO1, H2O\nphenol\n0\np-benzoquinone\n18.2.8 Difference between Alcohol and Phenol\nIn both alcohols and phenols hydroxyl group is the functional group. In phenols. hydroxyl group is attached to an aromatic carbon e.g. phenol, cresols, catechol etc. While in alcohols the hydroxyl group is attached to an aliphatic carbon atom. Alcohols are further classified into aliphatic and aromatic. If an alcohol does not contain an aromatic ring, it is called aliphatic alcohol e.g. methyl alcohol, ethyl alcohol, cyclohexanol etc. But alcohols containing hydroxyl group in a side chain attached to an aromatic ring are called aromatic alcohols e.g. benzyl alcohol, 2-phenylethanol etc. Some examples:\nAliphatic alcohols;\nCH, OH\nmethyl alcohol\nCH, -CH-OH ethyl alcohol\nOH\nOH\nCH,-CH-CH-OH\nn-propyl alcohol\nOH\nOH\ncyclopentanol (cyclopentyl alcohol)\ncyclohexanol (cyclohexyl alcohol)\ncyclohexane-1,2-diol\n215\nNOT FOR SALE\nAromatic alcohols;\nDa\nCH2-OH\nphenylmethanol (benzyl alcohol)\n-CH2- CH2- OH\n2-phenylethanol\nPhenols;\nOH\nphenol\nOH\nOH\nCH,\no-cresol\nm-chlorophenol\nIt should be noted that benzyl alcohol and cresols are also isomers. But benzyl alcohol is an aromatic alcohol, while cresols are phenols.\n18.3\nEthers\nEthers are a class of organic compounds in which the oxygen atom is linked to two- alkyl groups or two aryl groups or one alkyl and one aryl group. Thus the functional group of ethers is C-O-C. They can be represented by the general formula R-O-R Ethers "
