      "18.1.6",
      "Reactivity",
      "lithium aluminum hydride (LIAIH,)",
      "The functional group of the alcohols is the hydroxyl group, which is the reactive site. This group has two reactive covalent bonds, the C-O bond and the O-H bond. The electronegativity of oxygen is substantially greater than that of carbon and hydrogen. Consequently, the covalent bonds of this functional group are polarized so that is electron rich and both carbon and hydrogen are electrophilic. The oxygen atom of alcohols has two unshared pairs of electrons and hence is an electron rich centre.",
      "oxygen",
      "ilab",
      "lone pairs",
      "H",
      "polar bonds",
      "reagents.",
      "Alcohols are reactive compounds. They readily react with polar or ionic Generally, the reactions of alcohol involve the fission of either the O-H bond or the C-O bond. The common reactions of alcohols are substitution, elimination and oxidation reactions.",
