      "18.1.7.6 Cleavage of 1, 2 diols 1,2-diols or vicinal diols, are dihydric alcohols containing two hydroxyl groups on adjacent carbon atoms. They are also called glycols. For example, ethylene glycol, propylene glycol etc (Section 18.1.1).",
      "A characteristic reaction of vicinal diols is their oxidative cleavage. The most common reagent used for oxidative cleavage of vicinal diol is periodic acid (HIO). The carbon- carbon bond of the vicinal diol unit is broken and two carbonyl groups result. Periodic acid is reduced to iodic acid (HIO). For example cleavage of ethylene glycol with periodic acid yields two molecules of formaldehydes, while propylene glycol yields",
