    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",
        "def": "◆ Discuss the reactivity of phenol and their chemistry by electrophilic aromatic substitution,\nAlcohols, phenols and ethers are derivatives of water. In alcohol and phenol one hydrogen of water has been replaced by an alkyl group and aryl group respectively. Whereas in ether both hydrogen atom have been replaced by two alkyl (or aryl)\nAlcohols are organic compounds, in which a hydroxyl group (-OH) is attached to an aliphatic carbon atom. Hydroxyl group is the functional group of alcohols. The general formula of alcohols (containing one -OH group) is R-OH or CaH2n+1OH e.g. methyl alcohol (CH3OH), ethyl alcohol (C2H2OH) etc.\nmay be monohydric or polyhydric, depending upon the number of -OH roups in a molecule. Monohydric alcohol contain one -OH group, while polyhydric cohols contain more than one -OH group. Polyhydric alcohols may further be lassified into dihydric alcohols (or diols), trihydric alcohols (or triol), etc. containing wo and three hydroxyl groups respectively. Dihydric alcohols (diols) are also called ycols because of their sweet taste. (Greek glycys means &quot;sweet&quot;) (Examples are given nomenclature)."
      }
    ],
