        "sciNote": "100°C\npropyne\npropen-2-ol\n19.4.3\nOxidation of Alcohols\nacetone\nOxidation of a primary alcohol with strong oxidizing agents like, acidified chromate (CrO42), dichromate (Cr2O72) or permanganate (MnO4) solution leads to the formation of a carboxylic acid as final product, through aldehydes intermediate. However, good yield of aldehydes can be obtained using oxidizing agents such as\n232\nNOT FOR SALE\npyridinium chlorochromate (PCC), a complex of chromium trioxide with pyridine and HCl, without over (excessive) oxidation (Section 18.1.7.5).\nOH\nR-C-H + [0]\nT\nH\n1°-alcohol\nOH\nCH,-C-H + [0]\nH\nethyl alcohol\nPCC,CH,CI,\n-H2O\nR-C-H aldehyde\nPCC, CH, CI,\nCH,-C-H\n-H2O\nacetaldehyde\nSecondary alcohols are oxidized to ketones by the same reagents that oxidize primary\nalcohols.\nilab\nOH\nR-C-H + [0]\nR\n2°-alcohol\nOH\nCH,-C-H + [0]\nI\nCH,\nisopropyl alcohol.\nR-C-R\n-H2O\nketone\n-H2O\nCH1-C-CH1\nacetone\n19.4.4 Friedel-Crafts Acylation of Aromatics\nBenzene reacts with acid halides (or anhydrides) in the presence of a Lewis acid catalyst. to give aromatic ketones. For example, benzene react with acetyl chloride in the presence of aluminum chloride catalyst to form acetophenone (Section 16.8.5.2.6).\nbenzene\n+\nar\nCH, C-Cl\nacetyl chloride\nAICI,\nacetophenone\nCH,\n+ HCl\n233\n19.5 Reactivity of Carbonyl Group\nNOT FOR SALE\nderivatives. Their most common reactions are nucleophilic addition; addition of a Ketones and aldehydes undergo many reactions to give a wide variety of useful nucleophile and a proton across the C"
