      "19.6 Reactions of Aldehydes and Ketones.",
      "ein",
      "Aldehydes and ketones are very reactive substances mostly undergoing nucleophile addition reaction across C=O bond. These can also be oxidized as well as reduced.",
      "Some of their reactions are;",
      "19.6.1",
      "Nucleophilic Addition reactions: (Acid and Base catalyzed) As already discussed in Section 19.5, that aldehydes and ketones mainly undergo nucleophilic additions reactions. In nucleophilic addition reaction, the nucleophile attacks on the carbonyl carbon, and the hybridization of carbon changes from sp2 to sp3. The geometry also changes from trigonal planar to tetrahedral. The electrons of the pi bond are forced out to the oxygen atom to form an alkoxide ion, which is protonated to give the product of nucleophilic addition. A generalized example of nucleophilic addition of strong nucleophile (Nu), followed by protonation of alkoxide ion is shown below.",
