        "theory": "C2H, ethoxide\nion\nOH2O\nhydronium ion\nKa- 10-16 pKa = 16\nIonization of acetic acid:\nCH, C-OH + H2O\nacetic acid\nCH,-C-0+ H2O\nKa= 1.8x10\nacetate ion\nhydronium pKa = 4.76\nion\nThe ionization of ethanol yields ethoxide ion, in which the negative charge is localiz on oxygen. However, the carboxylate ion is stabilized by the delocalization of negativ charge on two oxygen atoms.\nThe acidities of the carboxylic acids vary considerably with the nature of the substituents present in the molecule. Any substituent that stabilizes the negatively charged carboxylate ion promotes dissociation and vice versa. Electron\nwithdrawing substituents (e.g. -X, -NO, etc.)\nTidbit\nElectron-withdrawing groups enhance acidity because they help to stabilit the negative charge of the conjugat base (the carboxylate ion).\nThe amount of stabilization depend on: the number of electro\nwithdrawing groups, the strength enhance the strength of an acid by the electron-withdrawing groups and withdrawing electron density from the withdrawing groups from the COO\ncarboxylate ion. This inductive effect can be\nquite large if one or more strongly electron-\nthe position of the electro\ngroup.\nwithdrawing groups are present on the carbon atom adjacent to carboxyl carb\n256\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nWhereas an electron donating substituent (e.g. alkyl groups) will tend to destabilize the carboxylate ion and thus decreases the acidity of the acid. (Table 20.2) lists values of pKa for some substituted carboxylic acids, showing how electron-withdrawing groups\nand electron-donating groups affect the strength of an acid.\nTable 20.2 pKa Values of some Substituted Carboxylic Acids\nCarboxylic acid\npropionic acid\nacetic acid\n. formic acid\nbromoacetic acid\npKa\nFormula\nCH,CH,COOH\n4.87\nCH,COOH\n4.76\nHCOOH\n3.68\nBrCH,COOH\n2.90\nchloroacetic acid\nCICH COOH\n2.86\nfluoroacetic acid\nFCH,COOH\n2.59\nnitroacetic acid\n02NCH COOH\n1.68\ndichloroacetic acid\nCl2CHCOOH\n1.29\ntrichloroacetic acid\nC1,CCOOH\n0.65\n20.5 Preparations of carboxylic Acids\nFollowing methods of preparation are used for carboxylic acids.\n20.5.1 Carbonation of Grignard Reagent\nCarboxylic acids can be prepared by the action of Grignard reagent with carbon dioxide. This reaction is known as carbonation or carboxylation of Grignard reagent. Grignard reagent reacts with carbon dioxide to give initially an addition product, which on acid workup finally yield a carboxylic acid (Section 17.2.3.3).\nR-MgX+ C\ndry ether\ncarbon dioxide\nLove\nR-C-OMgX addition product\nH2O*\n(workup)\nR-C-OH carboxylic acid\nThe acid obtained by the carboxylation of Grignard reagent has one more carbon atom than alkyl or aryl halide from which Grignard reagent is prepared, so the reaction provides a way to extend the length of carbon chain.\n*\n257\n20.5.2 Hydrolysis of Nitriles\nNOT FOR SALE\nHydrolysis of nitriles with aqueous acid or base yields a carboxylic acid. In the presence of base catalyst, the final product is actually salt of carboxylic acid, which on acid workup yields carboxylic acid.\nAcid-catalyzed hydrolysis:",
