        "id": "21.2",
        "title": "21.1.1 Classification",
        "theory": "CH,01\nFructose (open chain form)\nOH\nGlucose (cyclic form)\nCH OH\nOH\nCH2OH\nH\nH\nOH\nOH\nH\n274\nFructose (cyclic form)\n=\nNOT FOR SALE\ngroup\nThe monosaccharides are again subdivided on the basis of the type of functional p On the basis of types of functional group monosaccharides may be classified as aldoses or ketoses. There are only two trioses: glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone. Each has two hydroxyl groups, attached to different carbon atoms and one carbonyl group.\nCHOH\nCH2OH\nGlyceraldehyde (an aldose)\nCH,OH\n1\nCH2OH\nDihydroxyacetone\n(a ketuse)\nCH,OH\nI\nCHOH\nCH,OH\nGlycerol\nSimilarly, on the basis of number of carbon atom in a molecule monosaccharides may\nbe trioses, tetroses, pentoses, hexoses and so on.\n2. Oligosaccharide\nAn oligosaccharide is a carbohydrate that contains two to ten monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other.\nDisaccharides are the most common type of oligosaccharide.\nA disaccharide is a carbohydrate that contains two monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other. Like monosaccharides, disaccharides are crystalline, water- soluble substances. Sucrose (table sugar) and lactose (milk sugar) are disaccharides. Those oligosaccharides, which contain three and four units of monosaccharides per molecule, are called trisaccharides and tetrasaccharides respectively. Complete hydrolysis of an oligosaccharide produces similar or different types of monosaccharides. For example, maltose yields the same kind of two glucose units upon hydrolysis.\nC12H2O+ H2O\nmaltose\n2C, H120\nglucose\nWhile sucrose yields two different types of monosaccharides i.e. glucose and\nfructose upon hydrolysis.\nC12H2O1 + H2O\n22\nsucrose\nGlycosidic Linkage or Bond\nCH,O + CHO\nglucose\nfructose\nDuring the formation of disaccharides, the two monosaccharaide units combine together by an oxygen atom with the elimination of one water molecule. This type of linkage is\n275\ncalled glycosidic linkage or glycosidic bond e.g. two glucose units combined by a\nbond to form maltose as shown below.\nCH2OH\nALE glycosidic\nCH2OH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nO\nOH\nOH\nH\nglycosidic bond\nOH\nOH\nH\nH\nOH\nH\nOH\nmaltose\nPolysaccharides\nA polysaccharide is a complex class of carbohydrate that contains many monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other. The number of monosaccharide units present in a polysaccharide varies from above ten units to more than hundreds\nunits.\nPolysaccharides, like disaccharides and oligosaccharides, undergo hydrolysis under appropriate conditions to produce monosaccharides.",
        "content": "CH,01<br>Fructose (open chain form)<br>OH<br>Glucose (cyclic form)<br>CH OH<br>OH<br>CH2OH<br>H<br>H<br>OH<br>OH<br>H<br>274<br>Fructose (cyclic form)<br>=<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>group<br>The monosaccharides are again subdivided on the basis of the type of functional p On the basis of types of functional group monosaccharides may be classified as aldoses or ketoses. There are only two trioses: glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone. Each has two hydroxyl groups, attached to different carbon atoms and one carbonyl group.<br>CHOH<br>CH2OH<br>Glyceraldehyde (an aldose)<br>CH,OH<br>1<br>CH2OH<br>Dihydroxyacetone<br>(a ketuse)<br>CH,OH<br>I<br>CHOH<br>CH,OH<br>Glycerol<br>Similarly, on the basis of number of carbon atom in a molecule monosaccharides may<br>be trioses, tetroses, pentoses, hexoses and so on.<br>2. Oligosaccharide<br>An oligosaccharide is a carbohydrate that contains two to ten monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other.<br>Disaccharides are the most common type of oligosaccharide.<br>A disaccharide is a carbohydrate that contains two monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other. Like monosaccharides, disaccharides are crystalline, water- soluble substances. Sucrose (table sugar) and lactose (milk sugar) are disaccharides. Those oligosaccharides, which contain three and four units of monosaccharides per molecule, are called trisaccharides and tetrasaccharides respectively. Complete hydrolysis of an oligosaccharide produces similar or different types of monosaccharides. For example, maltose yields the same kind of two glucose units upon hydrolysis.<br>C12H2O+ H2O<br>maltose<br>2C, H120<br>glucose<br>While sucrose yields two different types of monosaccharides i.e. glucose and<br>fructose upon hydrolysis.<br>C12H2O1 + H2O<br>22<br>sucrose<br>Glycosidic Linkage or Bond<br>CH,O + CHO<br>glucose<br>fructose<br>During the formation of disaccharides, the two monosaccharaide units combine together by an oxygen atom with the elimination of one water molecule. This type of linkage is<br>275<br>called glycosidic linkage or glycosidic bond e.g. two glucose units combined by a<br>bond to form maltose as shown below.<br>CH2OH<br>ALE glycosidic<br>CH2OH<br>H<br>H<br>H<br>H<br>H<br>H<br>O<br>OH<br>OH<br>H<br>glycosidic bond<br>OH<br>OH<br>H<br>H<br>OH<br>H<br>OH<br>maltose<br>Polysaccharides<br>A polysaccharide is a complex class of carbohydrate that contains many monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other. The number of monosaccharide units present in a polysaccharide varies from above ten units to more than hundreds<br>units.<br>Polysaccharides, like disaccharides and oligosaccharides, undergo hydrolysis under appropriate conditions to produce monosaccharides.",
        "rules": [],
        "sciNote": "CH,01\nFructose (open chain form)\nOH\nGlucose (cyclic form)\nCH OH\nOH\nCH2OH\nH\nH\nOH\nOH\nH\n274\nFructose (cyclic form)\n=\nNOT FOR SALE\ngroup\nThe monosaccharides are again subdivided on the basis of the type of functional p On the basis of types of functional group monosaccharides may be classified as aldoses or ketoses. There are only two trioses: glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone. Each has two hydroxyl groups, attached to different carbon atoms and one carbonyl group.\nCHOH\nCH2OH\nGlyceraldehyde (an aldose)\nCH,OH\n1\nCH2OH\nDihydroxyacetone\n(a ketuse)\nCH,OH\nI\nCHOH\nCH,OH\nGlycerol\nSimilarly, on the basis of number of carbon atom in a molecule monosaccharides may\nbe trioses, tetroses, pentoses, hexoses and so on.\n2. Oligosaccharide\nAn oligosaccharide is a carbohydrate that contains two to ten monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other.\nDisaccharides are the most common type of oligosaccharide.\nA disaccharide is a carbohydrate that contains two monosaccharide units covalently bonded to each other. Like monosaccharides, disaccharides are crystalline, water- soluble substances. Sucrose (table sugar) and lactose (milk sugar) are disaccharides. Those oligosaccharides, which contain three and four units of monosaccharides per molecule, are called trisaccharides and tetrasaccharides respectively. Complete hydrolysis of an oligosaccharide produces similar or different types of monosaccharides. For example, maltose yields the same kind of two glucose units upon hydrolysis.\nC12H2O+ H2O\nma",
