        "sciNote": "NOT FOR SALE\nEnzyme activity is also effected by pH. Some enzymes work best in alkaline pH while others in acidic pH. As the pH is decreased or increased, the nature of the various acid and the side chains is altered, which results in change in the overall shape and\namino\non groups structure of the enzyme.\nEach enzyme has a pH at which it shows the maximum activity. This pH is called optimum pH. For example, the optimum pH of pepsin is around 2.0 while that of trypsin varies from 8-9 pH. The pH below which the enzyme loses its activity and at which the enzymes shows the lowest activity is called minimum pH. Most of the enzymes have optimum\nactivities in the range 5-9 pH. Pepsin is an exception to this rules.\nPlotting a graph between enzyme activity and pH, we get a curve as shown in the Figure 21.4.\nOptimum pH\nIncreasing enzyme activity\nEnzyme Inhibition\n5 6 7 8 9 10 11\npH\naz e in\nFigure 21.4 Effect of pH on enzyme.\nThe process by which the activity of an enzyme is decreased or inhibited by the addition of certain substances to the reaction is called enzyme inhibition. The different substance, which inhibit the activity of enzymes, are known as inhibitors. Poisons and drugs are examples of enzyme inhibitors. According to the similarity between the inhibitor and the\nsubstrate, enzyme inhibition is classified into:\nL Irreversible Inhibition\nWhen the inhibitor reacts and form a strong covalent bond with the active site of the enzyme then it termed as irreversible inhibition. The"
