        "theory": "Production of ethanol\nIn biological industry, cellulase are used to break down cellulose into sugar which can then be fermented to produce ethanol.\niv. High fructose syrup\nA sweetener that is widely used in food and drinks is high fructose corn syrup. It is manufactured from starch in corn fruit. The grains are milled to starch slurry and the enzyme amylase is added. Finally, the syrup is passed down a column of immobilized glucose isomerase enzymes. This converts much of glucose to fructose. It is added\nsweeten foods without adding too many calories.\n290\nNOT FOR SALE\nindustry, enzymes like amylases, xylanases and cellulases etc. are used. Beside this, numerous enzymes are used in other industrial processes; such as, in paper\n21.4 Lipids\nLipids\nare a heterogeneous group of organic\nTidbit\ncompounds. They are greasy substances Saponifiable and Non-saponifible Lipids which are relatively insoluble in water and Saponifiable lipids are easily hydrolysed soluble in non-polar organic solvents like in basic condition such as, sodium\nether, benzene etc.\nhydroxide and those generally have ester\nLipids perform several important functions in linkages. Most lipids are saponifiable.\nliving organisms.\nNon-saponifiable lipids cannot be\nThey act as storehouses of metabolic hydrolysed by sodium hydroxide because\nenergy.\nthey have no ester bonds.\nThey act as structural components of membranes.\nThey act as protective and insulating coatings.\nNervous tissues are rich in lipids where they play important role in their\nfunctions.\nThe sub-cutaneous fat serves the role of insulating against atmospheric heat and cold.\nLipids may be saponfiable or non-saponifiable. Fats, oils and waxes are saponifiable while terpenes and steroids are non-saponifiable lipids.\n21.4.1\nClassification\nLipids have been classified into following three major classes:\n1. Simple Lipids\nThese are the esters of fatty acids with different types of alcohols. These include,\n(1). Fats and oils, which yield fatty acids and glycerol upon hydrolysis. (ii). Waxes, which yield fatty acids and long-chain alcohols upon hydrolysis.\n(1) Fats and Oils\nBoth types of compounds are called Triacylglycerols or triglycerides because they are esters composed of three fatty acids joined to glycerol. The difference is on the basis of their physical states at room temperature. Fat is solid at room temperature while oil is liquid at room temperature. These differences in melting points reflect differences in the degree of unsaturation of the constituent fatty acids. Oils contain more un-saturation in the alkyl part of fatty acids than fats. Fatty acids are long chain carboxylic acids contain usually\n12-18 carbon atoms per molecule.\n291\n(ii)\nWaxes\nCH2OH\nCHOH+3RCOOH\nCH2OH\nglycerol fatty acid\nCH2COOR\nNOT FOR SALE\nCHCOOR + 3H2O\nΓ\nCH,COOR\nTriacylglycerol\n(triglyceride)\nWax is an ester of long-chain high molecular weight alcohol (usually mono-\nhydroxy) and a fatty acid.\nThe acids and alcohols normally found in waxes have chains of the order of 12-34\ncarbon atoms in length.\nFor example, bees wax and carnauba wax etc.\n2. Compound Lipids\nThese are the esters of fatty acids with alcohols containing some additional groups, as well. These are further divided into.\ni. Phospholipids\nThese lipids contain fatty acids, phosphoric acid, nitrogenous bases along with some other constituents. They are also called phosphatides.\nii. Glycolipids\nThey contain fatty acid, alcohol and carbohydrates. They are present in large amount in the white matter of brain and in the myelin sheaths of nerves. iii. Sulpholipids\nThey contain a sulphate group in addition to fatty acids, alcohol and carbohydrate. iv. Lipo-Proteins\nThese are the complexes of lipids with proteins.\n3. Derived Lipids\naz\nThese are the substances derived by the hydrolysis of simple and compound lipids. These include fatty acids, alcohols, steroids, terpens, arytenoids etc.\n21.4.2\nStructure\nLipid has no common structure. The most common occurring lipids are triglycerides\nglycerol\nand phospholipids. Triglycerides are fats and oils. Triglycerides have a backbone bonded to three fatty acids. If the three fatty acids are similar, the fats are called simple triglycerides. If the fatty acids are not similar, they are known as mixed\ntriglycerides.\nThe second most common lipids are phospholipids. They are found in cell membrane of animals and plants. Phospholipids contain glycerol and fatty acids as well as\n292\nNOT FOR SALE\nacid and low molecular weight alcohol. Some lipids and their structure are given in Table\n21.2\nTable 21.2 Lipids and their Structure\nName of Lipids/Fatty Acids\nStearic acid",
