        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 9",
        "theory": "CH2O-C-C17H15\n0\n35\nCHO-C-CH + H2O\n+ H2O\nCH2O-C-C1H35 Tristearin\nTypes of Rancidity\nNOT FOR SALE\nCH2-OH\n* C, HCOOH +CHO-C-CH\nThere are three types/pathways of rancidity: 1. Oxidative Rancidity\n0\nCH2O-C-CH35\nStearic Acid + Distearin\nOxidative rancidity also known as autoxidation. The oxidation by the atmospheric O2 cleave the double bonds of polyunsaturated fatty acids present in triacylglycerols by free radical reactions. This reaction causes the release of malodorous and highly volatile aldehydes and ketones.\n2. Hydrolytic Rancidity\nHydrolytic rancidity also known as hydrolysis/enzymatic oxidation. It is due to the contamination of fat by lipase leading to the release of free fatty acids. In particular, short chain fatty acids, such as butyric acid are malodorous.\n3. Microbial Rancidity\nIn which micro-organisms such as bacteria, moulds and yeast use their enzymes to break down fats, producing unwanted odours and flavours. Water needs to be\npresent for microbial growth to occur. It can be prevented by sterilization and addition of antioxidant ingredients, such as vitamin E.\nSociety, Technology And Science\nInsulin is a hormone released from the pancreas (an organ behind the stomach that also produces digestive enzymes). This hormone (insulin) controls the amount of glucose in the blood. Glucose in the bloodstream stimulates the pancreas to produce insulin. Insulin helps glucose to move from the blood into the cells. Inside the cells, glucose is converted to energy, which is used immediately, or the glucose is stored as fat or glycogen in the body until it is needed. The levels of glucose in the blood vary normally throughout the day. They rise after a meal and return to pre-meal levels within about 2 hours after eating. Once the levels of glucose in the blood return to pre-meal levels, insulin production decreases.\nThe variation in blood glucose levels is usually within a narrow range, about 70 to 110 mg levels may increase more. If the body does not produce enough insulin to move the glucose per 100cm of blood in healthy people. If people eat a large amount of carbohydrates, the into the cells, or if the cells stop responding normally to insulin (called insulin resistance),\nX\n295\nNOT FOR SALE\nthe resulting high levels of glucose in the blood and the inadequate amount of glucose in the\ncells together produce the symptoms and complications of diabetes.\nDiabetes mellitus is a disorder in which the amount of sugar in the blood is elevated. Doctors often use the full name diabetes mellitus, rather than diabetes alone, to distinguish this disorder from diabetes insipidus.\nDiabetes mellitus is a disorder in which blood sugar (glucose) levels are abnormally high because the body does not produce enough insulin to meet its needs.\nσ Diabetes insipidus is a relatively rare disorder that does not affect blood glucose\nlevels but, just like diabetes mellitus, also causes increased urination.\n21.4.4 Nutritional and Biological Importance of lipids\nLipids perform a number of different functions in living organisms. Some of these are listed\nbelow.\nLipids provide food, which is highly rich in calorific value. One gram of lipid on complete oxidation produces 9.3 kcal of heat, which is about double the produced by carbohydrates and proteins.\namount\nLipids are insoluble in water and, therefore, they can be stored easily in the body as food reserves. The organisms then use these food reserves during hibernation periods.\nLipids in association with different proteins form the important constituents of cell membranes.\nFats have high insulating capacity. Great quantities of fats are deposited in the sub- cutaneous layers in aquatic mammals such as whale and in animals of cold climates. These fat deposits protect these animals from severe cold and severe temperature changes.\nSome lipids especially phospholipids play a key role transportation of fatty acids in the body.\nthe absorption and\n◆ Sex hormones, adrenocorticoids, cholic acids are synthesized from a steroidal lipid\nknown as cholesterol.\nLipids serve as carrier of fats soluble vitamins such as vitamin A, D and E in the body.\nSquealing, a steroid present in the blood of shark has antibiotic and antifungal properties. This explains why sharks rarely contract infections and almost never get cancer.\n21.5 Nucleic Acids\nNucleic acids are macromolecules that allow organisms to transfer genetic information from one generation to the next. They are present in every living organism as well as viruses. They have been found to be the essential substance of the genes and the apparatus\n*\n296\n*\nNOT FURSALE\nby which the genes act or express themselves. They contain in their structure the blueprints for the normal growth and development of each and every organism\nNucleic acids are the main information-carrying molecules of the cell, and, by directing the process of protein synthesis, they determine the inherited characteristics of every living thing.\nThe two main classes of nucleic acids are deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and\nribonucleic acid (RNA).\nDNA is the genetic material found in living organisms, from single-celled bacteria\nto multicellular mammals.\nRNA is the genetic material of certain viruses, but it is also found in all living cells, where it plays an important role in certain processes such as the synthesis of proteins.\nThey are capable of being broken down to yield phosphoric acid, sugars, and a mixture of organic bases (purines and pyrimidines).\nNucleic acids are substances present in the nuclei of cells which are involved in the storage, expressing, transmission and processing of genetic information and mutation in living organisms. The monomer that makes up a nucleic acid is called a nucleotide.\nTypes of Nucleic Acids\nThere are two types of nucleic acids.\nDNA (Deoxyribonucleic acid) it is present in nuclei and in some viruses. It is the main constituent of chromosome.\nRNA (Ribonucleic acid) is present in the cytoplasm and in some viruses.\n21.5.1 Structural Components of DNA and RNA\nWatson and Crick determined the structure of nucleic acids. Both DNA and RNA are formed by the polymerization of large number of units known as nucleotides. That is why DNA and RNA are polynucleotides. A nucleotide is composed of a nitrogenous base, a sugar and a phosphate group. DNA differs from RNA in the type of nitrogenous base and type of sugar unit. The nitrogenous bases are of two types, purine and\npyrimidine.\nH\nN\nN\nC\nN\nCH\nHC\nN\nprurine\nH\n¥\npyrimidine",
