        "sciNote": "Sources of Calcium\nHigh amount of calcium is found in dairy products such as milk and yogurt. On average 250mL of cows' milk or 150g yogurt contains 300mg of calcium.\nThe main sources of calcium are milk and milk products, egg yolk, legumes, green leafy vegetables, almonds, hazelnuts, broccoli, okra, spinach, apricot, figs, pulses, sesame seeds.\nDeficiency of Calcium\nila\nDecrease of calcium in the diet is contributing to a factor known as osteoporosis, a condition that causes brittle bones in adults.\nDecrease of calcium level in plasma leads to a condition known as hypocalcemia. Excess of Calcium\nIncrease in the concentration of calcium in plasma leads to a condition known as hyperparathyroidism.\niii. Phosphorus\nPhosphorous is also the major constituent of bones and teeth. It is present in all the body cells in association with proteins, lipids and carbohydrates in the form of phosphoproteins, phospholipids and similar compounds.\nThe major function of phosphorus is in the formation of the bone component hydroxyapatite with calcium. 80% of the phosphorous in the body is present as calcium salts in the skeleton and, therefore, is essential for healthy bone and tooth structure. It has à unique role in the storage and transformation of energy in the body.\nDaily Requirement\nAn adult requires about 800mg per day of phosphorous.\nSources of Phosphorous\nMost foods contain phosphorus. It is present in association with calcium in its sources.\n303\nNOT FOR SALE\nFoods that are rich in prot"
