        "title": "22.3 Dyes",
        "theory": "22.2 Safety Considerations in Process Industries The following safety consideration in the process industry must be kept in view before\nlaunching an industrial activity.\nTo keep away the factory itself from corroding away, proper material construction should be selected by the designing chemical engineers. To avoid harmful impures in raw materials, to follow the course of chemical reactions and to secure the requisite yield and purity of products, careful process\n312\n*\ncontrol by periodic analysis is required as well as modern instrumentation and NOT FOR SALE\nautomatic control.\nTo transmit goods in a clean and economical manner from the manufacturer to the customer, suitable containers must be used.\nTo ensure the safety of workers and the plant all procedures, must be carried on in a non-hazardous manner.\nTo guarantee progress to continue profits and to replace obsolescent processes and equipments much attention and money must be spent upon containing research and development.\nTo prevent the contamination of water and air, factories must avoid discharge of toxic material into the air and water of their localities.\nTo label the chemicals that can help in identifying the more hazardous chemicals.\n22.3 Dyes\nDyes are substances that are used to impart colour to various substrates, which adds value to products for their cost, including paper, leather, fur, hair, drugs, cosmetics, waxes, greases, plastics and textile materials.\nA dye is a coloured compound, normally used in solution, which is capable of being fixed to a fabric.\nThe dye has a colour due to the presence of chromophore and its fixed is due to the acidic or basic groups such as-OH, -SO,H,-NH,,-NR,, etc.\nproperty\nThe polar auxochrome makes the dye water-soluble and binds the dye to the fabric by interaction with the oppositely charged groups of fabric structure. Properties of Dye\nDyes must possess the following four properties:\n1. Colour\n2. Solubility in water 3. Ability to be absorbed and retained by fibre (substantivity) or to be chemically combined with it (reactivity).\nAbility to withstand washing, dry\ncleaning and exposure to light.\nChemical Composition of a Dye\nTidbit\nPigment and Dye\nPigments and dyes are called colorants. The ways in which colorants are used determines\nwhether they are pigments or dyes.\nPigments are water and oil-insoluble, natural and synthetic products that impart colour to materials such as paper and plastics. Whereas,\nDyes, are water-soluble colorants, although some are converted into insoluble lake pigments by co-precipitating onto an inorganic base. Artists&#x27; colours are pigments\nA dye consists of a colour producing that are spread on a surface suspended in a\nstructure called the &#x27;chromogen&#x27; and a part\n313\nsuitable medium, such as oil.\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nto regulate the solubility and dying properties called the &quot;auxochrome&#x27;. The chromogen is an aromatic body containing a colour-giving group, commonly called the &#x27;chromosphore&quot;. Chromophore is that part of the molecule which when exposed to visible light will absorb and reflect a certain colour. Some common chromophores are given in the Table 22.1. Table 22.1 Some Common Chromophore Groups\nChormophore\nS. No\nNitroso group\n1.\n2.\nNitro group\n3.\nAzo group\n4.\nEthylene group\n5.\nCarbonyl group\nStructure\n-NO (or N-OH)\n-NO2 (or-NOOH)\n-N-N-\n-C=C-\n0\nC-\n6.\nCarbon nitrogen group\n7.\nCarbon Sulphur Groups\nZ=\n-CEN-C-\n- CH2- SH\nAuxochromes are commonly called as &#x27;colour helpers&#x27; or &#x27;colour intensifiers&quot;. The auxochrome, the part of the dye which causes it to adhere to the material which it colours (usually textiles) are -NH,, -OH, NR,,-COOH and SO,H of these groups. -NH, and - NR, cause solubility in acids while - OH, -COOH and -SO,H cause solubility in basic solutions.\nClassification of Dye: There are several ways for classification of dyes. One of the prominent ways of classification is the classification based on the source of materials. It is further divided into two main types:\ni. Natural Dyes ii. Synthetic Dyes\ni. Natural Dye\naw on the\nNatural dyes are dyes or colorants derived from plants, invertebrates, or minerals. The majority of natural dyes are vegetable dyes from plant sources e.g. roots, berries, bark, leaves and wood. Other organic sources include fungi and lichens.\nii. Synthetic Dyes\nAlmost all the colours in use today are synthetic dyes. Synthetic dyes are used everywhere in everything from clothes to paper, from food to wood. This is because they are cheaper to produce, brighter, more colour-fast and easy to apply to fabric.\nFor example, acid dyes, azo dyes, basic dyes, mordant dyes, etc.\n314\nTidbit\nNOT FOR SALE\nDifferences between dyes and pigments Dyes and pigments are substances that impart colour to a material. The major difference between dyes and pigments is the particle size. Dyes are much finer than pigments. Dyes, also known as colourants in which the colouring matter is dissolved in liquid, are absorbed into the material to which they are applied.\nPigments, on the other hand, consist of extremely fine particles of ground colouring matter suspended in liquid which forms a paint like film that actually bonds to the surface.\n22.4 Pesticides\nPesticides are chemicals that are used to destroy, repel or otherwise lower pest infestations to protect crops from damage or simply we can say a substance that kills a pest (insect, weed, bacteria, nematode etc.), for example lead arsenate, Pb,(ASO),\nnicotine sulphate etc.",
