        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 5",
        "theory": "-NH|\n(hexanedioic acid)\nPreparation of Nylon (6, 10)\nC-(CH,)-C-NH(CH,), NH+ 2nH,0\nnylon-6,6\nNylon is the most useful synthetic material with applications varying from daily life to industries. Nylon (6, 10) is a synthetic polymer. Nylons (6, 10) is prepared in the\n321\nNOT FOR SAL\nlaborating by reacting 1, 6 diaminohexane (hexamethylene diamine) with decanedic\ndichloride (sebacoyl chloride).\n0\n-2nHCl\nnH,N(CH,), NH2+ CIC(CH), CCI\nhexamethylene diamine\nsebacoyl chloride\nUses of PVC and Nylon\nN(CH),NC(CH),C-\nH\nH\nnylon-6,10\nUses of PVC\nPVC or polyvinyl chloride, is a type of plastic used in numerous industries. It durable, inexpensive and resistant to heat, water and chemicals.\nPVC plastic is manipulated to create a leather-like material called rexine. PVC are used for pipes. These pipes are strong, lightweight and less reacti They do not wear, rot or rust, a dependable and affordable material. PVC plastic is used to form the insulating material on electrical wires.\nPVC plastic is used to manufacture bottles.\nOther uses of PVC include medical tubing, PVC window frames, flex packaging, blood bags, resilient flooring, vinyl panelling, drainage pip gramophones records, carpet backing, etc.\nUses of Nylon\nNylon is a versatile and incredibly useful material.\nin\nStockings-nylons strong fibres can be woven together to produce a lightwe silky fabric that is perfect for stockings.\nParachutes-nylon is used in the manufacture of parachutes.\nTents and camping equipment - durability and resistance make nylon a g choice for all kinds of camping equipments.\nBoating and Sailing - nylon is used in the production of many sails and rop Sports clothing and footwear-many swimming suits, trunks and athletic sl contain nylon.\nBags and cases - camera cases, bags and luggage will invariably be mad Nylon as it offers excellent durability and protection.\nNylon is also used in moulded machine parts such as gears and bearings.\n322\nht\nat\nes\nof\nSociety, Technology And Science\nNOT FOR SALE\nproduced by polymerization. Synthetic fibres are manufactured usually from products obtained Synthetic fibres are man-made fibres. Most of the synthetic fibres are made from polymers from petroleum, etc. Depending on the type of fibres and nature of these chemicals, they are combined with acids, alcohol and sometimes heated to produce the synthetic fibre.\nHistory of Synthetic fibres\nIn 1865, the cellulose acetate, commonly known as acetate rayon, by treating cellulose\nwith acetic anhydride.\nIn 1894, the viscose fibre is prepared from viscous solution of xanthate, which is produced from the reaction of carbon disulphide and cellulose in basic medium.\nIn the 1930s, the chemical firm DuPont developed nylon, the first synthetic fibre. During 1941, produced the first polyester fibre known as the Dacron.\nIn 1950, the acrylic fibres (plastic fibres) resembling wool was prepared.\nDuring 1965, Kevlar was developed.\nUses of Synthetic fibres\nThe introduction of synthetic fibres changed the textile industry. Because their properties differed\nfrom natural fibres.\nO Nylon is used in clothing, shirts, garments, lingerie, raincoats, swimwear and cycle wear. Industrial uses includes seat belts, parachutes, airbags, nets and ropes, tarpaulins, thread, tents and fishnets.\nKevlar is used in ropes, tyres, sports equipment and bullet-proof vests. Polyesters are used in clothing fabrics such as shirts, jackets, pants and hats.\n22.7 Cosmetics: Lipsticks, Nail Varnish and Remover, Hair Dyes\nCosmetics are chemicals, which are intended to be applied to the human body for cleansing, beautifying or altering the appearance without affecting the body&#x27;s functions.\n(a). Lipstick\nLipstick are basically dispersions of colouring matter in a base containing a suitable blend of oils, fats and waxes suitably perfumed, flavoured and moulded in the form of stick and enclosed in a case. Lipstick is a cosmetic product containing pigments, oils, waxes and emollients that apply colour, texture and protection to lips. Lipstick is the cosmetic, which is generally formulated to provide both protection and colour. Some lipsticks are also lip balms, to add colour and hydration.\nChemical Composition of Lipstick\nThe chemical composition of lipsticks varies greatly. The general composition of\nlipsticks are\n323\nNOT FOR SALE\nA mixture of non- volatile oils, e.g. caster, vegetable, mineral or wool fat,\nlanolin oil.\nMixture of waxes, e.g. bee wax or\ncamauba.\n• Colours\nAntioxidants\n• Preservatives\nTidbit\nLipstick should be nontoxic, non-\nirritant to the lips and insoluble in\nwater so that it cannot dissolve\nwith saliva.\nUsually perfumes are also added in very small quantity to combat the unpleasant fatty odour of the oil. The oil makes the wax-based product soft and to be easily applied. In order to reduce the &#x27;stickiness&#x27;, usually, esters of fatty acids (like 2-propyl myristate) is also added. Lipsticks are made from hydrophobic materials. The dyes are water insoluble. Water-soluble dyes such as green or blue food dyes can be used to provide lipstick colouration but they are, usually, first combined with metal oxides such as aluminium hydroxide, Al(OH)3, to form an insoluble precipitate that is then suspended in the oil base of the lipstick. Colour is a key ingredient of lipsticks. Silicones and oily materials are also added to reflect light and provide shine.\n(b)\nNail Varnish or Nail Polish\ni. Nail Polish\nTidbit",
