        "sciNote": "Reactive\nNatural\nSynthetic\nDrying\nPressure-sensitive Multi-parts\nOne-part\nContact\nHot\nFigure 22.2 Types of adhesives.\nClassification of Adhesives by Origin\nAdhesives are divided according to their origin, therefore, we have synthetic adhesives and natural adhesives.\n1. Synthetic Adhesives\nin\nSynthetic adhesives are not found in nature and are designed and manufactured by man. Typical synthetic adhesives are epoxies, polyurethanes, cyanoacrylates, polyimides, silicones, acrylics, polyacrylates, polyvinyl acetate (PVA) and neoprene.\n2. Natural Adhesives (Glues)\nNatural adhesives are those adhesives that are obtained or extracted from natural resources such as plants or animals, starch, casein glues or natural rubber etc. Classification of Adhesives on Reactiveness\nAdhesives are mainly classified into two classes on reactiveness.\n1. Non-Reactive Adhesives\n2. Reactive Adhesives\nThe basis of classification is their method of adhesion.\n1. Non-Reactive Adhesives\nThese adhesives may be either of natural or synthetic origin. Drying adhesive, pressure\nclass.\n¥\nof this\n328\nL\nDrying Adhesives\nNOT FOR SALE\nThere are two types of adhesives which become hard by drying.\nii.\na. Solvent Based Adhesives\nWhich are a mixture of ingredients (typically polymers) dissolved in a solvent. White glue, contact adhesives and rubber cements are the members of the drying adhesive family. It is used for binding of the woods and\nb. Polymer Dispersion Adhesives\npapers.\nThese are also known as emulsion adhesiv",
