        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 2",
        "theory": "Characteristics of an Ideal Pesticide\nAn ideal pesticide should possess the following characteristics:\nMab\nIt should be effective only against the specific target organism. It should be non-toxic to other living organisms.\nIt should be non-persistent.\nIt should be biodegradable.\nIt should break down in nature into harmless products and cause no damage to environment.\nIt should be inexpensive and easily available.\nPesticides are the chemical substances that are used to control the pests such as DDT, heptachlor etc. DDT has the formula,\nCI\nCI\nH\nCI\ndichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT)\nDDT acts directly on insects. It is a very stable chemical, it&#x27;s half-life is estimated to be 10-15 years. DDT is insoluble in water, therefore, it accumulates in animals and in particular in their fatty tissues. Therefore, the environmentalists banned the use of DDT\nNowadays, parathion is used instead of DDT.\nin the world.\n315\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nOCH,\nOCH,\nNO, Parathion\nThis compound breaks down fairly readily to products that appear to cause no biological damage. So, it has no long term damages but carefully handling is necessary. In short, the ideal pesticide is clearly not yet available.\n22.5\nPetrochemicals\nused to\nPetroleum is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons formed by the decomposition of fossil remains. It exists as a gas (natural gas), liquid (crude oil) or as solids (oil shales) and is found deep underground or below seabed. About 10% of the petroleum prepare a range of chemicals called petrochemicals. Petrochemicals as a group of chemicals produced directly or indirectly from the hydrocarbons of petroleum or natural gas. Examples of petrochemicals are plastics, rubbers, fibres, paints, solvents, and detergents.\nSince every area of human activity makes use of petrochemicals or the materials made from them, our life without petrochemicals would be very different and less comfortable. The different petrochemicals are compiled in Table 22.2.\nTable 22.2 Some Important Petrochemicals\nS. No. Feedstock Primary petrochemicals\nW\nFormulae\nCH2=CH2\ne\nCH12-CH=CH2\nCH=CH\nEthene\n1.\nNatural gas\nPropene\nEthyne\nEthene\nCH2=CH2\n2.\nNaphtha\nPropene\nCH1-CH=CH2\nButadiene\nCH2=CH-CH=CH2\nReformed\n3.\nNaphtha\nBenzene\n316\nToluene\nNOT FOR SALE\nCH,\n0-xylene\nCH,\nCH,\northo-xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene)\nCH,\nab.com\nm-xylene\np-xylene\nCH,\nmeta-xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene)\nCH,\nCH,\nThe Basic Building Block Processes\npara-xylene (1,4-dimethylbenzene)\nThe basic building block petrochemical processes are divided into two groups. These processes are cracking and reforming process.\nL\nCracking Process.\nCracking is a process in which molecules of raw material are dissociated into smaller ones. There are two types of cracking process, thermal steam cracking and catalytic cracking.\na. Thermal Steam Cracking\nSteam cracking is the breaking of raw material such as ethane and propane yielding important petrochemical products, such as ethene and propene. Methane and\nhydrogen\nb. Catalytic Cracking",
        "content": "Characteristics of an Ideal Pesticide<br>An ideal pesticide should possess the following characteristics:<br>Mab<br>It should be effective only against the specific target organism. It should be non-toxic to other living organisms.<br>It should be non-persistent.<br>It should be biodegradable.<br>It should break down in nature into harmless products and cause no damage to environment.<br>It should be inexpensive and easily available.<br>Pesticides are the chemical substances that are used to control the pests such as DDT, heptachlor etc. DDT has the formula,<br>CI<br>CI<br>H<br>CI<br>dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT)<br>DDT acts directly on insects. It is a very stable chemical, it&#x27;s half-life is estimated to be 10-15 years. DDT is insoluble in water, therefore, it accumulates in animals and in particular in their fatty tissues. Therefore, the environmentalists banned the use of DDT<br>Nowadays, parathion is used instead of DDT.<br>in the world.<br>315<br>*<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>OCH,<br>OCH,<br>NO, Parathion<br>This compound breaks down fairly readily to products that appear to cause no biological damage. So, it has no long term damages but carefully handling is necessary. In short, the ideal pesticide is clearly not yet available.<br>22.5<br>Petrochemicals<br>used to<br>Petroleum is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons formed by the decomposition of fossil remains. It exists as a gas (natural gas), liquid (crude oil) or as solids (oil shales) and is found deep underground or below seabed. About 10% of the petroleum prepare a range of chemicals called petrochemicals. Petrochemicals as a group of chemicals produced directly or indirectly from the hydrocarbons of petroleum or natural gas. Examples of petrochemicals are plastics, rubbers, fibres, paints, solvents, and detergents.<br>Since every area of human activity makes use of petrochemicals or the materials made from them, our life without petrochemicals would be very different and less comfortable. The different petrochemicals are compiled in Table 22.2.<br>Table 22.2 Some Important Petrochemicals<br>S. No. Feedstock Primary petrochemicals<br>W<br>Formulae<br>CH2=CH2<br>e<br>CH12-CH=CH2<br>CH=CH<br>Ethene<br>1.<br>Natural gas<br>Propene<br>Ethyne<br>Ethene<br>CH2=CH2<br>2.<br>Naphtha<br>Propene<br>CH1-CH=CH2<br>Butadiene<br>CH2=CH-CH=CH2<br>Reformed<br>3.<br>Naphtha<br>Benzene<br>316<br>Toluene<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>CH,<br>0-xylene<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>ortho-xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene)<br>CH,<br>ab.com<br>m-xylene<br>p-xylene<br>CH,<br>meta-xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene)<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>The Basic Building Block Processes<br>para-xylene (1,4-dimethylbenzene)<br>The basic building block petrochemical processes are divided into two groups. These processes are cracking and reforming process.<br>L<br>Cracking Process.<br>Cracking is a process in which molecules of raw material are dissociated into smaller ones. There are two types of cracking process, thermal steam cracking and catalytic cracking.<br>a. Thermal Steam Cracking<br>Steam cracking is the breaking of raw material such as ethane and propane yielding important petrochemical products, such as ethene and propene. Methane and<br>hydrogen<br>b. Catalytic Cracking",
