        "sciNote": "Characteristics of an Ideal Pesticide\nAn ideal pesticide should possess the following characteristics:\nMab\nIt should be effective only against the specific target organism. It should be non-toxic to other living organisms.\nIt should be non-persistent.\nIt should be biodegradable.\nIt should break down in nature into harmless products and cause no damage to environment.\nIt should be inexpensive and easily available.\nPesticides are the chemical substances that are used to control the pests such as DDT, heptachlor etc. DDT has the formula,\nCI\nCI\nH\nCI\ndichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT)\nDDT acts directly on insects. It is a very stable chemical, it's half-life is estimated to be 10-15 years. DDT is insoluble in water, therefore, it accumulates in animals and in particular in their fatty tissues. Therefore, the environmentalists banned the use of DDT\nNowadays, parathion is used instead of DDT.\nin the world.\n315\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nOCH,\nOCH,\nNO, Parathion\nThis compound breaks down fairly readily to products that appear to cause no biological damage. So, it has no long term damages but carefully handling is necessary. In short, the ideal pesticide is clearly not yet available.\n22.5\nPetrochemicals\nused to\nPetroleum is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons formed by the decomposition of fossil remains. It exists as a gas (natural gas), liquid (crude oil) or as solids (oil shales) and is found deep underground or below seabed. About 10% of the petroleum prepare a range of chemicals called petro"
