      "The Basic Building Block Processes",
      "para-xylene (1,4-dimethylbenzene)",
      "The basic building block petrochemical processes are divided into two groups. These processes are cracking and reforming process.",
      "L",
      "Cracking Process.",
      "Cracking is a process in which molecules of raw material are dissociated into smaller ones. There are two types of cracking process, thermal steam cracking and catalytic cracking.",
      "a. Thermal Steam Cracking",
      "Steam cracking is the breaking of raw material such as ethane and propane yielding important petrochemical products, such as ethene and propene. Methane and",
      "hydrogen",
      "b. Catalytic Cracking",
      "are also some of the major by-products.",
      "Breaking of larger, stable molecules requires catalysis. Refinery products such as gas oil and fuel oil is passed through catalytic cracker to obtain gasoline and kerosene oil as major products and by-product, propene.",
      "317",
      "II. Reforming Process",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "The process of reforming of hydrocarbon is carried out by using heat, pressure and/or catalyst in order to obtain desired products. In this process, heavy naphtha is converted",
      "into aromatics such as benzene, toluene and xylene as well as hydrogen as",
      "by-product",
      "Reforming process breaks down the molecules of low octane rating naphtha into high octane gasoline blending components (reformates). It is one of the most important processes",
      "in oil refineries.",
      "Fractional Distillation of Petroleum",
      "Petroleum in the unrefined form is called crude oil. It is a naturally occurring thick viscous brown or greenish black liquid, which is obtained from earth crust. The crude oil consists of different substances. These substances in crude oil are miscible. Therefore they do not separate out into layers. Thus, fractional distillation, which is carried out in the fractionating towers, is used in refining petroleum. Fractional distillation is the process of heating up a mixture containing different substances with different boiling points and after condensation drawing the different fractions off. Each fraction is not pure compound but is a mixture of different hydrocarbons with similar boiling points may b Crude oil is first treated to remove the sulphur or sulphur compounds that present. The cleaned hydrocarbon material is then distilled and fractions with variou boiling ranges are collected. (Figure 22.1).",
