        "sciNote": "Therefore, any precipitation (rain, dew and snow) having a pH of less than 5.6, would be classified as acid rain. Apart from the acidifying effect of CO,, there are other pollutants which are also responsible of acid rain. Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxide\n343\nNOT FOR SALE\n(NOx), emitted into the atmosphere react with water, oxygen, and other chemicals to form sulphuric acid (H,SO,) and nitric acid (HNO,). These, then, mix with water and fall on the ground in the form of acid rain. A small portion of the SO, and NOx that cause acid rain is from natural sources such as volcanos and NO produced in lightening and forest fires, but most of them come from the burning of fossil fuels and other human\nactivities.\n4NO2+ 2H,0 +02\nSo, + H2O + 20,\n→4HNO,\nH2SO,\npower\nIn polluted areas, the concentrations of these acids can be much higher and can reduce the pH of rain water substantially. Acid rain can fall quite far from the sources of pollution, due to long-range atmospheric transport by wind and air currents. In particular, acid rain is a serious problem for areas located near the coal-fired plants. Acid rain, on one hand, is a serious threat to the human life as it contaminates the drinking water and on the other hand is a problem for plants, animals, aquatic life, and building material. Heavy metals e.g. Cu, Hg and Pb are also dissolved by acid rain producing various toxic effects.\nforms\nAcidic streams and lakes or rivers affect the aquatic life. High acidity may result in killing of"
