        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 5",
        "theory": "Pipes take water\nto treatment\ncenter\nFinal Treatment\nPrimary\nTreatment\nScreening stage\nstage\nSecondary stage\nFiltered\nTreatment\ninto river\nstage\n19412\nFigure: 23.4 Water treatment plant.\n351\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nAir is sometimes blown through the water to improve its odour and taste. A fluoride compound is added in some plants, to help fight tooth decay. The result of thorough &quot;water treatment&quot; operation is a water, that is as good as new.\n23.4\nGreen Chemistry\nThe green chemistry or sustainable chemistry is defined as: &quot;the invention, design and application of chemical products and processes to reduce or eliminate the use or generation of substances hazardous to humans, animals, plants, and the\nenvironment:\nThe aim of the green chemistry is to reduce chemical related impact on human health and virtually eliminate contaminants of the environment through dedicated sustainable prevention programs. Green chemistry searches for alternative, environmental friendly reaction media and at the same time strive to increase reaction rate and lower reaction temperature.\nGreen Chemistry is based on 12 principles proposed by Anastas and Warner. These 12 principles of green chemistry are considered fundamentals to contribute to sustainable development. The principles comprise of instructions to implement new chemical products, new synthesis and new processes as given below:\n1. Atom Economy: Design syntheses so that the final product contains the maximum proportion of the starting material. Waste few or no atoms of the reactant i.e. to maximize the incorporation of all materials, used in the process, into the final product.\n2.\n3.\n5.\nPrevent Waste: Design a process to prevent the generation of waste instead of later on treating and cleaning up the waste.\nuse and\nDesign less Hazardous Chemical Syntheses: Wherever possible design to generate substances, that possess little or no toxicity to either humans or to environment.\nDesigning Safer Chemicals: Chemical products should be designed to deliver their desired function while minimizing their toxicity.\nUse safer Solvents and Reaction Conditions: Use of solvents, separation agents, and other substances should be avoided whenever possible; if not possible, harmless and less toxic raw material should be used.\n6. Increase Energy Efficiency: Run chemical reactions at room temperature and pressure whenever possible or use of renewable energy sources (such as\nsolar energy) should be used.\n¥\n352\nX\n7.\n8.\n9.\nNOT FORSALE\nUse Renewable Feedstock: Use starting material (also known as feedstock) that are renewable (plants-based) rather than depletable. The source of renewable feedstock is often agriculture products or the waste or by-products\nof other processes.\nAvoid Chemical Derivatives: Avoid using blocking or protecting functional groups or any temporary modification by forming unnecessary derivatives, if possible; as such steps require additional reagents and may generate additional waste. This is because more derivatives involve:\nO additional reagents\nO generate more waste products O needs more time.\nOhigher cost of production.\nHence, it requires to reduce derivatives.\nUse Catalysts: Minimize waste by using catalytic reactions. Catalysts are effective in small amounts and can carry out a single reaction many times. Reactions at low temperature and pressure are possible due to use of new/improved catalysts.\n10. Design Chemicals and Products to Degrade after use: Chemical products have to be designed in such a way that, at the end of their life span, disintegrate into nontoxic degradation products more readily.\n11. Analyze the products in real-time to prevent Pollution: Advanced analytical methods for quality control analysis have to be developed, which permit the real-time, in-line process monitoring well before hazardous substances are generated.\n12. Minimize the potential for accidents: Design chemicals and their physical forms (solid, liquid, and gas) to minimize the potential for chemical accidents including explosions, fires, and release to the environment.\n353\nNOT FOR SALE\nKey Points\nEnvironmental chemistry is the branch of chemistry which deals with the presence of harmful and hazardous chemical and bio-chemicals in nature. Pollutant is any substance which contaminate our environment.\nAtmosphere is a big blanket of gases, surrounding the Earth&#x27;s surface.\nThe term acid rain is referred to all precipitation (rain, snow or dew) having pH less than 5.6.\nSmog is the combination of smoke and fog.\nWater is an important natural resource on this planet.\nAn important aspect of water quality is the amount of dissolved oxygen in it. The term green chemistry is defined as the invention, design and application of chemical products and processes to reduce or to eliminate the use and generation of hazardous substances.\nThere are two major types of smog: &#x27;industrial smog&#x27; and &#x27;photochemical smog&#x27;. Increased concentration of CO2 increases the Earth&#x27;s temperature, by greenhouse effect. This in turn results in climate changes and global warming.\nawaz e in\nC\n354\n*\nExercise\nNOT FOR SALE\nQI. Choose the correct one from the given options.\n1. Which one of the following is not a secondary pollutant?\na. 0,\nb. H,CO,\n2. The coldest region of atmosphere is\na. troposphere.\nc. stratosphere.\nc. H2SO..\nd. CO2\nb. thermosphere.",
