      "Сонь + 02 Carboxyhaemoglobin",
      "complex",
      "The iron (Fe) binding sites in hemoglobin bind carbon monoxide (CO) 320 times more tightly than Oz. The main source of CO to the environment are the vehicle emission, combustion of fuel such as natural gas, coal, or wood. When the concentration of the CO inhaled, reaches 100 ppm, particularly in the closed spaces like tunnels, parking garages etc., it may result in headache and shortness of breath. The severity of the effects depends on the duration of the exposure and level of exertion. At a concentration",
      "higher than 750 ppm, loss of consciousness or death may occur.",
      "Sulphur Dioxide (SO)",
      "The main sources of sulphur dioxide (SO,) emission to contaminate air, are combustion of coal and the smelting of metals (metallurgy) particularly copper. The sulphur content",
      "338",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "of the refined petroleum is generally quite low but the sulphur content of coal is quite high. The sulphur in coal is converted to SO2 during combustion. SO2 itself is a lung irritant and is harmful to people suffering from respiratory diseases. However, the most damaging health effects in urban atmosphere are not by SO, but by the sulphuric acid aerosol formed from its oxidation. Sulphuric acid not only irritates the fine vessels of the pulmonary region, causing them to swell and block the passages, but is also the major contributor to the acid rain.",
      "Nitrogen oxides NO, and Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs)",
      "NO is a common designation of nitrogen oxides, NO and NO2. Nitrogen oxide, particularly nitrogen dioxide, is emitted from high temperature combustion processes and is also produced during thunderstorm by natural electric discharge. NO, can cause serious health damage to humans, including respiratory diseases. NO. is also responsible for smog formation and typical brown clouds or haze that covers large cities which results in poor air quality. NO, emission also contributes to acid rain and formation of ground level ozone that can damage animal and plant life. NO. reacts with volatile organic compounds in the presence of sunlight and forms ground level ozone which can be transported with wind over longer distance. High level NO, concentration can have negative effects on vegetation and causes forest damage.",
      "Volatile organic compounds (VOCs) include methane, CH,. It is an efficient greenhouse gas which contributes to enhance global warming. The major sources of VOCs emission are the industrial processes, solvent utilization and vehicular emission.",
      "Ozone (0) as Pollutant",
      "Some VOCs are responsible for ozone production in lower atmosphere at the ground level. Ozone at ground level is quite harmful, producing cracks in rubber, destroying plants, and causing respiratory distress and eye irritation in humans. These effects set in at quite low concentrations, around 100 ppb.",
      "The naturally produced ozone in upper troposphere is useful for life as it prevents us from harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiations, however, some man-made chemicals like chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are harmful to this protective ozone layer. The CFCs are emitted from air conditioners, refrigerators, aerosols sprays, etc. On release into air, CFCs rise to stratosphere where, they come in contact with other gases and through chemical reaction damage the ozone layer. This allows harmful UV rays to reach the",
      "Earth's surface and have adverse effects.",
      "339",
      "23.1.3 Automobile, Pollutants and the Catalytic Converter",
      "Tidbit",
      "SALE",
      "The major pollutants, present in the exhaust gases emitted by automobiles, are carbon A 3-way catalytic converter is a monoxide (CO), nitric oxide (NO) and the device used in the exhaust system of hydrocarbons (HCs). These gases disturb the the engine of the vehicles to reduce composition of the clean air and thus cause air emission of hydrocarbon (HCs), carbon monoxide (CO) and nitric oxide (NO)",
      "pollution. When present in considerable amount,",
      "they cause damage to human health and plants life.",
      "One way of reducing emissions is to remove the pollutants from the exhaust gases. In automobiles, this is accomplished with a three-way catalytic converter.",
      "It converts harmful gases in the engine exhaust to relatively harmless gases; nitrogen (N2), carbon dioxide (CO2) and water.",
      "Working: A 3-way catalytic converter makes use of two catalysts to convert harmful gases to harmless gases in two chambers in succession. They are:",
      "Reduction catalyst: Reducing chamber is made up of platinum and rhodium. The exhaust gases are first sent over the reduction catalyst. It converts oxides of nitrogen (NO,) to nitrogen (N2) and oxygen (O2). The following reaction take place when the exhaust gases pass over the reduction catalyst.",
      "2NO",
      "2NO,",
      "N2 + 202",
      "Thus, the reduction catalyst removes oxides of nitrogen and convert to oxygen and nitrogen, the harmless gases.",
      "ii. Oxidation catalyst: The oxidation chamber is made up of platinum and palladium. The exhaust gases that are free of oxide of nitrogen (NOx) are then sent over the oxidation catalyst which converts carbon monoxide (CO) and hydrocarbon (HC) in the gases into carbon dioxide (CO2) and water. The following reaction takes place when the exhaust gases pass over the oxidation catalyst",
      "PUPd",
      "200 + 0,",
      "200,",
      "HC + 02",
      "PU/Pd",
      "+",
      "CO, * H,O",
      "(HC are hydrocarbon, may be methane, ethane, or other hydrocarbons) The gases that finally come out of the catalytic chamber are N2, CO2, and H2O. 3-way catalytic converters are so named because they are capable of eliminating",
      "three pollutants-NO, CO and HC.",
