      "23.1.6 Global Warming and Climate Change",
      "Several climatic changes are attributed to the large emissions of pollutants into the atmosphere. Generally, the global climate changes are studied from two aspects.",
      "i. Global warming caused by the greenhouse effect.",
      "ii. Destruction of the ozone layer in the stratosphere. The later will be discussed in",
      "detail in \"the chemistry of stratosphere\"",
      "Global warming",
      "average",
      "Global warming is the term used to described a gradual increase in the average temperature of the Earth's atmosphere and its oceans, a change that is believed to be permanent in the Earth's climate.",
      "The Earth maintains its long-term average temperature as a result of a balance between the heat received from the sun and the heat emitted to space by the Earth. The Earth receives mostly visible radiations from the sun and gets heated. The Earth emits most of its heat in the form of infrared (IR) radiation (Figure 23.2). This heat is absorbed by some of the gases present in the atmosphere and redirect it back to the Earth as heat. These gases trap the heat into the atmosphere which results in increase in the Earth's temperature and is called \"the greenhouse effect\". These gases are called the greenhouse gases because they behave like glass cover of the green house which is responsible for maintaining the temperature of the greenhouse used to cultivate",
      "vegetation in cold climate regions.",
      "The main greenhouse gases are CO2, CH4, SO2, N2O, 0, and some other gases",
      "emitted from variety of industrial processes.",
      "342",
      "absorbed",
      "reflected",
      "deforestation",
      "#",
      "escaping radiation",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "edge of atmosphere",
      "absorbed by atmosphere and Earth",
      "radiation absorbed by",
      "greenhouse",
      "greenhouse",
      "CFCs",
      "oil and",
      "gases",
      "petrol,",
      "engines",
      "gases and",
      "fossil fuels",
      "Figure 23.2 Illustration of greenhouse effect.",
      "The major gas contributing 50% to greenhouse effect is CO,. Other gasest (CH,, CFCS, SO, and NO.) together contribute the rest of 50%.",
      "Human activities are increasing the atmospheric concentration of CO, and other greenhouse gases, thereby altering the way the sun's heat is distributed on the Earth's surface and in the atmosphere. In addition, the stratospheric ozone shield, which protects us from the sun's UV radiations, is threatened by the emission of ozone- destroying chemicals. The Earth's average temperature is being increased (global warming) which ultimately changes our climates. The most serious outcome would be rise in sea level, floods and pattern of rain fall.",
      "23.1.7 Acid Rain",
      "The concept of acid rain is also associated with polluted air. Acid rain, also called acid precipitation or acid deposition, precipitation possessing a pH of about 5.6 or below primarily produced from the emission of sulfur dioxide (SO,) and nitrogenous oxides (NO.; the combination of NO and NO,) from human activities. Rain water is generally, slightly acidic (pH=5.6) due to the dissolved CO: which is a common constituent (0.036%) of the Earth's atmosphere making carbonic acid.",
      "H,O + CO.",
      "H.CO, Carbonic acid",
      "Therefore, any precipitation (rain, dew and snow) having a pH of less than 5.6, would be classified as acid rain. Apart from the acidifying effect of CO,, there are other pollutants which are also responsible of acid rain. Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxide",
      "343",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "(NOx), emitted into the atmosphere react with water, oxygen, and other chemicals to form sulphuric acid (H,SO,) and nitric acid (HNO,). These, then, mix with water and fall on the ground in the form of acid rain. A small portion of the SO, and NOx that cause acid rain is from natural sources such as volcanos and NO produced in lightening and forest fires, but most of them come from the burning of fossil fuels and other human",
      "activities.",
      "4NO2+ 2H,0 +02",
      "So, + H2O + 20,",
      "→4HNO,",
      "H2SO,",
      "power",
      "In polluted areas, the concentrations of these acids can be much higher and can reduce the pH of rain water substantially. Acid rain can fall quite far from the sources of pollution, due to long-range atmospheric transport by wind and air currents. In particular, acid rain is a serious problem for areas located near the coal-fired plants. Acid rain, on one hand, is a serious threat to the human life as it contaminates the drinking water and on the other hand is a problem for plants, animals, aquatic life, and building material. Heavy metals e.g. Cu, Hg and Pb are also dissolved by acid rain producing various toxic effects.",
      "forms",
      "Acidic streams and lakes or rivers affect the aquatic life. High acidity may result in killing of fish, reduces their growth and causes reproductive failure. Hatching of fish eggs is prevented due to change in pH of water. Moreover, green algae and",
      "many of beneficial bacteria, which are essential to aquatic life are also killed as a result of acidity in water. Plant growth is seriously inhibited due to acid rain. In addition, the acid rain also corrodes buildings, monuments, statues, and metals. For example, H.SO, destroys the CaCO, of the building material according to the following reaction.",
