        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 9",
        "theory": "A compound containing C, H and O, is found to contain 32% carbon and 4% hydrogen. Its molecular mass is 150 amu. Find its molecular formula.\nCalculate the empirical formula of the compound that contains 27.3% carbon\nand 72.7% oxygen.\niii. Acetic acid contains only C, H and O. A 5 g sample of acetic acid is completely burnt. It gives 7.33 g of carbon dioxide and 3 g of water. What is the mass percentage of each elements in acetic acid? Also calculate the empirical formula\niv.\nof acetic acid.\nIsoprene is a liquid compound that can be polymerized to form synthetic rubber. It is composed of 88.17% carbon and 11.83% hydrogen. Its molecular mass is 68.11 amu. What are its empirical and molecular formulae?\nmany\nPolyethylene is a hydrocarbon polymer used to produce food-storage bags and other flexible plastic items. A combustion analysis of a 1.26 g sample of polyethylene yields 3.94 g of carbon dioxide and 1.61 g of water. What is the empirical formula of polyethylene?\nQ4. Write detail answers of the following questions.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n(a) Define mass spectroscopy. Explain the construction and working of mass spectrometer.\n(b) How a mass spectrum of an element and compound can give information about atomic mass and molecular mass, respectively?\n(a) Differentiate between atomic emission and absorption spectroscopy. (b) Explain the working of flame photometer and atomic absorption spectrometer. (c) How atomic emission and absorption spectrophotometry is used for qualitative and quantitative analysis of elements?\n(a) What is meant by proton NMR spectroscopy?\n(b) Explain how chemical shift varies with shielding?\n(c) From proton NMR spectrum of a molecule, how would you know about presence of different types of protons in molecule?\n(a) Explain the principle of UV-Visible spectroscopy.\n(b) Explain different types of electron transitions in organic molecules.\nx\n382\n*\n5. (a) What is IR spectroscopy?\nNOT FOR SALE\n(b) Explain different types of molecular vibrations in a molecule.\n(c) Explain the significance of functional group and fingerprint regions in IR\nspectrum.\nProject\nThe molecular formula of a compound determined from combustion analysis and mass spectrometry is C,H,O. Proton NMR spectrum of compounds has only one signal. Draw the possible structure of compound.\nDraw the structures of four isomeric butyl bromides (C,H,Br) and predict the number of signals in proton NMR spectrum of each of the isomer.\nMab.co\nX\n383\nANSWERS OF MCQS\nNOT FOR SALE\nUnit- 13\n1. a\n2. a\n3. c\n4. C\n5. a\n6.\n7. b\n8. c\n9. b\n10. b\n11. b\n12. c\n13. c\n14. a\n15. a\nUnit- 14\n1. d\n2. d\n3. a\n4. d\n5. b\n6. d\n7. C\n8. a\n9. c\n10. d\n11. d\n12. b\n13. a\n14. C\n15. c\nUnit-15\n1. b\n3. a\n4. C\n5.\nd\n6. b\n7. c\n9. d\n10. d",
