        "id": "24.3",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 2",
        "theory": "NOT FOR SALE\n(melting point, boiling point, etc.) with the published values. For example; C,H,O is the molecular formula of both ethyl alcohol and dimethyl ether. But ethyl alcohol (C,H,OH) at room temperature is liquid and its boiling point is 78°C, while dimethyl ether (CH, -O-CH,) is gas at room temperature having a boiling point of -24°C. Chemical tests can suggest the functional groups and narrow the range of possible structures before the physical properties are used to make an identification. For example ethanol reacts with sodium metal liberating hydrogen, while dimethyl ether\ndoes not react with it.\nChemical analysis is insufficient for new compounds that have never been synthesized and characterized. They are also impractical with compounds that are difficult to obtain, because a relatively large sample is required to complete the elemental analysis and all the functional group tests. We need analytical techniques that work with tiny samples and that do not destroy the sample. Spectroscopic techniques are modern methods of analysis which often meet these requirements.\n24.2.1\nSpectroscopy\nThe interaction of electromagnet radiations with matter is called spectroscopy. Spectroscopy is the measurement of the amount of light absorbed (or emitted) by a compound as a function of the wavelength (or frequency) of light. In general, a spectrometer irradiates the sample with light, measures the amount of light transmitted as a function of wavelength (or frequency) and plots the results on a graph, called a spectrum.\nOrganic compounds absorb energy on interaction with electromagnetic radiations. The electromagnetic radiation (emr) extends from high-energy gamma rays to low-energy radio waves. X-rays and gamma rays (very high energy) are so energetic that they excite electrons, above all the energy levels, causing ionization. Energies in the ultraviolet-visible range excite electrons to higher energy levels within molecules. Infrared energies excite molecular vibrations, and microwave energies excite rotations. Radio-wave frequencies (very low energy) excite the nuclear spin transitions observed in NMR spectroscopy. Unlike chemical tests (classical methods of analysis), most of the spectroscopic and chromatographic techniques are nondestructive, required small amount of sample, less time consuming and provide more accurate and rapid results. These methods are simple and do not involve much chemistry as compared to the classical methods. The regions of electromagnetic spectrum of electromagnetic radiations are given in Figure 24.2.\n363\nHighest energy\nNOT FOR SALE\nLowest mer g\nWavelength (nm)\n10-2\n100\n102\n104\n106\n10\n1010\n1012\nGamma\nUltra-\nInfrared\nMicrowave\nX-my\nWolet\nRadio frequency\n1020\n1018\n1016\n1014\n1012\n1010\n109\n10\n104\nFrequency (1~1)\n700\n750 nm\n600\n500\n400\nVisible region\nFigure 24.2 The electromagnetic spectrum.\n24.2.2 Spectroscopic Methods\nThis book covers the Ultraviolet-Visible (UV-Vis) Spectroscopy, Infrared (IR) Spectroscopy, Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) Spectroscopy and Mass Spectrometry (MS) that serve as powerful tools for structure and molecular mass determination. This unit also includes Atomic Emission and Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy, which are used for qualitative and quantitative analysis of elements.\nTidbit\nrange\nA spectrometer is a device used to detect spectrum of a substance in a range; for example, a of mass-to-charge values as in mass spectrometry or a range of wavelengths as in absorption spectrometry like infrared spectroscopy etc. A spectrophotometer is spectrometer that only measures the intensity of electromagnetic radiations absorbed (and transmitted) by a material.\n24.2.2.1 Ultraviolet-Visible (UV-Vis) Spectroscopy\nAbsorption of electromagnetic radiation by a molecule in ultraviolet (10-400 nm) and visible (400-750 nm) region leads to electronic transitions. It is therefore, discusse together as Ultraviolet-Visible spectroscopy. This technique is also called electroni spectroscopy. Normal UV-Visible spectrometer uses near ultraviolet region o ultraviolet radiation which has wavelength range of 200 nm to 400 nm.\n364\n*\nE\nNOT FOR SALE\nAbsorption in Ultraviolet/Visible Radiations and Electronic Transitions Absorption of UV-Visible radiations by a molecule is associated with excitation of electron from ground to excited states. The common electronic transition that are associated with the absorption of UV-Vis radiation by organic molecules, are of four types i.e. o o*, n→ o*, → and n-*, as shown in Figure\n24.3.\n1. The o-* transitions occur in\nsaturated hydrocarbons such as alkanes and cycloalkanes which contain only sigma bonds. -* transition requires greater energy,\n#\n4079\n*272\n274\n元\nFigure 24.3 Common types of electronic transition in organic molecules.\nthus C-C bond absorbs at 135 nm and C-H bond absorbs at about 125 nm. il. The n-o* transitions occur in a saturated molecules containing heteroatoms, such as oxygen, sulphur, nitrogen or halogens. This transition requires slightly less energy than required for a transition. For example methyl alcohol shows n-* absorption at 183 nm.\nill. The a→* transitions occur in molecule, containing double or triple bonds or aromatic rings. For example ethylene absorbs at 171nm for → transition. However, a conjugated system of unsaturated bonds absorbs at much longer wavelength, e.g. buta-1,3-diene and isoprene (2-methylbuta-1,3-diene) shows maximum absorption at 217 nm and 222 nm respectively (Figure 24.4). iv. The n-* transitions occur in molecules that contain double or triple bonds involving hetero atoms such as aldehydes, ketones, nitriles etc. The n-z* absorption band for acetone appears at 279 nm. The d-d transitions (Section 14.2.4) of transition metal complexes occurs usually in visible region. The colour of transition metal tomatoes, has a conjugated system of Lycopene, a pigment in ripped complexes are usually coloured due to d-d 11 double bonds and absorbs visible transition. When a substance absorb a light. Its longest wavelength particular wavelength in visible region, the absorption is at 505 nm.\nTidbit\ncolour that our eyes perceives is not the colour corresponding to the wavelength of the light absorbed but its complementary colour that is transmitted. We can also predict the Possible colour of a transition metal complex from its UV-Visible spectrum showing is absorption wavelength, using Table 24.1.\n365\nColour of light absorbed\nWavelength (nm)\nViolet\n400-435\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable 24.1 Complementary Colours and their Approximate Wavelength Ranges\nColour of Complex (complementary colour)\nYellow\nBlue",
