        "theory": "435-480\nBlue-green\n480-500\nOrange Orange-red\nGreen\n500-560\nRed\nYellow\n560-595\nviolet\nOrange\n595-620\nBlue\nOrange-red\n620-650\nBlue-green\nRed\n650-750\nGreen\nThe UV-Visible Spectrum\nThe U.V-Vis Spectrum is a plot of absorbance (A) against wavelength (2). The wavelengti at which absorption is maximum is referred to mix and intensity of absorption at thi wavelength is referred to as Emax.\nabsorbance\n1.0\n0.9\nλ..... = 222\n0.8\n0.7\n0.6\n0.5\n0.4\n0.3\n0.2-\n0.1\nazen\nCH, CH-C=CH.\nCH\n(solvent = methanol)\n200 210 220 230 240 250 260 270 280 290 300 310 320 330 340 350\nλ(nm)\nFigure 24.4 U.V. spectrum of isoprene.\nApplications of UV-Visible Spectroscopy\nAny structural feature in a molecule that is responsible for the absorption electromagnetic radiations in UV-Visible region is called chromophore. Since differ chromophores absorbs different wavelengths, it is sometimes possible to identify unknown compound by comparing its Amax with that of a known compound. The U\nX\n366\n*\nf\nht\ne\nVisible spectroscopy can also give information about presence of conjugated systems NOT FOR SALE\nand\ngroups\n(Section\nlike carbonyl groups etc. UV-Visible spectroscopy is more meaningful\nwhen combined with Infrared (Section 24.2.2.2), Nuclear Magnetic Resonance 24.2.2.3) and Mass spectroscopy (Section 24.2.2.5) for complete identification of a compound.\nUV-Visible spectroscopy is also used for quantitative analysis, using Beer-Lambert&#x27;s Law.\nA = e.c.l\nWhere A is the absorbance, c is the molar concentration of solution and I is the path length of the sample solution in centimetres. e is called molar absorptivity (formerly molar extinction coefficient) a constant characteristic of the solute at a given wavelength. The concentration (c) of a sample solution can be calculated from this law, by measuring the absorbance (A) of solution at wavelength of maximum absorption (max) provided that molar absorptivity (max) and pathlenght (1) are known. Another method to find the concentration of unknown solutions is to record the absorbances of solutions of known concentrations at wavelength of maximum absorption, using UV-Visible spectrometer. A graph is then constructed by plotting the concentrations on x-axis and absorbance on y-axis. This graph is called working curve or calibration curve. The concentration of unknown solution can be determined easily, from its absorbance using working curve.\n24.2.2.2 Infrared (IR) Spectroscopy\nThe infrared region of the spectrum corresponds to wavelengths of about 0.8 μm to 100 μm, just above the visible region. Common infrared spectrometers operate in the middle of this region, at wavelengths between 2.5um to 16μm, corresponds to wavenumber to 4000-625 cm. Infrared photons do not have enough energy to cause electronic transitions, but they can cause groups of atoms to vibrate with respect to the bonds that connect them. Like electronic transitions, these vibrational transitions correspond to distinct energies, and molecules absorb infrared radiations only at certain wavelengths and frequencies.\nMolecular Vibrations\nAbsorption of a photon of infrared radiation excites a molecule from its lowest, or ground, vibrational state to a higher one. For a vibration to be IR active, the dipole moment of molecule must be changed. The bond vibration may be stretching or bending. In stretching vibrations, the bond length is increased or decreased at regular intervals. There are two types of stretching vibrations, symmetrical stretching and asymmetrical stretching. In bending vibrations, a change in bond angle occurs between bonds with a commcn atom. The bending vibrations are also called as deformation\nX\n367\nNOT FOR SALE\nvibrations. Deformation vibrations are of two types i.e. in-plane bending and out of plane bending vibrations. Scissoring and rocking are examples of in-plane bending while twisting and wagging are examples of out of plane bending. Different types of\nvibrations of methylene (-CH,-) groups are shown in Figure 24.5.\nStretching vibrations\nSymmetrical\nAsymmetrical\nBending vibrations\nRocking\nScissoring\nWagging\nTwisting\nFigure 24.5 Different type of bond vibrations in methylene group.\nThe Infrared Spectrum\nThe plot of wavenumber, cm (or sometimes wavelength in micron, μm) versus percent transmittance (%T) is called Infrared spectrum. This is recorded as percent transmittance because the spectrometer records the ratio of the intensities of the two beams.\npercent transmittance (%T) = 14x100\nI\naze",
