        "theory": "com\nД\nAE\nB spin\nhigher energy\na spin\nlower energy\nExternal feild\nBo increases.\nNo field spins in all directions\nno energy difference\nMagnetic field\nspins aligned with the field (a)\nor against the field (8)\nFigure 24.8 Nuclear spin states in absence and presence of magnetic field. The nuclear magnetic resonance phenomenon occurs when nuclei aligned with an applied field are induced to absorb energy and change their spin orientation with respect to the applied field. The energy of absorption is a quantized process and the energy absorbed must equal the energy difference between the two spin states involved. A nucleus, when irradiated with radio-frequency photons having energy equal to the energy difference between the two spin states, a proton in the a-spin state can absorb a photon and flip to the ẞ-spin state. The phenomenon is called flipping.\nAE-EB-E- hv\n*\n371\nΦι\nB state\nhv=AE\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe &#x27;H NMR Spectrum\na state\nFigure 24.9 Nuclear spin flipping.\nThe graph of absorption on the y-axis and chemical shift (8, delta in ppm) on the x-axis is NMR spectrum. The chemical shift is the frequency of the resonance expressed with reference to a standard compound which is defined to be at 0 ppm.\nchemical shift (8)\nshift from TMS (Hz) operating frequency of spectrometer(Hz)\n×10\nFor taking the NMR spectrum of a substance a little amount of tetramethylsilane (TMS) is added to it. For TMS the value of chemical shift (8) has been chosen as zero arbitrarily. Chemical shifts of other compounds are compared with this reference value TMS has been chosen as a standard because all of protons are equivalent and shielded due to electropositive nature of silicon, and show single absorption at lowest &amp; value Moreover it is chemically inert to most of the organic compounds, miscible with mos of the organic solvents, and highly volatile, and can be separated from the compoun after its spectrum has been recorded.\nMagnetic Shielding by Electrons\nav\nProtons in a molecule are surrounded by electrons and exist in slightly differe electronic (magnetic) environments from one another. The electron densities vary fro one proton to another. Thus, the net field felt by a proton in a molecule will always less than the applied field and the proton is said to be shielded. All of the protons of molecule are shielded from the applied field by the electrons, but some are less shield than others, because they may have less surrounding electron cloud and termed deshielded protons. Deshielded protons are generally in vicinity of electronegat atoms in a molecule. More shielded protons absorb low radio-frequency radiations their signals appear to the right side of spectrum, upfield region; while deshiel\n372\nprotons absorb high radio-frequency radiations and their signals therefore, NOT FOR SALE appears at left side of spectrum, downfield region. Protons in identical chemical environments with the same shielding have the same chemical shift and are called equivalent protons. In general, the number of NMR signals corresponds to the number of protons present in different electronic environment in the molecule. Methyl alcohol has two type of protons, therefore its &#x27;H NMR spectrum has only two signals; an upfield signal for three equivalent and shielded, methyl protons and a downfield signal for the deshielded proton of hydroxyl. (Figure 24.10)\nH-O-C-II\nTMS\nad\nd\n10.0 9.0\n8.0 7.0 6.0 5.0 4.0 3.0 2.0 1.0 0\n5(ppm)\nFigure 24.10 &#x27;H NMR spectrum of methyl alcohol.\n24.2.2.4 Atomic Emission and Absorption Spectroscopy The study of the electromagnetic spectrum of elements is called Atomic Spectroscopy. Electrons exist in energy levels within an atom. These levels have well defined energies and electrons moving between them must absorb or emit radiations having energy equal to the difference between them. Every element has unique emission or absorption spectrum. Thus, the emission or absorption atomic spectra are used as finger print to identify the elements. Atomic spectroscopy is thought to be the oldest instrumental method for the identification of elements. Atomic emission and absorption spectrophotometry is used for qualitative and quantitative analysis of different metals.\nFlame Photometry\nOne of the widely used emission spectroscopic technique is flame photometry (more accurately called Flame Atomic Emission Spectrophotometry). An aqueous analyte\n373\nNOT FOR SALE\nis introduced into flame. Water evaporates and solid salt is left behind. The salt breaks into constituent atoms. The atoms go to vapour state. The gaseous atoms get excited in the flame. The excited atoms loose energy of characteristic wavelength, which is dispersed by a grating or prism and detected in the spectrophotometer. The intensity of light emitted by the atoms is directly proportional to the concentration\nof analyte.\nFlame\nLens Slit Filter\nAmplifier\nPhoto detector\nBurner\nMixing chamber\nAtomizer\nLCD display\nSample\nCompressed Air supply\nCompressed Fuel supply\nFigure 24.11 Schematic diagram of Flame Photometer.\nin\nFlame photometer has both qualitative and quantitative applications. The radiations. emitted by the flame photometer are characteristic to particular metal. Flame photometer with monochromators emits radiations of characteristic wavelengths which help to detect the presence of a particular metal in the sample. The concentration of unknown solution can be calculated from working curve of standard solutions. This technique is usually used to find the concentration of various alkali and alkaline earth metals in soil samples which are critical for soil cultivation. Flame photometry is also used to determine the concentrations of Na and K+ ions in biological samples and also estimation of various metals in soft drinks and juices.\nAtomic Absorption Spectrophotometry\nAtomic absorption spectrophotometry is used for the analysis of elements. The sampl solution is evaporated in a flame, and the sample is converted to atomic vapour. Som of the atoms in the flame get excited but majority of them are in ground state. When light of particular wave length from the hollow cathode lamp is passed through the\n374\nvapours\nNOT FOR SALE\natoms, they absorb the light for excitation from ground state to high energy state. The absorbance of light by atoms is directly proportional to the concentration of atomic in the flame. For the analysis of different elements different types of lamps are used which produce light of different wave lengths. Each element shows absorption of particular wave length. Before introducing the sample of unknown concentration, solution of known concentrations of that element are aspirated into the flame one by one. Their absorbances are recorded and a working curve is constructed, between concentration and absorbance. Then sample of unknown concentration is aspirated into the flame and its concentration is determined from their absorbance using working curve.\nAtomic absorption spectroscopy is used for the estimation of over sixty metals in polluted water, body fluids, soft drinks etc.\nHollow cathode lamp\nMab\nSlit burner\nSlit\nMonochromator Detector\nAtomic Absorption\nFlame\nOxiding gas\nIndicator",
