        "theory": "device\nFuel gas\nMixing chamber\nCondensate\nSample\nflow\nFigure 24.12 Schematic diagram Atomic Absorption Spectrophotometer.\n24.2.2.5 Mass Spectrometry\nAtomic and molecular masses are determined by mass spectrometry. Mass spectrometry is a highly sensitive instrumental technique. A mass spectrometer ionizes gaseous atoms and molecules in a high vacuum, sorts the ions according to their masses, and records the abundance of ions of each mass.\nMass Spectrometer and Mass Spectrum\nThe gaseous sample is bombarded with high energy electrons from electron-gun and the resulting positively charged ions (which are radical cations) are directed into an analyzer tube surrounded by a magnet. This magnet deflects the ions from their original\n375\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\ntrajectory, causing them to adopt a circular path, the radius of which depends on their mass-to-charge ratio (m/z). Tons of small m/z values are deflecte more than those of larger m/z. By varying the magnetic field strength, ions of a particit m/z can be selectively focused through a narrow slit onto a detector, where they are counted. A mass spectrum is the graph plotted by the mass spectrometer, with the m/z values on x-axis and the relative number of ions of each mass on the y-axis. Higher the peak of a species higher will be its abundance.\nion\nSource\nion\nbeam\ninsulator\nelectron\nbear\nMAGNET\nions that are too light\nbend too much\nions that are too heavy\nbend too little\nflight tube\nonly ions of the right mass\ncan enter the detector\ndetector slits\nslits\naccelerator plate\nprobe\nsample\ndetector\nto vacuum\npump\nrecorder\nFigure 24.13 Schematic diagram of a Mass Spectrometer.\nMass spectrum helps in finding the number of isotopes of an element and also their relative abundances. The mass spectrum of neon shows three peaks with m/z equal to 20, 21, and 22 which correspond to 20Ne, 21Ne and 22Ne respectively. Neon-20 is the most abundant and Neon-21 is the least abundant isotope as clear from the heights of their peaks.\nAbundance\n90.92%\nHI-\n20\n0.57% 8.82%\n21\n22\nFigure 24.14 Mass spectrum of Neon.\nm/z →→\n376\nNOT FOR SALE\nWhen a molecule is introduced into mass spectrometer, the molecular ion (M) is produced due to loss of an electron. The molecular ion has the highest m/z value that represents the molecular mass of molecule. In addition to the molecular ion, more ions are produced due to fragmentation of the molecule. Fragmentation occurs due to rupture of chemical bonds. A mass spectrum of chlorine molecule is given in Figure 24.15 shows that there are two isotopes of chlorine 35CI (m/z = 35) and 37C1 (m/z = 37) and three types of chlorine molecules, &quot;CI-CI (m/z = 70), 35CI-CI (m/z = 72) and\n37C1–37Cl (m/z = 74).\nIsotopes CI-35 and CI-37\nMolecules Cl-70 C1,-72 CI,-74\nrelative abundance\nMab.com\n10 20 30 40 50 60 70\nm/z\nFigure 24.15 Mass spectrum of chlorine (Cl).\nThe fragmentation pattern of a molecule gives an idea of the structure of the molecule. Thus mass spectrometry is very helpful in the identification of molecular structures as well. Mass spectrum of methyl alcohol is given in Figure 24.16.\nX\nRel. Abundance\n100\n80-\n60-\n40-\nCH2OH\nCH2OH\nCOH\nCH2\n20-\nCHOH\nC&#x27;H.\nCHỊ CH\nCO\n0.0-\n0.0\n10",
        "content": "device<br>Fuel gas<br>Mixing chamber<br>Condensate<br>Sample<br>flow<br>Figure 24.12 Schematic diagram Atomic Absorption Spectrophotometer.<br>24.2.2.5 Mass Spectrometry<br>Atomic and molecular masses are determined by mass spectrometry. Mass spectrometry is a highly sensitive instrumental technique. A mass spectrometer ionizes gaseous atoms and molecules in a high vacuum, sorts the ions according to their masses, and records the abundance of ions of each mass.<br>Mass Spectrometer and Mass Spectrum<br>The gaseous sample is bombarded with high energy electrons from electron-gun and the resulting positively charged ions (which are radical cations) are directed into an analyzer tube surrounded by a magnet. This magnet deflects the ions from their original<br>375<br>*<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>trajectory, causing them to adopt a circular path, the radius of which depends on their mass-to-charge ratio (m/z). Tons of small m/z values are deflecte more than those of larger m/z. By varying the magnetic field strength, ions of a particit m/z can be selectively focused through a narrow slit onto a detector, where they are counted. A mass spectrum is the graph plotted by the mass spectrometer, with the m/z values on x-axis and the relative number of ions of each mass on the y-axis. Higher the peak of a species higher will be its abundance.<br>ion<br>Source<br>ion<br>beam<br>insulator<br>electron<br>bear<br>MAGNET<br>ions that are too light<br>bend too much<br>ions that are too heavy<br>bend too little<br>flight tube<br>only ions of the right mass<br>can enter the detector<br>detector slits<br>slits<br>accelerator plate<br>probe<br>sample<br>detector<br>to vacuum<br>pump<br>recorder<br>Figure 24.13 Schematic diagram of a Mass Spectrometer.<br>Mass spectrum helps in finding the number of isotopes of an element and also their relative abundances. The mass spectrum of neon shows three peaks with m/z equal to 20, 21, and 22 which correspond to 20Ne, 21Ne and 22Ne respectively. Neon-20 is the most abundant and Neon-21 is the least abundant isotope as clear from the heights of their peaks.<br>Abundance<br>90.92%<br>HI-<br>20<br>0.57% 8.82%<br>21<br>22<br>Figure 24.14 Mass spectrum of Neon.<br>m/z →→<br>376<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>When a molecule is introduced into mass spectrometer, the molecular ion (M) is produced due to loss of an electron. The molecular ion has the highest m/z value that represents the molecular mass of molecule. In addition to the molecular ion, more ions are produced due to fragmentation of the molecule. Fragmentation occurs due to rupture of chemical bonds. A mass spectrum of chlorine molecule is given in Figure 24.15 shows that there are two isotopes of chlorine 35CI (m/z = 35) and 37C1 (m/z = 37) and three types of chlorine molecules, &quot;CI-CI (m/z = 70), 35CI-CI (m/z = 72) and<br>37C1–37Cl (m/z = 74).<br>Isotopes CI-35 and CI-37<br>Molecules Cl-70 C1,-72 CI,-74<br>relative abundance<br>Mab.com<br>10 20 30 40 50 60 70<br>m/z<br>Figure 24.15 Mass spectrum of chlorine (Cl).<br>The fragmentation pattern of a molecule gives an idea of the structure of the molecule. Thus mass spectrometry is very helpful in the identification of molecular structures as well. Mass spectrum of methyl alcohol is given in Figure 24.16.<br>X<br>Rel. Abundance<br>100<br>80-<br>60-<br>40-<br>CH2OH<br>CH2OH<br>COH<br>CH2<br>20-<br>CHOH<br>C&#x27;H.<br>CHỊ CH<br>CO<br>0.0-<br>0.0<br>10",
