      "24.2.2.5 Mass Spectrometry",
      "Atomic and molecular masses are determined by mass spectrometry. Mass spectrometry is a highly sensitive instrumental technique. A mass spectrometer ionizes gaseous atoms and molecules in a high vacuum, sorts the ions according to their masses, and records the abundance of ions of each mass.",
      "Mass Spectrometer and Mass Spectrum",
      "The gaseous sample is bombarded with high energy electrons from electron-gun and the resulting positively charged ions (which are radical cations) are directed into an analyzer tube surrounded by a magnet. This magnet deflects the ions from their original",
      "375",
      "*",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "trajectory, causing them to adopt a circular path, the radius of which depends on their mass-to-charge ratio (m/z). Tons of small m/z values are deflecte more than those of larger m/z. By varying the magnetic field strength, ions of a particit m/z can be selectively focused through a narrow slit onto a detector, where they are counted. A mass spectrum is the graph plotted by the mass spectrometer, with the m/z values on x-axis and the relative number of ions of each mass on the y-axis. Higher the peak of a species higher will be its abundance.",
